1
00:00:00,078 --> 00:00:04,772
If you're trying to break into tech right now, you've probably noticed a frustrating problem.

2
00:00:05,358 --> 00:00:09,860
Tutorials teach you how to write code, but that's honestly the easy part.

3
00:00:10,382 --> 00:00:13,380
When you get hired, you aren't just a solo coder anymore.

4
00:00:13,742 --> 00:00:17,840
You have to integrate with a team, existing code, and moving parts.

5
00:00:18,382 --> 00:00:24,420
You're thrown into a world of Jira tickets, Git repositories, staging environments, and deployment pipelines.

6
00:00:25,198 --> 00:00:26,916
It's incredibly overwhelming.

7
00:00:27,534 --> 00:00:37,700
Most people only understand this stuff after 6 to 12 months on the job, and honestly, most beginners get massive imposter syndrome their first week because no one taught them this stuff.

8
00:00:38,542 --> 00:00:41,060
Most of that only comes from years on the job.

9
00:00:41,614 --> 00:00:43,524
This course hands you the short version.

10
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:49,956
So when you talk about it, you sound like someone who already has the experience, well before you've actually put in the time.

11
00:00:50,478 --> 00:00:52,580
This course exists to fix that.

12
00:00:53,230 --> 00:00:57,412
In under two hours, we are going to walk through the real-world developer workflow.

13
00:00:57,806 --> 00:00:59,300
We're not going deep into theory.

14
00:00:59,886 --> 00:01:02,948
The focus is how things actually work in real jobs.

15
00:01:03,566 --> 00:01:08,516
No dry history lessons on hardware, just the day-to-day reality of modern IT.

16
00:01:08,910 --> 00:01:13,348
We're going to look at how frontends and backends actually talk to each other using APIs.

17
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:18,084
How to navigate code reviews and QA pushback without taking it personally.

18
00:01:18,702 --> 00:01:23,556
How to read a status code and a log line so you can tell where a problem is before anyone else.

19
00:01:24,334 --> 00:01:38,212
How cloud platforms like AWS actually fit into the picture, and how developers are actually using AI tools like Copilot and Cursor today, including how to review what the AI hands you, because that's the skill that actually gets you trusted.

20
00:01:38,862 --> 00:01:44,996
If you want to stop feeling lost in tech jargon and actually understand how the industry operates, this is for you.

21
00:01:45,518 --> 00:01:48,640
If this is the part you've been missing, you'll get a lot out of this.

22
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:50,160
Welcome to module one.

23
00:01:50,606 --> 00:01:52,548
It's a short one with two ideas.

24
00:01:52,974 --> 00:01:57,892
We'll look at what IT covers inside a real company using one everyday example.

25
00:01:58,318 --> 00:02:04,388
You'll also see the two reasons companies depend on these systems, including companies that don't sell software at all.

26
00:02:04,814 --> 00:02:11,200
By the end of this module, you'll be able to explain what IT really is and why a business can't run without it.

27
00:02:11,406 --> 00:02:15,620
Ask 10 people what IT means, and you'll get 10 different answers.

28
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:18,436
Some think it's the guy who fixes your laptop.

29
00:02:18,990 --> 00:02:20,640
Some think it's just coding.

30
00:02:21,038 --> 00:02:23,108
Neither one is really the full picture.

31
00:02:23,758 --> 00:02:28,452
In real companies, IT is every system that keeps the business running digitally.

32
00:02:29,134 --> 00:02:40,740
That includes the app your customers use, the servers that host it, the database that stores their information, the network that connects everything, and yes, the person who resets your password when you forget it.

33
00:02:41,614 --> 00:02:43,492
Think about ordering food from an app.

34
00:02:43,886 --> 00:02:48,068
The moment you tap order, you've triggered a whole chain of IT systems.

35
00:02:48,846 --> 00:02:50,308
The app talks to a server.

36
00:02:50,606 --> 00:02:53,604
The server checks a database to confirm the restaurant is open.

37
00:02:54,030 --> 00:02:56,292
Another system calculates your delivery fee.

38
00:02:56,814 --> 00:02:59,012
A notification system pings the driver.

39
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:02,436
All of that, from end to end, is IT.

40
00:03:03,182 --> 00:03:12,356
So when people say they work in IT, that could mean building the app, maintaining the servers, securing the data, or supporting the people who use all of it.

41
00:03:12,814 --> 00:03:14,116
It's not one job.

42
00:03:14,606 --> 00:03:17,636
It's an entire ecosystem of jobs working together.

43
00:03:18,286 --> 00:03:20,356
And here's the part most beginners miss.

44
00:03:20,814 --> 00:03:23,236
None of these systems exist in isolation.

45
00:03:23,694 --> 00:03:28,164
They're all built and maintained by teams of people who have to coordinate constantly.

46
00:03:28,782 --> 00:03:30,436
Which brings us to the next question.

47
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:33,360
How do companies actually organize all of this?

48
00:03:33,678 --> 00:03:36,260
Here's something that surprises a lot of beginners.

49
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:40,260
Most companies aren't in the business of making software.

50
00:03:40,846 --> 00:03:42,724
A bank isn't a software company.

51
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:49,220
A hospital isn't a software company, but almost every single one of them runs entirely on software.

52
00:03:49,934 --> 00:03:52,260
Companies use IT for two big reasons.

53
00:03:52,750 --> 00:04:01,252
First, to run the business internally, things like payroll systems, internal messaging tools, and databases that track inventory or employees.

54
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:09,444
Second, to serve customers externally, things like a banking app, a hospital's patient portal, or an online store.

55
00:04:10,254 --> 00:04:16,356
Companies organize all of this in different ways, and we'll talk about what that means for your first job near the end of the course.

56
00:04:17,134 --> 00:04:20,512
In either case, IT isn't a side department anymore.

57
00:04:20,718 --> 00:04:21,732
It's the backbone.

58
00:04:22,222 --> 00:04:28,900
If the systems go down, the company stops making money, customers can't get service, and employees can't do their jobs.

59
00:04:29,742 --> 00:04:35,268
That's why companies invest so heavily in the people and processes that keep IT running smoothly.

60
00:04:36,014 --> 00:04:41,924
Now that you understand what IT is and how companies rely on it, let's zoom in on the machines themselves.

61
00:04:42,542 --> 00:04:48,272
In the next section, we're going to open up the hood and look at how computers and operating systems actually work.

62
00:04:48,558 --> 00:04:50,180
Let's recap module one.

63
00:04:50,734 --> 00:04:54,852
You now know that IT is every system that keeps a business running.

64
00:04:55,374 --> 00:04:59,204
That goes from the app customers use to the person who resets your password.

65
00:04:59,630 --> 00:05:03,492
You've seen how one food order sets off a whole chain of those systems.

66
00:05:03,982 --> 00:05:10,084
And you know companies use them in two ways, to run the business from the inside and to serve customers.

67
00:05:10,606 --> 00:05:13,872
When those systems go down, the business feels it right away.

68
00:05:14,158 --> 00:05:15,620
Welcome to module 2.

69
00:05:16,078 --> 00:05:18,372
Let's look at the machine your code runs on.

70
00:05:18,894 --> 00:05:24,580
We'll begin with the simple loop every program follows and the three physical parts your app depends on.

71
00:05:25,134 --> 00:05:29,316
After that comes the operating system and the rules it sets for your code.

72
00:05:29,870 --> 00:05:35,844
We finish with the screen full of plain text that developers work in and why many of them prefer it to a mouse.

73
00:05:36,686 --> 00:05:40,740
By the end of this module, you'll understand why an app can run out of memory.

74
00:05:41,134 --> 00:05:44,548
You'll also know why servers are often run by typing commands.

75
00:05:45,102 --> 00:05:45,712
Let's begin.

76
00:05:45,998 --> 00:05:47,632
Let's clear this up right away.

77
00:05:47,918 --> 00:05:52,356
If you get a computer science degree, you'll spend months calculating binary code.

78
00:05:52,718 --> 00:05:56,068
But honestly, I never calculate binary on the job.

79
00:05:56,558 --> 00:05:59,108
Let's look at computers the way a working developer does.

80
00:05:59,886 --> 00:06:02,724
At its core, a computer is a digital machine.

81
00:06:03,246 --> 00:06:09,860
That just means it stores everything as tiny electrical switches that are either on or off, ones and zeros.

82
00:06:10,606 --> 00:06:15,312
Every photo, song, and piece of software is just millions of these switches.

83
00:06:15,822 --> 00:06:18,020
But you don't need to read the ones and zeros.

84
00:06:18,446 --> 00:06:23,076
You just need to know a computer is a fast calculator that does exactly three things.

85
00:06:23,566 --> 00:06:26,020
Input, process, output.

86
00:06:26,702 --> 00:06:29,092
Think about logging into Instagram or Netflix.

87
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:31,332
You type your password and click a button.

88
00:06:31,694 --> 00:06:32,580
That's the input.

89
00:06:33,006 --> 00:06:35,140
The system checks if your password is correct.

90
00:06:35,406 --> 00:06:36,324
That's the process.

91
00:06:36,814 --> 00:06:37,860
It loads your feed.

92
00:06:38,158 --> 00:06:39,108
That's the output.

93
00:06:39,598 --> 00:06:43,556
Every piece of software you will ever work on is just doing that loop.

94
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:47,232
Your job as a developer is to write the process part.

95
00:06:47,630 --> 00:06:50,112
The computer doesn't try to fix your code.

96
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:53,444
It just follows instructions like a very obedient robot.

97
00:06:54,094 --> 00:06:58,148
If your code is broken, the machine will execute that broken code perfectly.

98
00:06:58,894 --> 00:07:03,620
That's exactly why debugging is such a massive part of the job, and we'll get into that later.

99
00:07:04,014 --> 00:07:07,952
But first, let's look at the physical hardware that actually runs your code.

100
00:07:08,174 --> 00:07:11,908
Every app you've ever used depends on just three physical parts.

101
00:07:12,718 --> 00:07:15,364
If even one of them fails, your app dies.

102
00:07:16,270 --> 00:07:19,684
Those parts are storage, RAM, and the CPU.

103
00:07:20,430 --> 00:07:22,084
Storage is your hard drive.

104
00:07:22,414 --> 00:07:24,196
It's where data lives permanently.

105
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:28,484
It holds a massive amount of data, but pulling that data is slow.

106
00:07:29,230 --> 00:07:30,980
RAM is your active memory.

107
00:07:31,406 --> 00:07:37,188
When someone opens your app, the system pulls it from slow storage into fast RAM so it runs smoothly.

108
00:07:37,806 --> 00:07:39,492
But RAM is strictly limited.

109
00:07:39,918 --> 00:07:44,388
If your code loads too much data at once, RAM fills up and the app freezes.

110
00:07:45,262 --> 00:07:46,980
The CPU is the engine.

111
00:07:47,246 --> 00:07:57,188
It executes your logic and does the actual math. You need to care about this because when your company hosts an app in the cloud, they pay for these resources based on usage.

112
00:07:57,710 --> 00:08:01,604
If you write bloated code, your app chews through RAM and CPU.

113
00:08:02,126 --> 00:08:08,388
That drives up your AWS bill, or worse, your app crashes in production because the server ran out of memory.

114
00:08:09,134 --> 00:08:11,108
Storage is where data is saved.

115
00:08:11,406 --> 00:08:15,268
RAM is where data works, and the CPU does the heavy lifting.

116
00:08:15,790 --> 00:08:18,308
But hardware is completely useless on its own.

117
00:08:18,606 --> 00:08:22,020
It needs a manager, which brings us to the operating system.

118
00:08:22,318 --> 00:08:29,540
You might think your code runs directly on the computer's hardware, but in reality, your code isn't even allowed to touch it.

119
00:08:30,094 --> 00:08:32,484
Hardware only understands electricity.

120
00:08:32,782 --> 00:08:36,192
It has no idea what a database or a web browser is.

121
00:08:36,622 --> 00:08:41,476
We use an operating system, like Windows, Mac, or Linux as a middleman.

122
00:08:42,190 --> 00:08:44,708
In tech, we call this abstraction.

123
00:08:45,294 --> 00:08:50,980
When your backend reads from a database or sends a response over the network, it's not doing it directly.

124
00:08:51,374 --> 00:08:54,436
Your code is asking the operating system to do it for you.

125
00:08:55,246 --> 00:08:59,940
To keep things safe, the operating system is divided into two strict zones.

126
00:09:00,622 --> 00:09:02,404
First, there is kernel mode.

127
00:09:02,816 --> 00:09:04,292
This is the core engine.

128
00:09:04,686 --> 00:09:07,012
It has absolute power over the hardware.

129
00:09:07,566 --> 00:09:09,380
Second, there is user mode.

130
00:09:09,710 --> 00:09:11,236
This is where your code runs.

131
00:09:11,854 --> 00:09:14,404
User mode is basically a restricted bubble.

132
00:09:14,862 --> 00:09:18,896
Your app cannot directly touch the hardware or mess with other programs.

133
00:09:19,470 --> 00:09:24,292
If your code tries to access memory it doesn't own, the kernel immediately shuts it down.

134
00:09:24,974 --> 00:09:28,804
This is exactly why one buggy app can't take down the whole machine.

135
00:09:29,358 --> 00:09:34,052
And it's why you'll see errors like segmentation fault or out of memory on the job.

136
00:09:34,606 --> 00:09:39,044
That's the kernel stepping in and stopping a program before it damages anything else.

137
00:09:39,758 --> 00:09:40,516
Here's the thing.

138
00:09:40,910 --> 00:09:47,300
You probably use a Mac or Windows right now, but the servers that run the internet overwhelmingly run Linux.

139
00:09:47,982 --> 00:09:56,580
If you look at the web servers where the operating system can be identified, more than 9 out of 10 run a Unix style system, and most of those are Linux.

140
00:09:57,294 --> 00:10:06,660
You don't need to be an expert in it today, but you need to understand that the environment where you write your code is usually different from the Linux server where your code actually lives.

141
00:10:07,182 --> 00:10:11,684
And when you deploy your code to that Linux server, you won't have a mouse to click around with.

142
00:10:12,142 --> 00:10:13,796
Let's talk about the command line.

143
00:10:14,094 --> 00:10:16,516
Here is something most beginners don't realize.

144
00:10:17,102 --> 00:10:22,980
When developers deploy code to a server thousands of miles away, they aren't clicking buttons on a screen.

145
00:10:23,726 --> 00:10:29,892
You've spent your entire life using a mouse, clicking icons, dragging folders, it's comfortable.

146
00:10:30,606 --> 00:10:32,996
Early on, most people rely on the mouse.

147
00:10:33,582 --> 00:10:41,604
Over time, you'll notice developers lean on the keyboard instead, because for repetitive or technical tasks, it's usually a lot faster.

148
00:10:42,382 --> 00:10:47,268
What you are used to is called a GUI, a graphical user interface.

149
00:10:48,142 --> 00:10:53,092
But when you get on a dev team, you'll see developers staring at black screens with white text.

150
00:10:53,616 --> 00:10:57,028
That is the CLI, the command line interface.

151
00:10:57,678 --> 00:11:00,964
It's not hacking, it does the exact same thing your mouse does.

152
00:11:01,550 --> 00:11:08,068
Typing a simple text command in the CLI is exactly the same as right-clicking and hitting new folder with your mouse.

153
00:11:08,686 --> 00:11:12,484
Developers do this for two reasons speed and automation.

154
00:11:13,230 --> 00:11:19,940
You cannot easily automate a hundred mouse clicks, but you can write a one-line script to do it instantly in the command line.

155
00:11:20,590 --> 00:11:28,196
More importantly, when you deploy your backend code to a Linux server in the cloud, there is often no screen and no mouse waiting for you.

156
00:11:28,782 --> 00:11:31,492
It's a remote machine that expects text commands.

157
00:11:32,142 --> 00:11:41,732
Even on Teams where deployments run through an automated pipeline, that pipeline is usually built and maintained using the CLI, so sooner or later, you'll need it.

158
00:11:42,510 --> 00:11:45,316
You don't need to memorize a dictionary of commands today.

159
00:11:45,710 --> 00:11:52,548
Just know that as you step into this industry, you'll notice developers rely heavily on the keyboard because it's much faster.

160
00:11:53,134 --> 00:11:57,924
Now that you know the hardware and the OS, it's time to look at the actual software.

161
00:11:58,414 --> 00:12:04,004
In the next section, we're going to break down exactly what happens when you type a website into your browser.

162
00:12:04,366 --> 00:12:05,056
I'll see you there.

163
00:12:05,326 --> 00:12:06,916
Let's recap module 2.

164
00:12:07,438 --> 00:12:14,244
You saw that programs take input, process it, and give output, and that your job is to write the process part.

165
00:12:14,926 --> 00:12:17,444
That code depends on three physical parts.

166
00:12:17,936 --> 00:12:23,748
Storage keeps data, RAM holds what's being worked on, and the CPU does the work.

167
00:12:24,206 --> 00:12:29,028
Use too much memory or CPU, and your cloud bill goes up or your app crashes.

168
00:12:29,614 --> 00:12:32,816
The operating system sits between your code and the hardware.

169
00:12:33,134 --> 00:12:36,484
That's why one buggy app usually can't take down the whole machine.

170
00:12:37,038 --> 00:12:43,684
And since most web servers run Linux, often with no screen or mouse, developers get comfortable with the command line.

171
00:12:43,982 --> 00:12:45,536
Welcome to module 3.

172
00:12:45,934 --> 00:12:48,516
Let's see what happens when someone uses a website.

173
00:12:49,070 --> 00:12:51,908
We'll take one page load apart, piece by piece.

174
00:12:52,462 --> 00:13:00,036
Along the way, you'll meet the front end, the back end, APIs, and the database, and you'll see what each one is responsible for.

175
00:13:00,526 --> 00:13:05,828
You'll also learn to read status codes, a small skill that helps you find problems much faster.

176
00:13:06,382 --> 00:13:08,900
The last part compares websites and mobile apps.

177
00:13:09,518 --> 00:13:14,816
By the end of this module, you'll be able to look at a failed request and say which part to check first.

178
00:13:15,278 --> 00:13:16,576
Let's start in the browser.

179
00:13:16,782 --> 00:13:21,796
You open your browser, type a website address, and within a second, the page appears.

180
00:13:22,446 --> 00:13:33,380
It feels simple, but behind that one click, your computer is talking to other computers, sending requests, receiving data, and putting everything together into a page you can use.

181
00:13:34,190 --> 00:13:36,100
Let's break down what actually happens.

182
00:13:36,846 --> 00:13:39,812
First, you open a browser like Chrome or Safari.

183
00:13:40,494 --> 00:13:44,612
You type a website address, such as example.com, and press Enter.

184
00:13:45,294 --> 00:13:48,356
Your browser needs to find the server hosting that website.

185
00:13:48,974 --> 00:13:50,852
This is where DNS comes in.

186
00:13:51,406 --> 00:13:53,924
DNS stands for Domain Name System.

187
00:13:54,414 --> 00:14:01,572
It translates a domain name, like example.com, into an IP address that computers can use to locate the destination.

188
00:14:02,350 --> 00:14:05,568
You don't need to memorize the entire DNS process right now.

189
00:14:05,870 --> 00:14:10,276
Just remember this: humans use names, computers use addresses.

190
00:14:11,374 --> 00:14:15,108
Once the browser knows where to connect, it sends a request to the server.

191
00:14:15,534 --> 00:14:18,116
This is called an HTTP request.

192
00:14:18,958 --> 00:14:22,308
For example, your browser might ask, give me the homepage.

193
00:14:22,958 --> 00:14:25,764
The server receives that request and decides what to do.

194
00:14:26,254 --> 00:14:28,288
Maybe it returns an HTML page.

195
00:14:28,590 --> 00:14:30,244
Maybe it needs to fetch some data.

196
00:14:30,638 --> 00:14:32,612
Maybe it needs to check whether you're logged in.

197
00:14:33,454 --> 00:14:36,772
The server processes the request and sends a response back.

198
00:14:37,230 --> 00:14:42,884
That response might contain HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, or data.

199
00:14:43,726 --> 00:14:47,460
Your browser receives those files and uses them to display the website.

200
00:14:48,046 --> 00:14:50,820
By the way, you can actually watch all of this happen.

201
00:14:51,470 --> 00:14:57,444
Every browser has a developer tools panel, and the network tab shows every single request your page makes.

202
00:14:58,126 --> 00:15:00,068
You'll use it in the assignment for this section.

203
00:15:00,878 --> 00:15:02,436
And that's the basic idea.

204
00:15:02,798 --> 00:15:06,884
You request something, a server processes it, you receive a response.

205
00:15:07,854 --> 00:15:09,476
Now, here's something important.

206
00:15:09,838 --> 00:15:14,148
The website you see is not necessarily running entirely on your laptop.

207
00:15:14,638 --> 00:15:18,084
The browser is responsible for displaying and running the front end.

208
00:15:18,926 --> 00:15:23,428
The back end may be running on a completely different machine, possibly in the cloud.

209
00:15:24,142 --> 00:15:27,652
That's why developers need to understand how these pieces communicate.

210
00:15:27,982 --> 00:15:35,748
Because when a user says the website isn't working, the problem could be in the browser, the network, the back end, or somewhere else.

211
00:15:36,494 --> 00:15:38,276
And that brings us to the next question.

212
00:15:38,766 --> 00:15:40,608
What exactly is the front end?

213
00:15:40,878 --> 00:15:42,436
And what does the back end do?

214
00:15:42,766 --> 00:15:49,508
When you use an application, there are usually two major parts you need to understand, the front end and the back end.

215
00:15:50,158 --> 00:15:54,308
And if you're entering software development, you'll hear these two words constantly.

216
00:15:54,766 --> 00:15:56,196
Let's use a simple example.

217
00:15:56,654 --> 00:15:58,660
You open an online shopping website.

218
00:15:58,990 --> 00:16:03,812
You can see products, search for items, add something to your cart, and place an order.

219
00:16:04,622 --> 00:16:08,900
Everything you see and interact with in the browser is part of the front end.

220
00:16:09,646 --> 00:16:12,708
The front end is the user-facing part of the application.

221
00:16:13,166 --> 00:16:21,476
It handles things like what appears on the screen, what happens when you click a button, how a page responds to your actions, and how data is displayed.

222
00:16:22,094 --> 00:16:27,044
Common front-end technologies include HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

223
00:16:27,630 --> 00:16:32,964
Frameworks like React, Angular, and Vue are also used to build front-end applications.

224
00:16:33,838 --> 00:16:35,140
But here's the important part.

225
00:16:35,566 --> 00:16:39,492
The front end usually doesn't own all the data or business logic.

226
00:16:40,142 --> 00:16:44,836
For example, when you open a product page, the front end needs product information.

227
00:16:45,326 --> 00:16:46,980
Where does that information come from?

228
00:16:47,630 --> 00:16:49,316
That's where the backend comes in.

229
00:16:50,318 --> 00:16:52,528
The back end runs on the server side.

230
00:16:52,846 --> 00:16:59,908
It handles things like processing requests, applying business rules, accessing data, and communicating with other systems.

231
00:17:00,846 --> 00:17:02,756
Let's say you click add to cart.

232
00:17:03,182 --> 00:17:11,844
The front end knows you clicked the button, but the back end may need to check the product, verify availability, calculate the price, and save the cart.

233
00:17:12,654 --> 00:17:14,528
The front end displays the result.

234
00:17:14,894 --> 00:17:17,380
The back end handles the work behind the scenes.

235
00:17:18,222 --> 00:17:23,972
Now, don't think of front end and back end as two completely separate applications that never interact.

236
00:17:24,462 --> 00:17:25,540
They work together.

237
00:17:25,934 --> 00:17:28,644
The front end is responsible for the user experience.

238
00:17:29,006 --> 00:17:33,028
The back end is responsible for server-side processing and data access.

239
00:17:33,486 --> 00:17:36,836
And they communicate through APIs, which we'll cover next.

240
00:17:37,550 --> 00:17:38,276
One more thing.

241
00:17:38,766 --> 00:17:43,364
A front-end developer doesn't necessarily need to know every back-end technology.

242
00:17:43,854 --> 00:17:47,364
And a back-end developer doesn't necessarily need to build the front end.

243
00:17:47,982 --> 00:17:51,620
But both developers need to understand how their work connects.

244
00:17:52,366 --> 00:17:56,452
Because if the front end sends the wrong request, the backend may reject it.

245
00:17:56,910 --> 00:18:01,572
And if the back end returns the wrong data, the front end may display something incorrectly.

246
00:18:02,254 --> 00:18:07,716
That's why understanding the complete application is useful, even if you specialize in one area.

247
00:18:08,238 --> 00:18:12,448
Now let's look at the piece that connects these two sides the API.

248
00:18:12,688 --> 00:18:16,420
API is one of those words you'll hear everywhere in IT.

249
00:18:17,134 --> 00:18:28,548
Front-end developers talk about APIs, back-end developers build APIs, QA engineers test APIs, and cloud applications communicate with APIs all the time.

250
00:18:29,198 --> 00:18:30,980
So what is an API?

251
00:18:31,630 --> 00:18:32,612
Let's keep it simple.

252
00:18:32,942 --> 00:18:40,260
An API, or application programming interface, is a defined way for software to communicate with other software.

253
00:18:40,910 --> 00:18:42,020
Think about a restaurant.

254
00:18:42,446 --> 00:18:44,580
You look at the menu and decide what you want.

255
00:18:44,942 --> 00:18:46,308
You tell the waiter your order.

256
00:18:46,766 --> 00:18:48,228
The waiter takes it to the kitchen.

257
00:18:48,622 --> 00:18:51,492
The kitchen prepares the food, and the waiter brings it back.

258
00:18:52,110 --> 00:18:57,924
You don't need to walk into the kitchen and figure out how everything works, you just use the interface provided to you.

259
00:18:58,670 --> 00:19:00,772
An API works in a similar way.

260
00:19:01,326 --> 00:19:03,972
Let's say your front end needs to display a list of products.

261
00:19:04,526 --> 00:19:07,300
It sends a request to the back end through an API.

262
00:19:07,758 --> 00:19:12,452
The backend processes that request, gets the required data, and sends a response.

263
00:19:12,942 --> 00:19:15,748
The front end receives the response and displays the products.

264
00:19:16,686 --> 00:19:21,668
For example, an API might have an endpoint that simply means give me the products.

265
00:19:22,126 --> 00:19:29,188
The response would come back as structured data, something like a list of products, each one with an ID, a name, and a price.

266
00:19:29,902 --> 00:19:33,316
The front end can use that data to display the products on the screen.

267
00:19:34,030 --> 00:19:36,592
You don't need to memorize the technical format right now.

268
00:19:36,942 --> 00:19:39,108
We'll keep the deeper details for another course.

269
00:19:39,662 --> 00:19:44,036
But understand this: an API is a contract between systems.

270
00:19:44,750 --> 00:19:49,316
It defines how one piece of software can request something from another piece of software.

271
00:19:49,934 --> 00:19:52,132
And it's not limited to front end and back end.

272
00:19:52,686 --> 00:19:56,548
A payment system can communicate with a shopping application through an API.

273
00:19:57,006 --> 00:20:00,260
A weather application can request weather data from a weather service.

274
00:20:00,814 --> 00:20:04,644
A mobile app can communicate with the same backend used by a website.

275
00:20:05,326 --> 00:20:08,900
That's why APIs are such a big part of modern software.

276
00:20:09,486 --> 00:20:13,892
Now, every one of those API calls comes back with a little status code.

277
00:20:14,254 --> 00:20:19,172
And once you can read that code, you can tell where a problem is before anyone else on the team.

278
00:20:19,662 --> 00:20:20,708
Let's look at that next.

279
00:20:21,070 --> 00:20:26,244
Here's a skill that takes about three minutes to learn and will make you look like you've been doing this for years.

280
00:20:26,798 --> 00:20:31,460
Every time the front end talks to the back end, it sends a request and gets a response.

281
00:20:31,918 --> 00:20:35,876
And every response carries a three-digit number called a status code.

282
00:20:36,334 --> 00:20:40,452
That number is the back end's one-word answer to the question, how did that go?

283
00:20:40,814 --> 00:20:42,340
You only need to know a handful.

284
00:20:42,830 --> 00:20:45,316
Anything starting with two means fine.

285
00:20:45,742 --> 00:20:47,232
200 is the classic.

286
00:20:47,566 --> 00:20:49,924
You asked for the products, here are the products.

287
00:20:50,350 --> 00:20:53,540
Anything starting with three means look somewhere else.

288
00:20:54,094 --> 00:20:58,052
A 301 or 302 is a redirect, the page moved.

289
00:20:58,606 --> 00:21:02,948
Anything starting with four means you, the caller, got something wrong.

290
00:21:03,534 --> 00:21:06,436
400 means the request itself was malformed.

291
00:21:06,958 --> 00:21:08,964
401 means you're not logged in.

292
00:21:09,390 --> 00:21:12,260
403 means you're logged in but not allowed.

293
00:21:12,782 --> 00:21:17,552
And 404, the famous one, means the thing you asked for doesn't exist.

294
00:21:18,158 --> 00:21:21,604
Anything starting with 5 means the backend got something wrong.

295
00:21:22,062 --> 00:21:24,548
500 is a generic crash on the server.

296
00:21:24,974 --> 00:21:29,540
502 or 503 usually mean the server is down or overloaded.

297
00:21:29,966 --> 00:21:30,788
So look at this one.

298
00:21:31,182 --> 00:21:34,564
The front end sent get slash API slash products.

299
00:21:35,246 --> 00:21:40,772
The backend answered 200, and the body is a small piece of structured text called JSON.

300
00:21:41,262 --> 00:21:43,364
An ID, a name, a price.

301
00:21:43,822 --> 00:21:45,156
That's the whole conversation.

302
00:21:45,614 --> 00:21:49,412
The front end takes that JSON and turns it into a product card on the screen.

303
00:21:50,030 --> 00:21:51,652
Now here's why this matters for you.

304
00:21:52,110 --> 00:21:53,952
Remember the question from the first lecture?

305
00:21:54,318 --> 00:21:55,552
The website isn't working.

306
00:21:55,758 --> 00:21:56,612
Where's the problem?

307
00:21:57,166 --> 00:22:00,292
If the request never left the browser, it's the front end.

308
00:22:00,750 --> 00:22:04,548
If it came back with a 4, the front end probably sent the wrong thing.

309
00:22:04,974 --> 00:22:07,684
If it came back with a 5, the back end broke.

310
00:22:08,110 --> 00:22:13,668
If it came back 200 and the screen is still wrong, the front end is displaying the data incorrectly.

311
00:22:14,254 --> 00:22:19,972
Four scenarios, one number tells you which one you're in, before you touch a single line of code.

312
00:22:20,462 --> 00:22:23,872
Now, the back end needs somewhere to keep the data it's sending back.

313
00:22:24,174 --> 00:22:25,188
That's the database.

314
00:22:25,550 --> 00:22:27,748
Let's say you create an account on a website.

315
00:22:28,174 --> 00:22:30,788
You enter your name, email, and password.

316
00:22:31,214 --> 00:22:35,108
You close the browser, come back tomorrow, and your account is still there.

317
00:22:35,726 --> 00:22:37,124
Where did that information go?

318
00:22:37,646 --> 00:22:38,980
It was stored somewhere.

319
00:22:39,406 --> 00:22:41,156
That's the job of a database.

320
00:22:41,902 --> 00:22:47,588
A database is a system used to store and manage data so applications can retrieve and work with it.

321
00:22:48,238 --> 00:22:59,108
In a typical application, the flow looks like this the front end collects the user's input, the back end processes the request, and the database stores or retrieves the information.

322
00:22:59,790 --> 00:23:01,124
Let's take a simple example.

323
00:23:01,678 --> 00:23:03,204
You log into an application.

324
00:23:03,822 --> 00:23:06,628
The front end sends your login details to the backend.

325
00:23:07,054 --> 00:23:10,788
The backend checks the stored account information and verifies the credentials.

326
00:23:11,342 --> 00:23:16,644
If the login is successful, the backend sends a response and the front end displays your dashboard.

327
00:23:17,230 --> 00:23:23,908
The database is where the application keeps information such as users, products, orders, or other business data.

328
00:23:24,558 --> 00:23:26,948
Now, there are different types of databases.

329
00:23:27,502 --> 00:23:34,724
Relational databases, such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server, store structured data in tables.

330
00:23:35,438 --> 00:23:44,004
NoSQL databases, such as MongoDB, use different data models and are often used for applications with particular data and scaling requirements.

331
00:23:44,750 --> 00:23:51,844
You don't need to learn database design in this course, but you should understand the difference between the application and the data it uses.

332
00:23:52,430 --> 00:23:55,748
Your backend code is not necessarily the permanent storage.

333
00:23:56,206 --> 00:23:59,844
The database is a separate system responsible for managing data.

334
00:24:00,366 --> 00:24:02,532
And this distinction matters in real jobs.

335
00:24:03,086 --> 00:24:08,900
If a user says their order disappeared, the problem might be in the front end, the back end, or the database.

336
00:24:09,550 --> 00:24:13,380
If an application is slow, the database could also be part of the problem.

337
00:24:13,870 --> 00:24:18,020
That's why developers need to understand how data moves through an application.

338
00:24:18,638 --> 00:24:20,132
Now, one last question.

339
00:24:20,590 --> 00:24:23,524
Does a mobile app work completely differently from a website?

340
00:24:24,110 --> 00:24:25,104
Not necessarily.

341
00:24:25,390 --> 00:24:27,428
You use a website on your laptop.

342
00:24:27,886 --> 00:24:32,356
You open the same service on your phone, and the experience looks a little different.

343
00:24:33,006 --> 00:24:38,948
The buttons may be arranged differently, the layout may be different, the app may use mobile specific features.

344
00:24:39,854 --> 00:24:41,252
But here's the important part.

345
00:24:41,614 --> 00:24:45,104
Both applications can communicate with the same backend.

346
00:24:45,966 --> 00:24:49,060
For example, imagine you're using a shopping application.

347
00:24:49,486 --> 00:24:51,556
You add an item to your cart on your laptop.

348
00:24:52,014 --> 00:24:55,652
Then you open the mobile app and the same item is still in your cart.

349
00:24:56,398 --> 00:24:57,284
How does that happen?

350
00:24:57,744 --> 00:25:03,236
Because both clients can use the same backend and access the same underlying account data.

351
00:25:03,982 --> 00:25:06,500
The website and the mobile app are different clients.

352
00:25:07,246 --> 00:25:09,188
They may have different front-end technologies.

353
00:25:09,902 --> 00:25:11,504
A website might use React.

354
00:25:11,950 --> 00:25:22,724
A mobile application might use Swift, Kotlin, or a cross-platform framework like Flutter or .NET MAUI, but they can still communicate with the same backend through APIs.

355
00:25:23,470 --> 00:25:26,788
This is a useful concept to understand before entering IT.

356
00:25:27,502 --> 00:25:31,236
You might work on a front-end team, a backend team, or a mobile team.

357
00:25:31,790 --> 00:25:36,036
Even if your technology is different, the application architecture can be connected.

358
00:25:36,846 --> 00:25:38,468
And that's the bigger picture.

359
00:25:38,784 --> 00:25:40,964
The front-end displays the experience.

360
00:25:41,390 --> 00:25:43,460
The back end processes the requests.

361
00:25:44,014 --> 00:25:45,988
The API connects systems.

362
00:25:46,350 --> 00:25:48,260
The database stores the data.

363
00:25:48,974 --> 00:25:53,764
Once you understand these four pieces, a lot of software development starts making more sense.

364
00:25:54,638 --> 00:26:01,220
In the next section, we'll move into programming basics and look at how developers actually write the logic behind these applications.

365
00:26:01,646 --> 00:26:03,396
Let's recap module 3.

366
00:26:03,822 --> 00:26:06,544
You followed one request from start to finish.

367
00:26:06,990 --> 00:26:09,424
DNS turned a name into an address.

368
00:26:09,774 --> 00:26:13,476
The browser asked a server for a page, and a response came back.

369
00:26:13,998 --> 00:26:17,412
You saw how the work splits between the two sides of an application.

370
00:26:17,838 --> 00:26:20,644
An API is the agreed way for them to talk.

371
00:26:21,134 --> 00:26:23,908
Status codes gave you a quick way to find a problem.

372
00:26:24,302 --> 00:26:32,260
A 4 usually points at the caller, a 5 points at the back end, and a 200 with the wrong screen often points at the front end.

373
00:26:32,718 --> 00:26:38,304
And since the data lives in a separate database, your cart can follow you from your laptop to your phone.

374
00:26:38,544 --> 00:26:40,068
Welcome to module 4.

375
00:26:40,430 --> 00:26:43,556
Now let's look at the code that makes an application work.

376
00:26:43,982 --> 00:26:46,180
You don't need any coding experience for this.

377
00:26:46,766 --> 00:26:51,904
We'll go through what programming really is and the few ideas you'll find in nearly every language.

378
00:26:52,366 --> 00:26:58,084
You'll see how developers avoid writing the same logic twice and why errors are a normal part of the work.

379
00:26:58,606 --> 00:27:02,020
We finish with the code your project uses that somebody else wrote.

380
00:27:02,542 --> 00:27:06,116
By the end of this module, you'll know the ideas behind most code.

381
00:27:06,542 --> 00:27:10,532
You'll also know why a real project is full of code your team didn't write.

382
00:27:11,086 --> 00:27:13,508
Let's start with what programming actually means.

383
00:27:13,984 --> 00:27:18,180
Programming is basically giving a computer a set of instructions to follow.

384
00:27:18,926 --> 00:27:23,716
When you're writing code, you're not really talking to the computer in some magical way.

385
00:27:24,270 --> 00:27:27,716
You're breaking a problem down into steps that a machine can execute.

386
00:27:28,622 --> 00:27:33,776
For example, let's say you are building a shopping application and the user clicks buy now.

387
00:27:34,734 --> 00:27:36,644
Something has to happen behind that button.

388
00:27:37,390 --> 00:27:47,300
Check whether the product exists, check whether it's in stock, calculate the price, create the order, save the order, return a response.

389
00:27:47,886 --> 00:27:49,028
That's programming.

390
00:27:49,582 --> 00:27:54,756
The language might be Python, Java, C sharp, JavaScript, or something else.

391
00:27:55,630 --> 00:27:59,300
The syntax changes, but the underlying idea stays the same.

392
00:27:59,726 --> 00:28:05,060
Take a problem, break it into logical steps, and tell the computer exactly what to do.

393
00:28:05,646 --> 00:28:08,644
And this is one of the biggest mindset shifts for beginners.

394
00:28:09,070 --> 00:28:16,416
Programming isn't primarily about memorizing syntax, it's about learning how to turn a problem into a sequence of instructions.

395
00:28:16,718 --> 00:28:22,084
Before you work on real applications, there are a few programming concepts you'll see everywhere.

396
00:28:22,670 --> 00:28:24,228
The first is a variable.

397
00:28:24,750 --> 00:28:27,652
A variable is simply a name pointing to some data.

398
00:28:28,142 --> 00:28:33,796
You might have a username, an order total, a product ID, or whether a user is logged in.

399
00:28:34,510 --> 00:28:37,380
Variables let you store information and use it later.

400
00:28:37,806 --> 00:28:40,516
You can also update their values as your program runs.

401
00:28:41,294 --> 00:28:47,684
For example, when a customer adds another item to their cart, you can update the variable holding the total number of items.

402
00:28:48,494 --> 00:28:50,052
Then you have conditions.

403
00:28:50,638 --> 00:28:52,740
This is where your program makes decisions.

404
00:28:53,262 --> 00:28:56,676
For example, if the user is logged in, show their dashboard.

405
00:28:57,006 --> 00:28:58,916
Otherwise, show the login page.

406
00:28:59,598 --> 00:29:03,172
You can also combine conditions to handle more complex situations.

407
00:29:03,854 --> 00:29:10,180
For example, you might check whether a user is logged in and whether they have permission to access a particular page.

408
00:29:10,766 --> 00:29:16,100
Conditions help your application respond differently depending on the data or situation it encounters.

409
00:29:16,814 --> 00:29:18,212
Then you have loops.

410
00:29:18,766 --> 00:29:21,188
A loop is useful when you need to repeat something.

411
00:29:21,838 --> 00:29:26,148
Let's say you get a hundred products from a database and need to process every one of them.

412
00:29:26,606 --> 00:29:28,708
You don't write the same code a hundred times.

413
00:29:29,006 --> 00:29:31,204
You write the logic once and repeat it.

414
00:29:31,758 --> 00:29:38,500
Loops can also stop when a particular condition is met, which is useful when you don't know exactly how many repetitions you'll need.

415
00:29:39,118 --> 00:29:44,644
For example, you could use a loop to calculate the total price of every product in a customer's shopping cart.

416
00:29:45,390 --> 00:29:47,652
These three ideas show up everywhere.

417
00:29:48,016 --> 00:29:51,296
Store data, make decisions, repeat work.

418
00:29:51,726 --> 00:29:58,916
Whether you're working in Python, Java, C, Sharp, JavaScript, or another language, you'll keep coming back to these concepts.

419
00:29:59,278 --> 00:30:05,124
One of the first things that separates beginner code from more practical code is understanding functions.

420
00:30:06,094 --> 00:30:09,732
Let's say your application needs to calculate the total price of an order.

421
00:30:10,606 --> 00:30:13,668
You could write that logic directly inside your checkout code.

422
00:30:14,542 --> 00:30:18,436
But what happens when another part of the application needs the same calculation?

423
00:30:19,150 --> 00:30:20,676
You'd end up copying the code.

424
00:30:21,198 --> 00:30:22,820
That's where functions help.

425
00:30:23,566 --> 00:30:28,708
You put a specific piece of logic inside a reusable function and call it whenever you need it.

426
00:30:29,774 --> 00:30:39,108
For example, you might write a small function called calculateTotal, which takes in a price and a quantity and simply returns the price multiplied by the quantity.

427
00:30:39,982 --> 00:30:45,060
Now you don't care how many times you need that calculation, you can call the function whenever you need it.

428
00:30:45,902 --> 00:30:51,300
You can also update the calculation in one place without changing every part of your application.

429
00:30:52,398 --> 00:30:57,956
For example, if you later need to include a discount, you can update the function to handle that requirement.

430
00:30:58,830 --> 00:31:03,556
Every part of the application that calls this function can then use the updated logic.

431
00:31:04,366 --> 00:31:12,176
In real projects, functions get combined into larger components services, classes, modules, and applications.

432
00:31:12,878 --> 00:31:17,284
So when developers talk about writing reusable code, this is what they're getting at.

433
00:31:17,966 --> 00:31:23,684
Functions also make code easier to test because you can check a specific piece of logic independently.

434
00:31:24,590 --> 00:31:29,892
They help break large problems into smaller pieces that are easier to understand and maintain.

435
00:31:30,702 --> 00:31:35,312
Don't write the same logic again and again when you can isolate it and reuse it.

436
00:31:35,566 --> 00:31:38,724
Here's something beginners usually discover very quickly.

437
00:31:39,310 --> 00:31:40,992
Your code will break.

438
00:31:41,326 --> 00:31:42,992
And that's completely normal.

439
00:31:43,182 --> 00:31:46,372
You might have a syntax error because you wrote something incorrectly.

440
00:31:46,958 --> 00:31:51,152
You might have a runtime error because something went wrong while the application was running.

441
00:31:51,438 --> 00:31:55,460
Or the code might run perfectly but still produce the wrong result.

442
00:31:55,758 --> 00:31:56,932
That's a logic error.

443
00:31:57,550 --> 00:32:00,100
And that last one is especially important.

444
00:32:00,686 --> 00:32:07,044
A program can be completely valid from the computer's perspective and still be wrong from the user's perspective.

445
00:32:07,918 --> 00:32:11,108
That's why developers spend a surprising amount of time debugging.

446
00:32:11,726 --> 00:32:15,684
We'll walk through how that actually looks, error message and all, in the next section.

447
00:32:16,526 --> 00:32:18,948
For now, the mindset is what matters.

448
00:32:19,502 --> 00:32:21,348
You fix the actual cause.

449
00:32:21,934 --> 00:32:24,772
You don't randomly change code until the error disappears.

450
00:32:25,614 --> 00:32:28,900
Sometimes, the error message points directly to the problem.

451
00:32:29,518 --> 00:32:34,340
Other times, you need to trace the execution and understand what the code is actually doing.

452
00:32:35,022 --> 00:32:38,724
That's why reading error messages carefully is such an important skill.

453
00:32:39,342 --> 00:32:43,876
They often provide clues about where things went wrong and what to investigate next.

454
00:32:44,526 --> 00:32:47,556
You are not trying to write perfect code that never breaks.

455
00:32:48,110 --> 00:32:51,460
You're learning how to understand and fix problems when it does.

456
00:32:51,822 --> 00:32:57,860
One final concept beginners should understand is that developers rarely build everything from scratch.

457
00:32:58,446 --> 00:33:00,836
Let's say your application needs to send an email.

458
00:33:01,294 --> 00:33:04,772
You could spend weeks building an entire email system yourself.

459
00:33:05,358 --> 00:33:09,988
Or you could use an existing library or service that already solves the problem.

460
00:33:10,574 --> 00:33:13,444
That's why developers use libraries and frameworks.

461
00:33:13,966 --> 00:33:16,612
A library gives you reusable functionality.

462
00:33:17,006 --> 00:33:20,324
A framework gives you a larger structure for building applications.

463
00:33:21,102 --> 00:33:24,164
And these projects often depend on many other packages.

464
00:33:24,654 --> 00:33:26,244
Those are called dependencies.

465
00:33:27,118 --> 00:33:33,700
For example, a library might handle email formatting, while another package handles communication with an email service.

466
00:33:34,446 --> 00:33:40,292
Using these existing tools saves time and lets you focus on the specific requirements of your application.

467
00:33:41,038 --> 00:33:46,660
Of course, you still need to understand what these dependencies do and whether they fit your project's requirements.

468
00:33:47,630 --> 00:33:51,748
This is why you'll eventually see certain files in almost every project.

469
00:33:52,206 --> 00:33:55,392
JavaScript projects have a file called package.json.

470
00:33:56,142 --> 00:33:59,632
Python projects have a file called requirements.txt.

471
00:34:00,430 --> 00:34:08,324
Java projects have a file called POM.xml, and .NET projects have a project file ending in .csproj.

472
00:34:08,974 --> 00:34:12,100
These files help define what your application depends on.

473
00:34:12,782 --> 00:34:18,500
They also help other developers install the required packages and get the project running in their own environments.

474
00:34:19,278 --> 00:34:23,876
So when you join a real project, you're usually not starting with an empty folder.

475
00:34:24,462 --> 00:34:30,752
You're joining an existing code base with existing frameworks, libraries, dependencies, and conventions.

476
00:34:31,118 --> 00:34:34,404
And that's much closer to what real software development looks like.

477
00:34:34,670 --> 00:34:36,388
Let's recap module 4.

478
00:34:36,910 --> 00:34:40,900
Programming means breaking a problem into steps a computer can follow.

479
00:34:41,390 --> 00:34:45,796
The syntax changes from one language to another, but that idea stays the same.

480
00:34:46,414 --> 00:34:52,324
Variables, conditions, and loops let a program store data, make decisions, and repeat work.

481
00:34:52,846 --> 00:34:56,644
Functions let you write some logic once and use it wherever you need it.

482
00:34:57,134 --> 00:35:03,652
Errors come in three kinds, and the logic error is the hard one, because the code runs and the result is still wrong.

483
00:35:04,238 --> 00:35:08,036
And libraries and frameworks mean you rarely build everything yourself.

484
00:35:08,558 --> 00:35:12,192
In module 5, we look at how a team works on code together.

485
00:35:12,494 --> 00:35:14,116
Welcome to module 5.

486
00:35:14,432 --> 00:35:16,580
This is where the real job begins.

487
00:35:17,166 --> 00:35:20,708
On a real team, you rarely decide on your own what to build.

488
00:35:21,230 --> 00:35:23,268
Work comes to you in a certain way.

489
00:35:23,694 --> 00:35:27,460
It moves through a set of steps and other people are involved at each one.

490
00:35:27,918 --> 00:35:32,004
You'll join a code base you didn't write, and you'll get a ticket that says what's needed.

491
00:35:32,590 --> 00:35:34,788
Someone will ask how long it's going to take.

492
00:35:35,246 --> 00:35:39,460
You'll make your change with Git, and you'll see the different environments code passes through.

493
00:35:39,982 --> 00:35:43,552
When you're done, you'll hand your work to a teammate in a pull request.

494
00:35:43,950 --> 00:35:48,740
By the end of this module, you'll be able to take a ticket and follow it through your team's steps.

495
00:35:49,294 --> 00:35:51,152
Let's start with the code that's already there.

496
00:35:51,438 --> 00:35:57,828
One of the biggest surprises for new developers is that most of the time, you're not building something from an empty folder.

497
00:35:58,574 --> 00:36:05,380
You join a company, open your laptop, clone a repository, and discover that hundreds of files are already there.

498
00:36:06,126 --> 00:36:17,476
There are APIs, database queries, configuration files, tests, logging, authentication, deployment scripts, and code written by people who may have left the company years ago.

499
00:36:18,414 --> 00:36:20,484
And then your manager gives you a ticket.

500
00:36:20,910 --> 00:36:24,068
Something like add a new field to the customer API.

501
00:36:24,846 --> 00:36:42,788
That sounds simple, but before changing anything, you need to figure out where that API is implemented, where the data comes from, which database table contains the field, whether another service uses the same model, whether tests already exist, and whether adding the field could break something else.

502
00:36:43,854 --> 00:36:50,180
You might also need to understand how the application is configured and how changes move from development into production.

503
00:36:51,118 --> 00:36:57,988
Sometimes, the hardest part isn't making the change itself, it's discovering all the places that change might affect.

504
00:36:58,798 --> 00:37:02,212
This is why being a developer is not just about writing code.

505
00:37:02,990 --> 00:37:06,532
A huge part of the job is understanding code that already exists.

506
00:37:07,630 --> 00:37:15,780
Over time, you'll learn to follow the flow of data, identify important files, and recognize how different parts of an application connect.

507
00:37:16,494 --> 00:37:26,308
Your first skill on a real project isn't typing faster, it's getting comfortable moving through code you didn't write, a skill worth its own closer look later in this course.

508
00:37:26,638 --> 00:37:29,476
In many companies, your work starts with a ticket.

509
00:37:30,190 --> 00:37:35,296
You might hear terms like Jira, Azure DevOps, Linear, or GitHub issues.

510
00:37:35,822 --> 00:37:38,468
The tool changes, but the idea is similar.

511
00:37:38,894 --> 00:37:41,412
Someone creates a piece of work that needs to be done.

512
00:37:42,030 --> 00:37:47,108
For example, a ticket might say, add email validation to customer registration.

513
00:37:47,822 --> 00:37:52,804
The description explains that users should not be able to register with an invalid email address.

514
00:37:53,518 --> 00:38:06,084
And the acceptance criteria might say the email format needs to be validated, an error message should show up when it's wrong, unit tests need to be added, and existing valid registrations must continue working.

515
00:38:06,798 --> 00:38:13,988
The important part is that a ticket usually describes the problem and the expected outcome, not every line of code you need to write.

516
00:38:14,606 --> 00:38:18,436
It's your job to understand the requirement and figure out how to implement it.

517
00:38:19,214 --> 00:38:22,692
Sometimes, the ticket might leave important details unclear.

518
00:38:23,246 --> 00:38:29,092
You may need to ask questions, discuss edge cases, or clarify what should happen when something goes wrong.

519
00:38:29,742 --> 00:38:31,780
You'll also see different types of tickets.

520
00:38:32,334 --> 00:38:34,820
A bug means something isn't working correctly.

521
00:38:35,278 --> 00:38:37,636
A task is a piece of implementation work.

522
00:38:38,190 --> 00:38:46,532
A story is a larger piece of functionality from the user's perspective, and an epic is a larger body of work made up of multiple smaller tickets.

523
00:38:47,406 --> 00:38:55,332
Once you understand this, a ticket stops looking like an instruction from your manager and starts looking like the starting point for an engineering problem.

524
00:38:56,014 --> 00:39:02,084
You also need to think about how your change fits into the existing application and whether it introduces any risks.

525
00:39:02,766 --> 00:39:06,532
And once you understand the problem, someone's going to ask you the next question.

526
00:39:07,182 --> 00:39:08,736
How long do you think this will take?

527
00:39:08,942 --> 00:39:14,372
Once a ticket exists, someone usually has to answer a question that feels harder than it should be.

528
00:39:14,896 --> 00:39:16,452
How long will this take?

529
00:39:17,102 --> 00:39:19,076
Many teams don't answer that in hours.

530
00:39:19,662 --> 00:39:26,372
They use story points, a rough, relative measure of effort and complexity rather than a literal time estimate.

531
00:39:26,990 --> 00:39:30,496
A small, well-understood change might be a one or a two.

532
00:39:30,990 --> 00:39:36,676
Something that touches several parts of the system or has a lot of unknowns might be a five or an eight.

533
00:39:37,326 --> 00:39:40,164
The exact scale differs by team, and that's fine.

534
00:39:40,494 --> 00:39:44,100
The number itself matters less than the conversation that produces it.

535
00:39:44,974 --> 00:39:47,044
Here's the part beginners aren't warned about.

536
00:39:47,534 --> 00:39:54,468
Your estimates will sometimes be wrong, often in your first year, and that's expected, not a sign you're bad at the job.

537
00:39:55,182 --> 00:40:04,164
A ticket that looked like a two day change can turn into a week once you discover an edge case nobody mentioned, or a piece of the system that behaves differently than documented.

538
00:40:04,974 --> 00:40:07,812
What actually matters isn't estimating perfectly.

539
00:40:08,302 --> 00:40:10,628
It's noticing early and saying something.

540
00:40:11,246 --> 00:40:17,316
If you're a day into a two-day ticket and you can already tell it's going to take four, that's the moment to flag it.

541
00:40:17,582 --> 00:40:20,868
In stand up, on the ticket, wherever your team tracks this.

542
00:40:21,358 --> 00:40:23,780
Not on the final day when it's already late.

543
00:40:24,430 --> 00:40:28,304
Something like, this is turning out bigger than expected because of X.

544
00:40:29,006 --> 00:40:30,756
I think it's closer to four days now.

545
00:40:31,374 --> 00:40:37,220
That single sentence, said early, is the difference between a manageable surprise and a stressful one.

546
00:40:37,934 --> 00:40:40,900
So don't treat an estimate as a promise carved in stone.

547
00:40:41,550 --> 00:40:49,700
Treat it as your best guess with the information you had at the time, and treat updating it honestly as part of the job, not a failure to admit.

548
00:40:50,574 --> 00:40:56,384
Once your ticket is estimated and picked up, the next thing you'll actually spend most of your day using is Git.

549
00:40:56,590 --> 00:41:01,636
If you're working in software development, Git is going to become part of your everyday workflow.

550
00:41:02,190 --> 00:41:09,504
You pull the latest code, you create a branch, you make your changes, you commit them, you push the branch.

551
00:41:09,774 --> 00:41:11,620
Then you create a pull request.

552
00:41:12,238 --> 00:41:15,684
Someone reviews it, and once it's approved, it gets merged.

553
00:41:16,334 --> 00:41:20,868
The important thing to understand is that Git isn't just a place where code is stored.

554
00:41:21,358 --> 00:41:24,260
It's how teams safely work on the same code base.

555
00:41:24,942 --> 00:41:29,636
Two developers can work on different features without constantly overwriting each other's work.

556
00:41:30,222 --> 00:41:33,156
Your branch gives you an isolated place to make changes.

557
00:41:33,742 --> 00:41:39,428
And the pull request gives the team a chance to review those changes before they become part of the main code base.

558
00:41:40,174 --> 00:41:45,764
For example, imagine you're adding a new payment feature while another developer is fixing a login issue.

559
00:41:46,286 --> 00:41:50,212
You can both work on separate branches and commit your changes independently.

560
00:41:50,734 --> 00:41:54,244
Once your work is ready, Git helps bring those changes together.

561
00:41:54,894 --> 00:42:00,036
Sometimes you'll encounter merge conflicts when different changes affect the same part of a file.

562
00:42:00,590 --> 00:42:04,740
You'll need to understand what changed and decide how to combine the work correctly.

563
00:42:05,486 --> 00:42:12,164
Git also keeps a history of your changes so you can understand what happened and return to earlier versions when necessary.

564
00:42:12,942 --> 00:42:21,380
You'll eventually encounter terms like commit, branch, merge, pull request, rebase, conflict, checkout, and clone.

565
00:42:22,126 --> 00:42:31,184
You don't need to master all of Git before your first job, but you absolutely need to understand why Git exists and how it fits into the development workflow.

566
00:42:31,438 --> 00:42:35,236
Here's another concept that becomes very real when you join a company.

567
00:42:35,950 --> 00:42:40,132
Your code usually doesn't go directly from your laptop to the real users.

568
00:42:40,846 --> 00:42:42,404
There are different environments.

569
00:42:43,150 --> 00:42:46,340
Development is where developers build and test their changes.

570
00:42:47,086 --> 00:42:57,220
Some teams also have an int environment, short for integration, which is where your changes are combined with everyone else's changes for the first time, just to make sure nothing conflicts.

571
00:42:58,062 --> 00:43:02,820
Test, or QA, is where the application is validated before release.

572
00:43:03,534 --> 00:43:13,476
Many companies also have a staging environment, which is set up to look and behave almost exactly like production, so the team can do one final check before anything goes live.

573
00:43:14,286 --> 00:43:18,436
And production is the real environment used by actual customers.

574
00:43:19,438 --> 00:43:29,184
Your code typically moves through several of these stages in order, from your machine to dev, maybe to int, to test, to staging, and finally to prod.

575
00:43:29,966 --> 00:43:42,340
Not every company uses all of these environments, and some combine a few of them, but the idea tends to be the same catch problems earlier in a safer environment before they ever reach a real customer.

576
00:43:43,150 --> 00:43:44,868
And this distinction matters.

577
00:43:45,486 --> 00:43:50,436
You don't want to test the database migration by experimenting directly on the production database.

578
00:43:51,246 --> 00:43:54,276
You don't want unfinished features appearing in front of customers.

579
00:43:54,958 --> 00:44:00,484
And you don't want a developer casually changing production configuration because they were trying to fix something.

580
00:44:01,326 --> 00:44:06,980
Later in the course, we'll talk about how CICD helps move changes between these environments safely.

581
00:44:07,758 --> 00:44:09,732
For now, remember one thing.

582
00:44:10,190 --> 00:44:12,324
Your laptop is not production.

583
00:44:12,654 --> 00:44:15,748
You finish your ticket, you open a pull request.

584
00:44:16,238 --> 00:44:25,380
And now a teammate, who wasn't in your head while you were writing this, has to figure out what you changed and why, usually in the middle of their own busy day.

585
00:44:26,158 --> 00:44:30,340
A weak pull request just says fixed the bug and links the ticket.

586
00:44:31,054 --> 00:44:33,764
A good one saves the reviewer real time.

587
00:44:34,350 --> 00:44:39,140
It says what changed, why you approached it that way, and how to actually test it.

588
00:44:39,502 --> 00:44:44,064
A specific input, a specific screen, a specific expected result.

589
00:44:44,846 --> 00:44:48,836
Think of it like handing off a task to a coworker who's about to go on vacation.

590
00:44:49,518 --> 00:44:55,876
You wouldn't just say, I did the thing, you'd say what you touched, what you didn't touch, and what to double check.

591
00:44:56,622 --> 00:44:58,692
This matters for your commit messages too.

592
00:44:59,438 --> 00:45:02,276
Fixed stuff tells a future developer nothing.

593
00:45:02,958 --> 00:45:09,828
Fixed null pointer when discount is applied to an empty cart tells them exactly what problem existed and when it happened.

594
00:45:10,542 --> 00:45:12,932
The same habit applies inside your code.

595
00:45:13,454 --> 00:45:16,868
A comment shouldn't restate what the next line obviously does.

596
00:45:17,550 --> 00:45:26,180
It should explain the part the code can't show on its own, why this particular edge case needed a special check, or why you didn't use the simpler approach.

597
00:45:26,926 --> 00:45:31,044
Good documentation also helps when someone needs to investigate a problem later.

598
00:45:31,694 --> 00:45:35,940
They can understand the original decision without having to track down the person who made it.

599
00:45:36,558 --> 00:45:38,500
None of this is about writing more.

600
00:45:39,086 --> 00:45:45,380
It's about writing exactly enough that the next person, maybe you, six months from now, doesn't have to guess.

601
00:45:46,286 --> 00:45:50,308
So once your pull request is actually open, what happens next?

602
00:45:50,798 --> 00:45:52,452
That's where code review comes in.

603
00:45:52,718 --> 00:45:54,692
Code review is simple to describe.

604
00:45:55,150 --> 00:45:58,304
A teammate reads your change before it joins the main code base.

605
00:45:58,798 --> 00:46:00,612
Sometimes they approve it right away.

606
00:46:01,006 --> 00:46:02,820
Often they'll leave a few comments.

607
00:46:03,278 --> 00:46:08,228
They might ask a question, point out a case you missed, or suggest a simpler way to do something.

608
00:46:08,910 --> 00:46:13,316
You make the changes and push them to the same branch, and the pull request picks them up.

609
00:46:13,998 --> 00:46:16,836
Once your teammate approves, your branch gets merged.

610
00:46:17,422 --> 00:46:20,068
Those comments can feel personal the first few times.

611
00:46:20,622 --> 00:46:23,824
They're about the code, and senior developers get them too.

612
00:46:24,270 --> 00:46:26,852
They also teach you how your team likes things done.

613
00:46:27,470 --> 00:46:30,692
So let's recap module 5 and how far your ticket came.

614
00:46:31,246 --> 00:46:33,444
You found your way around code you didn't write.

615
00:46:33,902 --> 00:46:38,532
You understood what was being asked, gave an estimate, and worked on your own branch.

616
00:46:39,022 --> 00:46:43,396
Along the way, you saw the environment's code passes through on its way to customers.

617
00:46:43,918 --> 00:46:48,580
Then you wrote a pull request that someone could follow, and it got reviewed and merged.

618
00:46:49,134 --> 00:46:52,580
Up to this point, your change has only moved inside the team.

619
00:46:53,102 --> 00:46:57,796
In module 6, it goes the rest of the way, out to the people using the app.

620
00:46:58,094 --> 00:46:59,652
Welcome to module 6.

621
00:47:00,046 --> 00:47:03,344
Now let's see what happens between your laptop and production.

622
00:47:03,726 --> 00:47:06,864
This one is longer because a lot happens at this stage.

623
00:47:07,246 --> 00:47:13,316
You'll see how code becomes something that can run, what a container is, and how a pipeline moves it all along.

624
00:47:13,902 --> 00:47:18,148
You'll also see where settings and passwords belong, since they stay out of the code.

625
00:47:18,702 --> 00:47:20,164
Security comes up twice.

626
00:47:20,590 --> 00:47:24,144
The first time, it's the habits every company assumes you have.

627
00:47:24,430 --> 00:47:30,084
The second time, it's how you handle people's data and how you build for people who use software differently.

628
00:47:30,606 --> 00:47:32,772
The rest is about going live safely.

629
00:47:33,102 --> 00:47:36,036
That covers deployments and going back to an older version.

630
00:47:36,430 --> 00:47:42,436
It also covers turning features on without a new release and changing an API without breaking the apps that use it.

631
00:47:42,958 --> 00:47:49,060
By the end of this module, you'll know what people mean by a failed build, a red pipeline, or a rollback.

632
00:47:49,486 --> 00:47:50,064
Let's start.

633
00:47:50,318 --> 00:47:51,524
You've finished your ticket.

634
00:47:51,822 --> 00:47:56,452
The code works on your machine, but that doesn't mean the feature is ready for customers.

635
00:47:56,974 --> 00:48:01,156
Before your change reaches production, several things usually need to happen.

636
00:48:01,614 --> 00:48:09,572
Your code needs to be built, tests need to run, the application may need to be packaged, security or quality checks might run.

637
00:48:10,030 --> 00:48:13,316
Then the application needs to be deployed to an environment.

638
00:48:13,934 --> 00:48:16,544
A simplified version of that journey looks like this.

639
00:48:16,942 --> 00:48:26,404
You write the code, you commit it, it gets built, it gets tested, it gets packaged, it gets deployed, and finally it gets verified.

640
00:48:27,022 --> 00:48:30,500
The exact process depends on the company and technology stack.

641
00:48:31,022 --> 00:48:33,984
A small application might have a very simple pipeline.

642
00:48:34,286 --> 00:48:39,812
A large enterprise application might have dozens of automated checks before anything reaches production.

643
00:48:40,270 --> 00:48:47,588
For example, if a test fails after your latest change, the pipeline can stop the deployment before the problem reaches customers.

644
00:48:48,270 --> 00:48:54,084
Teams also use different environments, such as development, testing, staging, and production.

645
00:48:54,606 --> 00:49:00,900
Each environment serves a different purpose, helping teams validate changes before exposing them to real users.

646
00:49:01,518 --> 00:49:06,692
Sometimes, a deployment succeeds technically, but the application still behaves incorrectly.

647
00:49:07,342 --> 00:49:13,316
That's why teams also monitor logs, metrics, and application health after releasing a change.

648
00:49:13,966 --> 00:49:19,332
And if something goes wrong, teams need a way to roll back the release or quickly deploy a fix.

649
00:49:19,982 --> 00:49:25,956
This is one reason teams automate these steps instead of relying on someone to remember every check manually.

650
00:49:26,638 --> 00:49:33,188
You'll often hear these processes described as continuous integration and continuous delivery, or CICD.

651
00:49:33,614 --> 00:49:38,784
The important thing to understand is that writing the code is only one part of delivering software.

652
00:49:39,086 --> 00:49:42,436
You will hear developers talk about building an application.

653
00:49:42,830 --> 00:49:44,292
What does that actually mean?

654
00:49:44,782 --> 00:49:46,116
It depends on the language.

655
00:49:46,446 --> 00:49:51,044
Some languages need source code to be compiled into another form before it can run.

656
00:49:51,502 --> 00:49:54,308
Others may be interpreted or packaged differently.

657
00:49:54,798 --> 00:49:56,612
But the general idea is the same.

658
00:49:57,006 --> 00:50:01,796
You start with your source code and produce something that can actually be executed or deployed.

659
00:50:02,318 --> 00:50:09,264
For example, a project might contain your source code, its dependencies, some configuration, and a set of tests.

660
00:50:09,646 --> 00:50:14,624
All of that goes through a build process, and out the other end comes an application package.

661
00:50:15,054 --> 00:50:22,692
That package could be a container image, a compiled application, a deployment artifact, or another format, depending on the technology.

662
00:50:23,150 --> 00:50:28,144
And this is why developers don't simply copy random source files onto a production server.

663
00:50:28,462 --> 00:50:33,028
There is usually a repeatable process that turns source code into something deployable.

664
00:50:33,550 --> 00:50:40,836
A build process can also catch problems early, such as compilation errors, missing dependencies, or incorrect configuration.

665
00:50:41,454 --> 00:50:49,636
In a team environment, the goal is to make sure the same source code produces a consistent, predictable result every time the application is built.

666
00:50:50,190 --> 00:50:58,788
Once the build is complete, the resulting artifact can move through testing and deployment without developers having to manually assemble the application each time.

667
00:50:59,374 --> 00:51:03,556
And these days, more often than not, that something is a container.

668
00:51:04,174 --> 00:51:05,828
Let's talk about what that actually is.

669
00:51:06,222 --> 00:51:12,964
You will hear the word container in your first week, probably your first day, so let's make sure it doesn't scare you.

670
00:51:13,742 --> 00:51:15,684
Here's the problem containers solve.

671
00:51:16,142 --> 00:51:18,180
The application works on your laptop.

672
00:51:18,606 --> 00:51:21,284
Then it goes to the test server and breaks.

673
00:51:21,838 --> 00:51:23,584
Different operating system version.

674
00:51:24,014 --> 00:51:30,852
Different version of a library, a missing configuration file, and the sentence every developer has said at least once.

675
00:51:31,406 --> 00:51:32,900
But it works on my machine.

676
00:51:33,742 --> 00:51:38,820
A container fixes that by packaging the application together with everything it needs to run.

677
00:51:39,438 --> 00:51:45,700
The code, the runtime, the libraries, the settings, all of it, in one sealed box.

678
00:51:46,222 --> 00:51:53,348
That box runs the same way on your laptop, on the test server, and in the cloud, because it carries its own environment with it.

679
00:51:54,158 --> 00:52:01,124
A widely used tool for this is Docker, and the recipe for a container is a small text file called a Docker file.

680
00:52:01,518 --> 00:52:03,716
You'll see it at the root of many repositories.

681
00:52:04,398 --> 00:52:11,172
It just says, start from this base, copy my code in, install the dependencies, and here's the command to run.

682
00:52:12,078 --> 00:52:13,700
Two words you'll hear together.

683
00:52:14,126 --> 00:52:17,124
An image is the packaged box sitting on a shelf.

684
00:52:17,870 --> 00:52:20,420
A container is that image actually running.

685
00:52:21,134 --> 00:52:23,620
Same relationship as a recipe and a meal.

686
00:52:24,334 --> 00:52:30,180
So when the build pipeline from the last lecture finishes, what often comes out the other end is a container image.

687
00:52:30,958 --> 00:52:35,044
That image gets pushed to a registry, which is just a storage shelf for images.

688
00:52:35,726 --> 00:52:39,620
And when it's time to deploy, the server pulls the image down and runs it.

689
00:52:40,462 --> 00:52:50,628
You don't need to write Docker files on day one, but when someone says the image didn't build or the container keeps restarting, you now know exactly what they're talking about.

690
00:52:51,214 --> 00:52:55,076
Now let's look at the machinery that builds and ships all of this automatically.

691
00:52:55,566 --> 00:52:56,592
CICD.

692
00:52:56,782 --> 00:53:01,028
Now we get to one of the most important concepts in modern software development.

693
00:53:01,472 --> 00:53:02,628
CICD.

694
00:53:03,342 --> 00:53:06,532
CI usually means continuous integration.

695
00:53:07,086 --> 00:53:14,724
The idea is that developers integrate their changes into the shared code base regularly, while automated checks help catch problems early.

696
00:53:15,342 --> 00:53:20,832
CD can refer to continuous delivery or continuous deployment, depending on the setup.

697
00:53:21,230 --> 00:53:26,948
The basic idea is that software can move through the delivery process in a controlled and repeatable way.

698
00:53:27,630 --> 00:53:33,188
For example, a developer pushes their code and the CICD pipeline picks it up automatically.

699
00:53:33,646 --> 00:53:38,116
It builds the code, runs the tests, runs quality checks, and deploys it.

700
00:53:38,702 --> 00:53:46,628
These pipelines are usually triggered by events such as pushing code, opening a pull request, or merging changes into a particular branch.

701
00:53:47,150 --> 00:53:55,300
Instead of a developer manually logging into a server every time and copying files around, the pipeline can perform these steps automatically.

702
00:53:55,822 --> 00:54:01,412
This is one of the major differences between a small personal project and a mature engineering environment.

703
00:54:02,062 --> 00:54:09,252
Continuous delivery means the application is kept ready for release, often with a manual approval before production deployment.

704
00:54:09,806 --> 00:54:15,908
Continuous deployment takes this further by automatically releasing changes that pass all required checks.

705
00:54:16,654 --> 00:54:20,228
The more software you have, the more valuable automation becomes.

706
00:54:20,878 --> 00:54:24,132
And when one of those steps turns red, that's not a disaster.

707
00:54:24,462 --> 00:54:26,352
That's the pipeline doing its job.

708
00:54:26,670 --> 00:54:29,124
It caught the problem before a customer did.

709
00:54:29,742 --> 00:54:36,036
Teams can also configure notifications, so developers know which step failed and can investigate the problem quickly.

710
00:54:36,750 --> 00:54:41,632
You don't want your production process to depend on someone remembering 15 manual steps.

711
00:54:41,966 --> 00:54:44,432
You want the process itself to be repeatable.

712
00:54:44,686 --> 00:54:49,860
Here's another thing beginners often discover when they move from small projects to real applications.

713
00:54:50,574 --> 00:54:55,716
Your code is usually the same across environments, but the configuration is not.

714
00:54:56,398 --> 00:55:05,924
For example, your application might need a database URL, an API endpoint, a storage account, some application settings, feature flags, and credentials.

715
00:55:06,670 --> 00:55:11,172
You don't want your production database URL hard-coded inside your source code.

716
00:55:11,662 --> 00:55:24,932
Instead, applications usually get this information through configuration or environment-specific settings, often stored as environment variables with names like database URL, API URL, or storage account.

717
00:55:25,742 --> 00:55:30,020
The actual values can change between development, testing, and production.

718
00:55:30,734 --> 00:55:38,468
For example, your development environment might connect to a local database, while production connects to a managed database hosted in the cloud.

719
00:55:39,054 --> 00:55:44,228
The application code can stay the same while the configuration determines which database it uses.

720
00:55:45,070 --> 00:55:47,780
This is also why secrets need special handling.

721
00:55:48,334 --> 00:55:55,012
Passwords, API keys, and credentials should not simply be committed to Git and left sitting inside the repository.

722
00:55:55,694 --> 00:56:00,740
Even if you remove a secret in a later commit, it may still exist in the repository's history.

723
00:56:01,326 --> 00:56:07,364
That's why you should rotate exposed credentials immediately, rather than assuming deleting the file has solved the problem.

724
00:56:08,014 --> 00:56:14,660
In real projects, you'll often work with secret managers, environment variables, or other secure configuration systems.

725
00:56:15,566 --> 00:56:23,492
One important distinction is that environment variables are a way to provide configuration, not automatically a secure place to store secrets.

726
00:56:24,398 --> 00:56:31,044
Production applications often use dedicated secret management systems to control access and manage credentials safely.

727
00:56:31,918 --> 00:56:33,380
The key idea is simple.

728
00:56:33,870 --> 00:56:37,860
Code and environment-specific configuration should be treated separately.

729
00:56:38,542 --> 00:56:45,412
And since we just mentioned secrets, let's spend a few minutes on the security habits every developer is expected to have.

730
00:56:45,870 --> 00:56:47,856
Not the security team, you.

731
00:56:48,176 --> 00:56:55,812
Nobody expects a junior developer to be a security expert, but there are five habits that every company assumes you already have.

732
00:56:56,366 --> 00:56:58,788
If you don't, you find out the hard way.

733
00:56:59,406 --> 00:57:02,372
First, secrets never go into Git.

734
00:57:02,894 --> 00:57:12,516
Passwords, API keys, tokens, connection strings, once a secret is committed, it's in the history forever, even if you delete it in the next commit.

735
00:57:13,070 --> 00:57:20,484
And on public repositories, automated bots are commonly scanning for exactly this kind of leaked key, often catching them quickly.

736
00:57:21,006 --> 00:57:31,588
This is why production projects typically keep secrets in environment variables or a secret manager, and why there's a file called.gitignore that tells git which files to never touch.

737
00:57:32,270 --> 00:57:37,988
If you accidentally commit a secret, tell your team immediately and rotate or revoke that credential.

738
00:57:38,478 --> 00:57:42,180
Removing it from the latest version of the code is not enough to make it safe.

739
00:57:42,958 --> 00:57:47,428
Second, never paste production data or secrets into an AI tool.

740
00:57:47,886 --> 00:57:49,060
Or a public chat.

741
00:57:49,326 --> 00:57:50,340
Or a ticket.

742
00:57:50,766 --> 00:57:52,932
It's incredibly tempting when you're debugging.

743
00:57:53,198 --> 00:57:56,708
Just paste the whole log with the customer's email in it into the assistant.

744
00:57:57,198 --> 00:57:57,696
Don't.

745
00:57:57,998 --> 00:57:59,056
Strip it first.

746
00:57:59,502 --> 00:58:03,428
Leaks like this have caused real, costly incidents at real companies.

747
00:58:04,238 --> 00:58:10,436
Even when a tool is approved by your company, understand its data handling rules before sharing sensitive information.

748
00:58:10,830 --> 00:58:13,220
Use sanitized examples whenever possible.

749
00:58:13,838 --> 00:58:16,932
Third, never trust input from the outside.

750
00:58:17,422 --> 00:58:19,012
Remember our registration form?

751
00:58:19,374 --> 00:58:22,148
A user can type anything into that email field.

752
00:58:22,496 --> 00:58:25,860
Anything, including things designed to break your database.

753
00:58:26,414 --> 00:58:30,724
The backend validates every input as if the user is actively trying to hurt you.

754
00:58:31,150 --> 00:58:33,796
Not because most are, because one will.

755
00:58:34,478 --> 00:58:36,996
And validation alone isn't enough for every threat.

756
00:58:37,582 --> 00:58:45,476
For example, database queries should use parameterized statements instead of building SQL by joining user input directly into a query.

757
00:58:46,094 --> 00:58:48,164
Fourth, HTTPS.

758
00:58:48,496 --> 00:58:49,060
Always.

759
00:58:49,550 --> 00:58:51,136
That's the padlock in the browser.

760
00:58:51,406 --> 00:58:54,564
It means the data between the user and the server is encrypted.

761
00:58:54,990 --> 00:58:59,856
Every login form, every API, every environment, including test.

762
00:59:00,526 --> 00:59:04,708
You should also avoid disabling certificate validation just to make a connection work.

763
00:59:05,198 --> 00:59:08,740
That check helps verify that you're communicating with the intended server.

764
00:59:09,326 --> 00:59:11,296
Fifth, least privilege.

765
00:59:11,630 --> 00:59:14,976
You get access to what you need for your job and nothing more.

766
00:59:15,342 --> 00:59:22,692
So when you don't have right access to the production database on day one, that's not the company being difficult, that's the company protecting you.

767
00:59:23,086 --> 00:59:28,580
A junior developer with full production access is one typo away from a very bad day.

768
00:59:29,166 --> 00:59:31,972
The same principle applies to application permissions.

769
00:59:32,494 --> 00:59:39,652
A service should only have the access it needs, and credentials should never be shared casually between people or applications.

770
00:59:40,494 --> 00:59:46,372
None of this is complicated, but you'll notice that experienced developers do all five without thinking.

771
00:59:46,862 --> 00:59:50,596
Start now, and by your first job, it'll be automatic for you too.

772
00:59:51,214 --> 00:59:55,524
Alright, the code is built, packaged, configured, and secured.

773
00:59:55,982 --> 00:59:58,416
Now it needs to actually reach the environment.

774
00:59:58,862 --> 01:00:00,964
Let's look at what happens during a deployment.

775
01:00:01,198 --> 01:00:02,976
Let's say everything passed.

776
01:00:03,278 --> 01:00:08,068
The application has been built, tests passed, the deployment pipeline is ready.

777
01:00:08,526 --> 01:00:11,108
Now the new version needs to reach the environment.

778
01:00:11,694 --> 01:00:20,548
That might mean deploying a container, updating a cloud service, publishing an application package, running infrastructure changes, or updating a server.

779
01:00:20,910 --> 01:00:24,996
And deployment isn't always just upload new code and hope for the best.

780
01:00:25,486 --> 01:00:35,140
A mature deployment process might include a health check right after deploying, then some smoke tests, then ongoing monitoring before it's considered a real success.

781
01:00:35,918 --> 01:00:43,844
Some teams also use gradual rollouts, releasing the new version to a small percentage of users before making it available to everyone.

782
01:00:44,398 --> 01:00:46,820
This helps limit the impact if something goes wrong.

783
01:00:47,374 --> 01:00:49,856
And sometimes the deployment fails.

784
01:00:50,286 --> 01:00:53,616
Maybe the application starts but can't connect to the database.

785
01:00:53,966 --> 01:00:55,940
Maybe a configuration value is missing.

786
01:00:56,430 --> 01:00:59,268
Maybe a new change causes an unexpected error.

787
01:00:59,758 --> 01:01:07,620
Sometimes the deployment itself succeeds, but users start experiencing slower response times or errors that weren't caught during testing.

788
01:01:08,270 --> 01:01:12,644
That's why teams watch application metrics and logs closely after a release.

789
01:01:13,294 --> 01:01:22,916
For critical applications, teams may also use blue green deployments, where two environments are maintained so traffic can switch between the old and new versions when needed.

790
01:01:23,630 --> 01:01:31,588
Another approach is a canary deployment, where the new version is introduced gradually while the team checks its behavior before expanding the rollout.

791
01:01:32,046 --> 01:01:34,692
That's why deployment and monitoring are connected.

792
01:01:35,086 --> 01:01:37,540
You don't just ask, did the deployment finish?

793
01:01:37,806 --> 01:01:42,052
You also ask, is the application actually healthy after the deployment?

794
01:01:42,542 --> 01:01:47,492
Because ultimately, a successful deployment isn't just about getting new code into production.

795
01:01:47,918 --> 01:01:52,624
It's about making sure the application continues working reliably for the people using it.

796
01:01:52,910 --> 01:01:57,220
Now let's talk about something every developer eventually learns the hard way.

797
01:01:57,710 --> 01:02:02,500
A deployment can pass every automated test and still cause a problem in production.

798
01:02:03,054 --> 01:02:05,220
Maybe a strange edge case wasn't covered.

799
01:02:05,614 --> 01:02:08,484
Maybe the production data is different from your test data.

800
01:02:08,942 --> 01:02:11,300
Maybe an external service behaves differently.

801
01:02:12,014 --> 01:02:14,916
When that happens, one option is a rollback.

802
01:02:15,374 --> 01:02:19,460
That means moving the application back to a previous known good version.

803
01:02:20,142 --> 01:02:23,780
Say you released version 1.5 and it caused a production issue.

804
01:02:24,206 --> 01:02:29,924
A rollback simply puts version 1.4 back in place while the team figures out what went wrong.

805
01:02:30,574 --> 01:02:35,556
For example, imagine a new release causes the checkout page to fail for some customers.

806
01:02:36,110 --> 01:02:42,660
Instead of making everyone wait for a fix, the team can roll back to the previous version and restore the checkout experience.

807
01:02:43,214 --> 01:02:48,740
The goal isn't to pretend mistakes don't happen, the goal is to make failures recoverable.

808
01:02:49,326 --> 01:02:54,212
Of course, rolling back application code doesn't automatically reverse database changes.

809
01:02:54,830 --> 01:03:03,332
If a release changes the database structure or modifies existing data, the team needs to consider whether those changes are compatible with the previous version.

810
01:03:03,854 --> 01:03:06,276
That's a major principle in modern engineering.

811
01:03:06,702 --> 01:03:10,052
You don't design systems assuming nothing will ever go wrong.

812
01:03:10,478 --> 01:03:17,316
You design the process so that when something does go wrong, the team can detect it, respond to it, and recover safely.

813
01:03:17,870 --> 01:03:25,476
This is also why teams plan their recovery strategy before releasing risky changes, rather than figuring everything out during an incident.

814
01:03:26,126 --> 01:03:31,504
And recovering safely starts with knowing exactly what data you're responsible for in the first place.

815
01:03:31,790 --> 01:03:40,324
A support engineer pastes a customer's full account details, name, email, and order history, into a chat tool to ask for help debugging.

816
01:03:40,878 --> 01:03:48,324
Nothing malicious happened, but that data just left a system built to protect it and landed somewhere it was never supposed to go.

817
01:03:49,006 --> 01:03:54,116
This is what people mean by PII, personally identifiable information.

818
01:03:54,542 --> 01:04:00,384
It's anything that can identify a specific person a name, an email, a phone number.

819
01:04:00,782 --> 01:04:07,012
Sometimes it's even a combination of details that aren't identifying on their own, but become identifying together.

820
01:04:07,662 --> 01:04:10,628
Handling it carelessly isn't just a security slip.

821
01:04:10,926 --> 01:04:15,524
In many places, it's a legal one too, under rules like GDPR in Europe.

822
01:04:16,046 --> 01:04:22,692
Those rules require companies to know where personal data lives, who can see it, and how to delete it if someone asks.

823
01:04:23,438 --> 01:04:27,300
The habit that matters most day-to-day is minimizing exposure.

824
01:04:27,886 --> 01:04:35,524
When you're debugging, strip personal details out of logs and examples before you share them, the same way you'd strip a password out of a screenshot.

825
01:04:36,206 --> 01:04:44,100
When you're building a feature, ask whether you actually need to store a piece of personal data at all, or whether a reference to it elsewhere would do the job.

826
01:04:44,782 --> 01:04:47,524
Data you never collected can never leak.

827
01:04:48,174 --> 01:04:52,804
Accessibility is a related habit that's easy to treat as optional and isn't.

828
01:04:53,358 --> 01:04:59,396
A meaningful share of users navigate software with a screen reader, or without a mouse, or with reduced vision.

829
01:04:59,950 --> 01:05:02,372
Small things carry a lot of weight here.

830
01:05:02,894 --> 01:05:04,964
Don't convey meaning through color alone.

831
01:05:05,486 --> 01:05:08,708
Give images alternative text that describes what they show.

832
01:05:09,230 --> 01:05:13,824
Make sure interface elements can be reached and activated using only a keyboard.

833
01:05:14,254 --> 01:05:17,156
None of this requires becoming an accessibility specialist.

834
01:05:17,518 --> 01:05:23,876
It requires checking, before you ship a screen, whether someone using only a keyboard could actually get through it.

835
01:05:24,558 --> 01:05:26,912
Both of these habits share the same shape.

836
01:05:27,310 --> 01:05:34,244
They're invisible when you get them right, and they only become visible, often publicly and expensively, when you get them wrong.

837
01:05:34,798 --> 01:05:44,512
That's exactly why they're easy to skip under a deadline, and exactly why the teams that build them in as habits rather than afterthoughts tend to avoid the worst version of that story.

838
01:05:44,782 --> 01:05:50,692
A team finishes a new checkout flow and wants to ship it carefully, not all at once to every user.

839
01:05:51,182 --> 01:05:56,740
So, they wrap the new flow in a feature flag and deploy it to production with the flag switched off.

840
01:05:57,294 --> 01:05:59,428
The code is live on every server.

841
01:05:59,726 --> 01:06:00,900
Nobody sees it yet.

842
01:06:01,422 --> 01:06:03,332
That split is the whole idea.

843
01:06:03,790 --> 01:06:06,212
Deploying code means moving it to production.

844
01:06:06,638 --> 01:06:09,444
Releasing a feature means users can actually reach it.

845
01:06:09,966 --> 01:06:12,352
Most of the time, those two things happen together.

846
01:06:12,718 --> 01:06:15,428
You deploy and users see the change immediately.

847
01:06:15,918 --> 01:06:18,724
A feature flag breaks that link, on purpose.

848
01:06:19,310 --> 01:06:25,988
A feature flag is a setting, often just true or false, that the code checks before deciding which path to run.

849
01:06:26,542 --> 01:06:28,964
Turn it off, and users get the old behavior.

850
01:06:29,326 --> 01:06:31,140
Turn it on and they get the new one.

851
01:06:31,758 --> 01:06:39,908
The team can turn it on for themselves first, then a small percentage of real users, then everyone, watching for problems at each step.

852
01:06:40,494 --> 01:06:42,724
This changes how risk gets managed.

853
01:06:43,182 --> 01:06:47,716
If the new checkout flow breaks something, the fix isn't a rollback and a redeploy.

854
01:06:48,046 --> 01:06:51,524
It's flipping the flag back off, which usually takes seconds.

855
01:06:52,046 --> 01:06:56,036
The bad code is still sitting in production, but nobody is running it anymore.

856
01:06:56,622 --> 01:06:59,460
Flags show up in smaller, everyday forms too.

857
01:06:59,982 --> 01:07:03,908
A flag that hides an unfinished feature from everyone except the team building it.

858
01:07:04,398 --> 01:07:09,796
A flag that lets support turn a feature off for one customer having trouble, without touching anyone else.

859
01:07:10,350 --> 01:07:17,284
A flag used for an A slash B test, where two groups of users run different code paths so the team can compare results.

860
01:07:18,062 --> 01:07:20,260
The catch is that flags aren't free.

861
01:07:20,814 --> 01:07:29,508
Every flag is a branch in the code, an if this, else that, and old flags that nobody removes pile up into code that's hard to follow.

862
01:07:29,934 --> 01:07:38,276
A flag left in by accident, still checked in some corner of the code base long after the decision was made, is a common source of confusing bugs.

863
01:07:38,766 --> 01:07:45,732
Teams that use flags well usually clean them up once a decision is final, the same way they'd clean up a temporary debug log.

864
01:07:46,350 --> 01:07:51,524
So when you hear that something is deployed but not released yet, this is usually why.

865
01:07:51,982 --> 01:07:53,988
The code already made it to production.

866
01:07:54,350 --> 01:07:56,192
Someone just hasn't flipped the switch.

867
01:07:56,398 --> 01:08:00,932
A mobile app calls an endpoint that returns a customer's name as a single field.

868
01:08:01,390 --> 01:08:08,068
The backend team decides that's messy and changes it to two fields, first name and last name, in the same response.

869
01:08:08,686 --> 01:08:09,380
They deploy it.

870
01:08:09,902 --> 01:08:17,508
Within minutes, the app is crashing for anyone who hasn't updated it because it's still looking for the old field and it isn't there anymore.

871
01:08:18,126 --> 01:08:19,908
Nothing was wrong with the new shape.

872
01:08:20,238 --> 01:08:27,428
The problem is that the old mobile app was never updated to expect it, and apps don't update themselves the moment a server changes.

873
01:08:28,110 --> 01:08:31,844
Someone is always running older code against your newest API.

874
01:08:32,142 --> 01:08:39,108
A phone that hasn't auto-updated, a partner's system that integrates with you, a script someone wrote last year that still works.

875
01:08:39,662 --> 01:08:41,860
That's what backward compatibility means.

876
01:08:42,446 --> 01:08:49,700
A change is backward compatible when the old callers keep working exactly as before, even though something changed underneath.

877
01:08:50,670 --> 01:08:52,644
Some changes are naturally safe.

878
01:08:53,006 --> 01:08:59,332
Adding a new, optional field usually doesn't break anyone because old code just ignores fields it doesn't recognize.

879
01:09:00,014 --> 01:09:10,244
Renaming a field, removing one, or changing what a field means are the changes that break old callers, because they expected something specific and now it's gone or different.

880
01:09:11,214 --> 01:09:16,996
When a breaking change is unavoidable, teams usually version the API instead of changing it in place.

881
01:09:17,550 --> 01:09:25,316
That might mean a version number in the URL, like a path that says V1 vs V2, or a version noted in a request header.

882
01:09:25,902 --> 01:09:28,676
Old callers keep hitting V1 and keep working.

883
01:09:29,134 --> 01:09:31,652
New callers can move to V2 once they're ready.

884
01:09:32,270 --> 01:09:34,608
The old version doesn't disappear immediately.

885
01:09:34,830 --> 01:09:42,628
It usually sticks around for a deprecation window, often announced well in advance, giving everyone time to move before it's finally turned off.

886
01:09:43,470 --> 01:09:50,052
This is why a seemingly simple request, just rename this field, is rarely simple in a real system.

887
01:09:50,574 --> 01:09:52,740
It isn't about the code being hard to write.

888
01:09:53,134 --> 01:09:59,168
It's about every caller you can't see and don't control, still depending on the shape you're about to change.

889
01:09:59,534 --> 01:10:01,248
Let's recap module 6.

890
01:10:01,550 --> 01:10:04,420
It was a long one, so here's where your change has been.

891
01:10:04,974 --> 01:10:08,420
It got turned into a package, often a container image.

892
01:10:08,782 --> 01:10:13,348
A pipeline checked it and moved it along so nobody had to copy files by hand.

893
01:10:13,806 --> 01:10:18,788
The settings stayed outside the code, so the same build could run in test and in production.

894
01:10:19,214 --> 01:10:22,564
When it went live, the team watched whether the app stayed healthy.

895
01:10:22,862 --> 01:10:24,804
If it didn't, they had a way back.

896
01:10:25,262 --> 01:10:28,196
You also saw how teams lower the risk of a change.

897
01:10:28,590 --> 01:10:33,316
Feature flags are one way, and keeping an API working for older callers is another.

898
01:10:33,742 --> 01:10:40,100
And you picked up the security, privacy, and accessibility habits that stay with you on every change you make.

899
01:10:40,462 --> 01:10:45,540
Through all of this, we kept saying your code runs on a server, or in production.

900
01:10:45,966 --> 01:10:49,860
We haven't said much about where those servers are or who looks after them.

901
01:10:50,350 --> 01:10:53,092
For a lot of companies, the answer is the cloud.

902
01:10:53,486 --> 01:10:55,044
And that's module 7.

903
01:10:55,342 --> 01:10:56,900
Welcome to module 7.

904
01:10:57,326 --> 01:10:59,780
You've heard the word cloud a few times by now.

905
01:11:00,174 --> 01:11:01,540
Let's see what it really means.

906
01:11:02,126 --> 01:11:07,748
We'll go through the few building blocks most cloud apps use and how much of the work you can hand to the provider.

907
01:11:08,270 --> 01:11:14,212
When an app grows, you'll hear words like scaling and availability, so we'll explain those too.

908
01:11:14,670 --> 01:11:16,196
Then it gets practical.

909
01:11:16,654 --> 01:11:21,988
You'll see what a developer really touches in the cloud and how a forgotten server turns into a bill.

910
01:11:22,542 --> 01:11:24,164
The last part is about you.

911
01:11:24,686 --> 01:11:29,316
We'll look at the different jobs around all this, and you'll get a way to tell which one might suit you.

912
01:11:29,806 --> 01:11:34,372
By the end of this module, you'll be able to say what it means for an app to run in the cloud.

913
01:11:34,734 --> 01:11:37,488
You'll also know which parts of it you deal with yourself.

914
01:11:37,870 --> 01:11:38,608
Let's get into it.

915
01:11:38,862 --> 01:11:42,404
The cloud sounds like some completely different kind of technology.

916
01:11:42,766 --> 01:11:43,396
It isn't.

917
01:11:43,822 --> 01:11:49,060
At a basic level, the cloud is still computers, storage, networks, and databases.

918
01:11:49,678 --> 01:11:59,300
The difference is that instead of your company buying physical servers, putting them in a data center, and managing everything yourself, you can rent those resources from a cloud provider.

919
01:11:59,758 --> 01:12:06,436
Companies like AWS, Azure, and Google Cloud operate huge data centers filled with infrastructure.

920
01:12:06,958 --> 01:12:18,020
You request what you need through their platform, maybe a virtual machine, some object storage, a managed database, or a serverless function that just runs your code without you managing a server at all.

921
01:12:18,638 --> 01:12:23,204
You use the service, configure it, and pay based on the service and usage model.

922
01:12:23,790 --> 01:12:27,332
For example, imagine you're building an online shopping application.

923
01:12:27,790 --> 01:12:36,196
You might host the application on a virtual machine, store product images in cloud storage, and use a managed database to store customer and order information.

924
01:12:36,782 --> 01:12:40,036
These services work together to support the same application.

925
01:12:40,750 --> 01:12:43,488
You can also scale resources as your needs change.

926
01:12:43,886 --> 01:12:52,324
If more customers start using your application, you can increase capacity or configure certain services to scale automatically, depending on the architecture.

927
01:12:53,134 --> 01:12:58,180
Another important difference is that you don't always have to manage the underlying infrastructure yourself.

928
01:12:58,734 --> 01:13:07,108
With managed services, the cloud provider handles many operational tasks, allowing your team to focus more on building and maintaining the application.

929
01:13:07,854 --> 01:13:14,052
So when someone says our application runs in the cloud, it doesn't mean the application is floating somewhere.

930
01:13:14,414 --> 01:13:18,848
It means the application is running on infrastructure provided through a cloud platform.

931
01:13:19,118 --> 01:13:21,988
You don't need to memorize hundreds of cloud services.

932
01:13:22,510 --> 01:13:24,292
Start with the basic building blocks.

933
01:13:24,878 --> 01:13:27,620
Compute is where your application actually runs.

934
01:13:28,110 --> 01:13:34,276
That could be a virtual machine, a container platform, a serverless function, or another compute service.

935
01:13:34,926 --> 01:13:41,188
Storage is where you keep files and objects, things like images, videos, backups, and documents.

936
01:13:41,998 --> 01:13:49,124
Databases store your application's actual data, customers, orders, products, transactions, and so on.

937
01:13:49,710 --> 01:13:58,212
And networking is what connects all these pieces together, controlling how traffic moves between users, applications, databases, and other services.

938
01:13:58,894 --> 01:14:02,180
For example, imagine an online shopping application.

939
01:14:02,702 --> 01:14:04,420
Compute runs the application.

940
01:14:04,814 --> 01:14:13,508
The database stores customer and order details, storage holds product images, and networking allows customers to access everything through their browsers.

941
01:14:13,998 --> 01:14:21,220
A very simple application might just have users hitting an application, which talks to a database and maybe some object storage.

942
01:14:21,646 --> 01:14:25,988
A larger application usually adds more pieces in front of and around that.

943
01:14:26,510 --> 01:14:36,004
A load balancer spreading traffic across multiple copies of the application, a cache sitting in front of the database to speed things up, and storage handling the larger files.

944
01:14:36,622 --> 01:14:40,128
Each additional component usually solves a specific problem.

945
01:14:40,622 --> 01:14:43,248
A load balancer helps distribute incoming traffic.

946
01:14:43,566 --> 01:14:50,116
A cache reduces repeated requests to the database, and additional application instances help handle more users.

947
01:14:50,638 --> 01:15:00,868
As the application grows, you might also introduce monitoring to track its health, identity services to control who can access it, and backup systems to help recover from failures.

948
01:15:01,422 --> 01:15:03,876
You don't need to understand every service yet.

949
01:15:04,366 --> 01:15:11,076
What matters is that cloud applications are usually built by combining these same few building blocks, just at different scales.

950
01:15:11,470 --> 01:15:13,924
Here's one of the biggest advantages of the cloud.

951
01:15:14,286 --> 01:15:17,924
You don't always have to manage the underlying infrastructure yourself.

952
01:15:18,382 --> 01:15:20,644
Let's say your application needs a database.

953
01:15:21,102 --> 01:15:24,772
One option is to run that database yourself on a virtual machine.

954
01:15:25,230 --> 01:15:32,772
Now you're the one responsible for updates, backups, monitoring, scaling, and keeping the operating system maintained.

955
01:15:33,326 --> 01:15:44,580
Another option is to use a managed database service, where the cloud provider handles much of that underlying infrastructure for you, so your team can focus more on the database and the application itself.

956
01:15:45,262 --> 01:15:49,476
Of course, managed doesn't mean the provider handles absolutely everything.

957
01:15:49,998 --> 01:15:58,372
Your team may still be responsible for database access, data security, query performance, and deciding how backups and recovery should work.

958
01:15:59,022 --> 01:16:04,260
That same idea shows up across cloud services, and you'll often hear three terms for it.

959
01:16:04,782 --> 01:16:11,140
Infrastructure as a service, or IAAS, means you manage more of the infrastructure yourself.

960
01:16:11,758 --> 01:16:18,532
Platform as a service, or PAAS, means the provider manages more of the underlying platform for you.

961
01:16:19,150 --> 01:16:24,528
And software as a service, or SaaS, means you simply use the finished software.

962
01:16:24,814 --> 01:16:31,044
Your company might use Microsoft 365, for example, without managing a single server that runs it.

963
01:16:31,694 --> 01:16:38,532
Think about it this way With IAAS, you get more control but take on more operational responsibility.

964
01:16:39,086 --> 01:16:42,820
With PAAS, you spend less time managing the platform.

965
01:16:43,406 --> 01:16:48,768
With SAAS, you use an application that's already built and maintained by the provider.

966
01:16:49,454 --> 01:16:57,796
The right choice depends on what you're building, how much control you need, your team's expertise, and how much operational work you're prepared to take on.

967
01:16:58,446 --> 01:17:01,412
The important lesson isn't memorizing those three letters.

968
01:17:01,870 --> 01:17:06,672
It's understanding how much infrastructure your team is actually responsible for managing.

969
01:17:06,926 --> 01:17:11,072
Cloud platforms become especially useful when an application grows.

970
01:17:11,470 --> 01:17:14,692
Let's say your application normally has a thousand users.

971
01:17:15,150 --> 01:17:19,012
Then a marketing campaign suddenly brings in a hundred thousand.

972
01:17:19,534 --> 01:17:23,972
Your system needs to handle that jump in traffic, and that's where scaling comes in.

973
01:17:24,398 --> 01:17:31,556
Adding more copies of your application, giving existing ones more resources, or adding caching to absorb the extra load.

974
01:17:32,046 --> 01:17:34,308
There are two common approaches to scaling.

975
01:17:34,862 --> 01:17:40,740
Vertical scaling means giving an existing server more resources, such as CPU and memory.

976
01:17:41,230 --> 01:17:47,332
Horizontal scaling means adding more instances of the application so the workload can be shared across them.

977
01:17:47,822 --> 01:17:50,052
You'll also hear the word availability.

978
01:17:50,574 --> 01:18:05,444
Businesses don't want their application to go down just because one server failed, so cloud architectures often spread things across multiple instances, zones, and regions with backups in place, specifically to reduce the impact when something does fail.

979
01:18:05,902 --> 01:18:12,996
For example, if one application instance becomes unhealthy, a load balancer can direct traffic to other healthy instances.

980
01:18:13,518 --> 01:18:17,412
This helps keep the application available while the team investigates the failure.

981
01:18:17,870 --> 01:18:22,532
However, simply running multiple instances doesn't guarantee high availability.

982
01:18:23,054 --> 01:18:30,532
The architecture also needs to account for dependencies, database failures, network issues, and other potential points of failure.

983
01:18:31,022 --> 01:18:32,592
And then there's cost.

984
01:18:33,102 --> 01:18:34,980
Cloud infrastructure isn't free.

985
01:18:35,374 --> 01:18:40,432
Compute, storage, networking, and databases all come with a price tag attached.

986
01:18:40,814 --> 01:18:45,348
So developers eventually start thinking about something beyond, does this code work?

987
01:18:45,774 --> 01:18:50,244
They also start asking, how much does this application actually cost to run?

988
01:18:50,766 --> 01:18:53,552
That's a very real part of modern software engineering.

989
01:18:53,806 --> 01:18:58,404
You might be wondering, am I expected to become a cloud administrator as a developer?

990
01:18:58,926 --> 01:19:00,112
Not necessarily.

991
01:19:00,558 --> 01:19:02,852
Your role depends on the company and the team.

992
01:19:03,374 --> 01:19:07,748
But even application developers commonly interact with cloud systems day to day.

993
01:19:08,174 --> 01:19:18,596
You'll open up application logs to see what happened, check the status of a deployment, look at environment variables, glance at metrics, and confirm whether a service is actually healthy.

994
01:19:19,150 --> 01:19:28,228
When something breaks, you might check database connectivity, dig into why a deployment failed, or investigate why an API suddenly started returning errors.

995
01:19:28,686 --> 01:19:39,876
For example, if an API suddenly becomes slow, you might check its response times, look for errors in the logs, and see whether the database or another dependent service is causing the delay.

996
01:19:40,334 --> 01:19:49,540
You may also use cloud dashboards to understand resource usage, identify unhealthy services, and determine whether an issue started after a recent deployment.

997
01:19:50,062 --> 01:19:53,476
These clues help you narrow down the problem before making changes.

998
01:19:54,062 --> 01:19:57,796
So you don't necessarily need to know every cloud service that exists.

999
01:19:58,414 --> 01:20:05,956
You need to understand how your application fits into the cloud environment around it, and that's becoming a basic part of working in IT.

1000
01:20:06,574 --> 01:20:15,072
There's one more part of that environment worth understanding early, because it has nothing to do with whether your code works what it costs to keep running.

1001
01:20:15,406 --> 01:20:19,108
Someone spins up a cloud server to test something on a Friday afternoon.

1002
01:20:19,534 --> 01:20:21,392
They forget about it over the weekend.

1003
01:20:21,742 --> 01:20:27,236
On Monday, a message shows up in a shared channel asking whose instance has been running since Friday.

1004
01:20:27,854 --> 01:20:32,196
This happens often enough in real companies that most of them build small habits around it.

1005
01:20:32,654 --> 01:20:39,488
Cloud resources are built by usage, not by a fixed yearly fee the way older on-site hardware often was.

1006
01:20:39,854 --> 01:20:47,716
A server, a database, or a block of storage keeps costing money for every hour it exists, whether or not anyone is actually using it.

1007
01:20:48,174 --> 01:20:51,844
Cloud infrastructure is appealing because you can spin something up in minutes.

1008
01:20:52,526 --> 01:20:57,284
That same convenience is exactly what makes it easy to accidentally leave something running.

1009
01:20:57,838 --> 01:21:04,676
Here's the simplest habit, and one of the first things many developers are told on day one at a company that takes the cloud seriously.

1010
01:21:05,168 --> 01:21:11,108
Stop or delete anything you spun up for testing once you're done with it, especially before a weekend.

1011
01:21:11,694 --> 01:21:22,820
It sounds almost too obvious to mention, and it's also one of the most repeated pieces of advice in real engineering onboarding because the mistake is so easy to make and so visible when it happens.

1012
01:21:23,534 --> 01:21:30,500
Most cloud providers also offer tagging, a way of labeling resources with who created them and what project they belong to.

1013
01:21:31,118 --> 01:21:36,324
Many also offer budgets or alerts that notify someone when spending crosses a threshold.

1014
01:21:36,846 --> 01:21:46,436
These exist specifically because which team's resource is this and why is it still running is a common enough question that it needed tooling, not just good intentions.

1015
01:21:47,086 --> 01:21:57,732
Cost isn't usually a developer's main job, but it's part of the judgment that separates someone who thinks only about whether code works from someone who thinks about what it costs to keep working.

1016
01:21:58,382 --> 01:22:07,012
A feature that works perfectly and quietly runs up an unnecessary bill every month is still a problem, just one that shows up on a different kind of dashboard.

1017
01:22:07,502 --> 01:22:15,568
That same judgment, knowing which parts of the job are actually yours, is worth applying to your career path too, not just your cloud bill.

1018
01:22:15,808 --> 01:22:17,700
Should I become a DevOps engineer?

1019
01:22:17,934 --> 01:22:27,236
Is a question a lot of developers ask without being totally sure what the job actually involves day to day versus what a job title just sounds like it should involve.

1020
01:22:27,886 --> 01:22:37,060
These roles overlap more than their name suggest, and the boundaries shift from company to company, so treat what follows as a rough map, not a strict definition.

1021
01:22:37,838 --> 01:22:39,748
Start with the one you already know.

1022
01:22:40,398 --> 01:22:46,116
A software developer mainly writes the application itself, the features customers actually interact with.

1023
01:22:46,862 --> 01:22:49,508
A DevOps engineer is working on a different layer.

1024
01:22:49,966 --> 01:22:54,212
They focus on the systems that build, test, and deploy that application.

1025
01:22:54,702 --> 01:23:02,212
That means the pipelines, the infrastructure, and the automation that gets code from a developer's machine into production reliably.

1026
01:23:02,830 --> 01:23:07,396
Then there's site reliability engineering, usually shortened to SRE.

1027
01:23:08,046 --> 01:23:13,700
It grew out of a similar need, but it leans further toward keeping things that already exist running well.

1028
01:23:14,286 --> 01:23:23,556
That means deciding how reliable a service needs to be, watching for when it isn't, and often writing code that cuts down on manual, repetitive operational work.

1029
01:23:24,238 --> 01:23:37,188
Platform engineering is a newer, related focus again, building the internal tools and self-service systems that let other developers deploy and operate their own services without needing deep infrastructure expertise themselves.

1030
01:23:37,774 --> 01:23:44,580
In a lot of companies, that's what lets a growing engineering team avoid needing a DevOps specialist embedded in every single team.

1031
01:23:45,230 --> 01:23:52,740
And DevSecOps takes that same DevOps idea, the build and deploy pipeline, and folds security checks directly into it.

1032
01:23:53,262 --> 01:24:02,596
It scans code and dependencies for known vulnerabilities automatically, as part of every change, instead of treating security as a separate review bolted on at the end.

1033
01:24:03,310 --> 01:24:09,284
None of these are strictly more advanced than writing application code, despite how the industry sometimes talks about them.

1034
01:24:09,870 --> 01:24:13,188
They're different focuses on different parts of the same system.

1035
01:24:13,710 --> 01:24:22,148
The feature itself, the pipeline that ships it, the infrastructure it runs on, the reliability of all of it, and the security of all of it.

1036
01:24:22,734 --> 01:24:32,804
Plenty of developers move between these focuses more than once across a career, and plenty of companies blend several of these responsibilities into a single role, especially smaller ones.

1037
01:24:33,646 --> 01:24:41,056
If you're trying to decide what to lean toward, a reasonable question to ask yourself is which part of a production incident you find most interesting?

1038
01:24:41,296 --> 01:24:46,628
If it's why did this specific feature behave wrong, that's closer to application development.

1039
01:24:47,022 --> 01:24:51,844
If it's why did our deployment pipeline let this through, that's closer to DevOps.

1040
01:24:52,238 --> 01:24:58,020
If it's, why didn't we catch this was getting slower weeks before it broke, that's closer to SRE.

1041
01:24:58,510 --> 01:25:02,884
There's no wrong answer here, and the honest truth is you don't have to know yet.

1042
01:25:03,406 --> 01:25:09,892
Most people figure this out by actually doing the work for a while, not by picking a lane before their first job even starts.

1043
01:25:10,350 --> 01:25:12,004
Let's recap module 7.

1044
01:25:12,526 --> 01:25:19,076
The cloud is still computers, storage, networks, and databases, and you rent them instead of buying them.

1045
01:25:19,598 --> 01:25:25,828
Most cloud apps use the same few building blocks, and how much you manage yourself depends on the services you choose.

1046
01:25:26,478 --> 01:25:31,812
You saw two ways to scale and why extra copies alone don't guarantee an app stays up.

1047
01:25:32,174 --> 01:25:40,836
You also saw what a developer really touches in the cloud, like logs, deployments, and metrics, and anything you leave running keeps costing money.

1048
01:25:41,486 --> 01:25:45,604
Then we looked at roles like DevOps, SRE, and platform engineering.

1049
01:25:45,998 --> 01:25:47,524
You don't have to pick one yet.

1050
01:25:47,854 --> 01:25:51,332
Whichever you lean toward, one change is reaching all of them.

1051
01:25:51,630 --> 01:25:53,776
And module 8 is about that change.

1052
01:25:54,062 --> 01:25:55,696
Welcome to module 8.

1053
01:25:56,046 --> 01:26:00,324
You've now seen the whole route, from a ticket to an app running in the cloud.

1054
01:26:00,814 --> 01:26:03,748
There's one question you've probably been asking for a while.

1055
01:26:04,270 --> 01:26:08,292
AI tools can write code now, so does any of this still matter?

1056
01:26:08,846 --> 01:26:13,604
We'll start with what these tools can do today and where developers use them on normal tickets.

1057
01:26:14,286 --> 01:26:17,892
You'll also see how they get things wrong in ways that are hard to notice.

1058
01:26:18,382 --> 01:26:23,044
Then you'll read through one AI written change with me and look for the problems yourself.

1059
01:26:23,598 --> 01:26:25,988
The last part steps away from the code editor.

1060
01:26:26,286 --> 01:26:31,172
It looks at other roles on a team and at how companies are changing the way they plan their work.

1061
01:26:31,630 --> 01:26:36,708
By the end of this module, you'll be able to check an AI written change instead of just accepting it.

1062
01:26:37,230 --> 01:26:38,816
Let's start with today's tools.

1063
01:26:39,022 --> 01:26:42,724
There was a time when AI encoding just meant autocomplete.

1064
01:26:43,054 --> 01:26:46,116
It finished your line, maybe suggested a small function.

1065
01:26:46,670 --> 01:26:48,292
That's not what it means anymore.

1066
01:26:48,750 --> 01:26:55,716
Today, developers work inside AI native IDEs like cursor, Claude Code, and Copilot's agent mode.

1067
01:26:56,270 --> 01:26:58,308
These tools don't just suggest a line.

1068
01:26:58,670 --> 01:27:07,492
You can hand them a task and they'll read across your code base, edit multiple files, run your tests, see what failed, and try again, mostly on their own.

1069
01:27:08,142 --> 01:27:10,020
Think about what that means in practice.

1070
01:27:10,606 --> 01:27:20,708
A task that once required you to jump between files, trace dependencies, make changes, and run tests manually can now be handled by an AI agent in one workflow.

1071
01:27:21,166 --> 01:27:23,204
That's a real jump in capability.

1072
01:27:23,822 --> 01:27:25,764
But here's the part that hasn't changed.

1073
01:27:26,126 --> 01:27:29,296
These tools only know what's inside their context.

1074
01:27:29,678 --> 01:27:41,700
They don't actually know why a workaround exists in your billing code, or what a stakeholder really meant by a vague ticket, or which obvious refactor will quietly break a rule nobody wrote down anywhere.

1075
01:27:42,286 --> 01:27:46,852
And even when the code looks clean and the tests pass, there can still be a problem.

1076
01:27:47,310 --> 01:27:53,732
The tests might not cover that business rule, or the agent might have misunderstood what the feature was supposed to do in the first place.

1077
01:27:54,510 --> 01:28:02,660
Let's say you have a five-year-old application with thousands of files, internal APIs, business rules, and assumptions nobody documented.

1078
01:28:03,182 --> 01:28:06,372
You can hand the whole task to an agent and get something that runs.

1079
01:28:06,894 --> 01:28:08,388
That doesn't mean it's correct.

1080
01:28:08,750 --> 01:28:15,524
You still need to understand the problem, give the tool the right context, and actually review what comes back before it ships.

1081
01:28:16,078 --> 01:28:19,044
This is why understanding the code base still matters.

1082
01:28:19,502 --> 01:28:27,652
The better you understand the system, the better you can guide the agent, spot mistakes, and decide whether its solution is actually safe to release.

1083
01:28:28,366 --> 01:28:32,548
So the useful mindset isn't the AI will handle this for me now.

1084
01:28:32,878 --> 01:28:43,168
It's the AI can do a lot more of the work, which means my job has shifted from typing code to reviewing and directing it, and I'm still the one responsible for the result.

1085
01:28:43,534 --> 01:28:45,604
Let's take a normal development task.

1086
01:28:46,062 --> 01:28:50,756
You receive a ticket saying, add validation to the customer registration API.

1087
01:28:51,246 --> 01:28:56,612
In an AI native IDE, a developer might just describe that task directly to the agent.

1088
01:28:57,038 --> 01:29:10,692
It can go explore the existing code on its own, find the registration endpoint, draft the validation logic, write a few tests, run them, and fix anything that fails, often before the developer has read a single line of the existing file.

1089
01:29:11,246 --> 01:29:12,740
But notice what's still true.

1090
01:29:13,230 --> 01:29:15,940
Someone still had to write that ticket clearly.

1091
01:29:16,366 --> 01:29:24,996
Someone still decided what valid actually means for this business, whether an empty phone number should be allowed, whether existing users are grandfathered in.

1092
01:29:25,582 --> 01:29:28,768
The agent can execute a well-scoped task fast.

1093
01:29:29,230 --> 01:29:31,588
It can't decide what the right scope is.

1094
01:29:32,366 --> 01:29:34,468
And this is where experience makes a difference.

1095
01:29:35,118 --> 01:29:45,284
A developer who understands the business can spot missing requirements, ask the right questions, and give the agent enough context to avoid making assumptions that could cause problems later.

1096
01:29:45,774 --> 01:29:56,868
And once the agent produces a change, the developer still opens the diff, reads through what actually changed across those files, checks it against the ticket, and sends it through code review like anything else.

1097
01:29:57,454 --> 01:30:08,484
That review matters because the agent might solve the immediate problem while introducing a different one, such as breaking an existing API contract or changing how another part of the application behaves.

1098
01:30:09,166 --> 01:30:12,292
So the workflow hasn't disappeared, it's shifted.

1099
01:30:12,782 --> 01:30:20,228
Less time typing the implementation, more time scoping the task clearly up front, and reviewing the result carefully afterward.

1100
01:30:20,782 --> 01:30:24,004
Experienced developers know exactly where that balance sits.

1101
01:30:24,654 --> 01:30:30,688
When to just let the agent run, and when a task is sensitive enough that they need to slow down and drive it themselves.

1102
01:30:31,022 --> 01:30:33,808
Here's the part beginners really need to understand.

1103
01:30:34,094 --> 01:30:38,372
And it matters even more now that AI can touch your whole code base at once.

1104
01:30:38,990 --> 01:30:45,316
An agent can change 10 files and every single test can pass, and the change can still be wrong.

1105
01:30:45,870 --> 01:30:49,488
It might quietly break a business rule that wasn't written down anywhere.

1106
01:30:49,742 --> 01:30:56,196
It might clean up a piece of logic that looked redundant but was actually handling a rare, important case.

1107
01:30:56,846 --> 01:31:01,412
It might introduce a security gap while technically solving the ticket exactly as written.

1108
01:31:01,998 --> 01:31:03,588
And this is the tricky part.

1109
01:31:03,950 --> 01:31:07,428
A small AI suggestion that's wrong is easy to spot.

1110
01:31:07,888 --> 01:31:15,780
A large multi-file AI change that's mostly right and subtly wrong is much harder to catch, because it looks thorough.

1111
01:31:16,078 --> 01:31:16,688
It ran.

1112
01:31:17,102 --> 01:31:18,244
The tests passed.

1113
01:31:18,638 --> 01:31:21,444
It reads like something a careful engineer would write.

1114
01:31:22,030 --> 01:31:27,108
So when you're reviewing AI-generated code, don't stop at asking whether the tests passed.

1115
01:31:27,630 --> 01:31:36,356
Ask yourself what the code actually changed, why those changes were necessary, and whether anything else in the application depends on the old behavior.

1116
01:31:36,910 --> 01:31:40,388
That's exactly why review matters more now, not less.

1117
01:31:40,942 --> 01:31:51,204
You still read through what changed, you still run it yourself, you still check it against the actual business requirement, and you still ask what this change assumed that might not be true.

1118
01:31:51,854 --> 01:31:56,164
Approving an AI-generated change you don't understand doesn't save you time.

1119
01:31:56,590 --> 01:31:59,556
It just moves the hard part to whenever it eventually breaks.

1120
01:31:59,950 --> 01:32:04,164
Except now you're debugging code you never actually understood in the first place.

1121
01:32:04,526 --> 01:32:13,380
If you take one sentence out of this section, make it this one The better your fundamentals are, the more useful an AI native IDE becomes.

1122
01:32:14,062 --> 01:32:14,756
Here's why.

1123
01:32:15,470 --> 01:32:19,876
An agent hands you a change across six files using a pattern you've never seen.

1124
01:32:20,462 --> 01:32:26,468
If you don't understand programming, APIs, databases, and Git, you'll accept it and hope.

1125
01:32:26,958 --> 01:32:37,092
If you do understand them, you can read that diff, spot the bad assumption, ask the agent to fix just that part, and judge whether the whole approach even fits your application.

1126
01:32:37,614 --> 01:32:40,388
And the same principle applies when something goes wrong.

1127
01:32:40,814 --> 01:32:50,852
If you understand how the pieces fit together, you can trace the problem, give the agent useful feedback, and guide it toward a better solution instead of blindly asking it to try again.

1128
01:32:51,470 --> 01:32:53,252
Take Git as another example.

1129
01:32:53,710 --> 01:33:03,780
If you understand branches, commits, and diffs, you can see exactly what the agent changed, compare it with the original implementation, and undo a change if something goes wrong.

1130
01:33:04,334 --> 01:33:09,252
Without that knowledge, you're relying on the tool without knowing how to recover when things break.

1131
01:33:09,742 --> 01:33:12,292
The same goes for databases and APIs.

1132
01:33:12,846 --> 01:33:25,028
When an agent changes a database query or modifies an API response, your fundamentals help you recognize whether the change could affect performance, break another service, or cause problems for existing users.

1133
01:33:25,614 --> 01:33:37,860
You don't need to memorize every programming language or understand every framework in advance, but you do need a solid foundation, because that's what helps you separate a good solution from one that only looks good.

1134
01:33:38,446 --> 01:33:40,900
Same tool, completely different outcome.

1135
01:33:41,358 --> 01:33:42,928
The difference is you.

1136
01:33:43,470 --> 01:33:45,028
So let's actually do it.

1137
01:33:45,422 --> 01:33:50,768
In the next lecture, we're going to look at an AI-generated change together and find what's wrong with it.

1138
01:33:51,022 --> 01:33:52,048
Let's make this real.

1139
01:33:52,494 --> 01:33:53,648
Same ticket as before.

1140
01:33:53,966 --> 01:33:56,740
Add email validation to customer registration.

1141
01:33:57,102 --> 01:33:59,300
You describe it to the agent in your IDE.

1142
01:33:59,662 --> 01:34:01,248
Two minutes later, it's done.

1143
01:34:01,550 --> 01:34:02,372
Tests pass.

1144
01:34:02,798 --> 01:34:03,780
Here's the change.

1145
01:34:04,238 --> 01:34:07,108
Now, most beginners look at this and see two things.

1146
01:34:07,598 --> 01:34:10,340
Green means added, tests are green, ship it.

1147
01:34:10,798 --> 01:34:13,604
Let's slow down and read it the way a reviewer reads it.

1148
01:34:13,998 --> 01:34:14,852
Start at the top.

1149
01:34:15,246 --> 01:34:18,340
The agent added a function called isvalidEmail.

1150
01:34:18,702 --> 01:34:18,928
Good.

1151
01:34:19,214 --> 01:34:20,048
It's reusable.

1152
01:34:20,128 --> 01:34:21,060
That's what we'd want.

1153
01:34:21,454 --> 01:34:24,388
Inside, there's a pattern that checks the email format.

1154
01:34:24,750 --> 01:34:29,408
Letters and numbers, an at sign, a domain, a dot, a couple of letters.

1155
01:34:29,710 --> 01:34:30,500
Looks reasonable.

1156
01:34:30,894 --> 01:34:33,968
Look closer at the characters it allows before the at sign.

1157
01:34:34,510 --> 01:34:38,244
Letters, numbers, dots, underscores, hyphens.

1158
01:34:38,894 --> 01:34:40,932
Now think about real email addresses.

1159
01:34:41,422 --> 01:34:43,428
Have you ever seen one with a plus sign in it?

1160
01:34:43,662 --> 01:34:46,692
Like name plus newsletter at gmail.com?

1161
01:34:47,054 --> 01:34:48,208
Lots of people use those.

1162
01:34:48,462 --> 01:34:51,780
They're completely valid, and this pattern rejects them.

1163
01:34:52,238 --> 01:34:53,648
Every test passed.

1164
01:34:53,998 --> 01:34:59,716
Because the agent also wrote the tests, and it only tested addresses it already believed were valid.

1165
01:34:59,982 --> 01:35:00,928
That's the trap.

1166
01:35:01,230 --> 01:35:04,964
The tests agreed with the code because the same assumption wrote both.

1167
01:35:05,614 --> 01:35:06,208
Keep going.

1168
01:35:06,606 --> 01:35:09,988
The new check runs every time someone hits the registration endpoint.

1169
01:35:10,318 --> 01:35:11,556
Now scroll to the ticket.

1170
01:35:11,822 --> 01:35:16,848
The acceptance criteria said existing valid registrations must continue working.

1171
01:35:17,230 --> 01:35:19,460
Does anything here touch existing users?

1172
01:35:19,982 --> 01:35:22,448
Actually, look at where the agent put the call.

1173
01:35:22,702 --> 01:35:26,532
It's in the shared save customer function, not the register function.

1174
01:35:26,862 --> 01:35:30,688
That function also runs when an existing customer updates their profile.

1175
01:35:30,990 --> 01:35:36,132
So a longtime customer with a plus sign in their email would now be blocked from changing their address.

1176
01:35:36,750 --> 01:35:37,924
That's two problems.

1177
01:35:38,382 --> 01:35:40,804
One is a wrong assumption inside a pattern.

1178
01:35:41,166 --> 01:35:43,268
The other is a change in the wrong place.

1179
01:35:43,758 --> 01:35:45,796
Neither one shows up as a red test.

1180
01:35:46,126 --> 01:35:48,708
Both would show up as a support ticket next week.

1181
01:35:49,134 --> 01:35:50,820
So what does the review look like?

1182
01:35:51,118 --> 01:35:52,580
Not this is wrong.

1183
01:35:52,848 --> 01:35:56,804
Something like, this pattern rejects plus addresses, which are valid.

1184
01:35:57,134 --> 01:36:01,572
Also, the validation runs in Save Customer, so it affects profile updates.

1185
01:36:02,030 --> 01:36:05,476
Can we move it to the registered path and add a test for a plus address?

1186
01:36:05,934 --> 01:36:09,252
Then you hand that back to the agent and it'll fix it in 30 seconds.

1187
01:36:09,678 --> 01:36:10,884
That's the whole skill.

1188
01:36:11,246 --> 01:36:13,316
You're not competing with the tool on typing.

1189
01:36:13,646 --> 01:36:19,488
You're reading its work against the ticket, against the business, and against what real users actually do.

1190
01:36:19,918 --> 01:36:22,788
You'll get a diff like this in assignment 3 for this section.

1191
01:36:23,214 --> 01:36:26,916
Try to find both problems yourself before you look at the answer.

1192
01:36:27,470 --> 01:36:31,524
Now let's zoom out, because this isn't only happening to developers.

1193
01:36:31,918 --> 01:36:34,852
And this shift isn't only happening in code editors.

1194
01:36:35,310 --> 01:36:53,060
The same kind of AI agent is showing up across IT, summarizing a messy incident timeline for a support engineer, drafting and explaining SQL for someone on the data team, generating a first pass of test scenarios for a tester, or pulling together a project update for a manager before a meeting.

1195
01:36:53,614 --> 01:37:02,084
For example, a data engineer might use AI to draft a SQL query, but still needs to check whether the joins are correct and the results make sense.

1196
01:37:02,766 --> 01:37:09,860
A tester might generate dozens of test cases in seconds, but still needs to decide whether the important edge cases are covered.

1197
01:37:10,382 --> 01:37:15,012
The work gets faster, but knowing what good work looks like still matters.

1198
01:37:15,598 --> 01:37:21,156
So when you enter IT today, don't think of AI as one tool bolted onto your editor.

1199
01:37:21,614 --> 01:37:33,508
Think of it as a layer running underneath almost every role on a team, doing more of the first draft so people can spend more time deciding, reviewing, and judging whether that first draft is actually right.

1200
01:37:33,998 --> 01:37:36,772
And this changes what it means to be good at your job.

1201
01:37:37,230 --> 01:37:40,676
It's no longer just about how quickly you can produce something yourself.

1202
01:37:41,102 --> 01:37:48,292
It's also about how well you can use these tools, recognize their mistakes, and take responsibility for the final outcome.

1203
01:37:48,782 --> 01:37:50,884
The specific tools will keep changing.

1204
01:37:51,342 --> 01:37:59,216
The skill that doesn't go out of date is knowing when to hand something to AI, how to direct it well, and how to verify what it hands back.

1205
01:37:59,534 --> 01:38:08,656
AI-native IDEs aren't just changing how individual developers write code, they're starting to change how entire companies plan and build software.

1206
01:38:09,102 --> 01:38:11,908
Think about how software development has evolved over time.

1207
01:38:12,526 --> 01:38:18,308
Years ago, teams used Waterfall, planning everything up front and building it in one long sequence.

1208
01:38:18,926 --> 01:38:22,532
Then came Agile, breaking that into short, iterative cycles.

1209
01:38:23,086 --> 01:38:30,276
Then CICD and DevOps, automating testing and deployment so releases could happen constantly instead of once a year.

1210
01:38:30,734 --> 01:38:35,588
AI is becoming the next layer on top of that evolution, not a replacement for it.

1211
01:38:36,334 --> 01:38:37,540
Here's the interesting part.

1212
01:38:37,966 --> 01:38:43,492
Now that an agent can build and test a well-defined task in minutes, the bottleneck has moved.

1213
01:38:43,822 --> 01:38:50,564
It's no longer how fast can we write this code, it's how clearly can we define what we actually want built.

1214
01:38:51,022 --> 01:38:55,780
Companies are realizing they need to invest more, not less, into the planning stage.

1215
01:38:56,238 --> 01:39:04,196
Real effort goes into defining the problem, the requirements, and what is explicitly out of scope before any agent touches the code.

1216
01:39:04,686 --> 01:39:07,236
You might hear people call this shifting left.

1217
01:39:07,662 --> 01:39:10,276
Strictly speaking, that phrase comes from testing.

1218
01:39:10,670 --> 01:39:14,596
It means running your tests earlier in the process instead of at the very end.

1219
01:39:15,022 --> 01:39:16,452
But the spirit is the same.

1220
01:39:16,910 --> 01:39:19,460
Catch the problem while it's still cheap to fix.

1221
01:39:20,302 --> 01:39:22,020
Why does that matter so much now?

1222
01:39:22,510 --> 01:39:28,548
Because an AI agent follows instructions literally, and it can move very fast in the wrong direction.

1223
01:39:29,102 --> 01:39:35,044
Give it a vague, half-finished requirement, and it will guess, confidently and often incorrectly.

1224
01:39:35,598 --> 01:39:43,364
Give it a clear, well-scoped problem, including what's explicitly out of scope, and it can move remarkably fast in the right direction.

1225
01:39:44,270 --> 01:39:46,436
None of this replaces human judgment.

1226
01:39:46,830 --> 01:39:53,316
Deciding whether a problem is even worth solving or whether a product is worth building at all is still a human call.

1227
01:39:53,838 --> 01:39:56,996
AI can execute that decision faster once it's made.

1228
01:39:57,358 --> 01:39:59,268
It doesn't make the decision for you.

1229
01:40:00,110 --> 01:40:02,820
You'll likely see this play out wherever you end up working.

1230
01:40:03,246 --> 01:40:15,332
Smaller, more flexible teams, more time spent up front turning a rough idea into a clear, detailed plan, and AI native tools handling more of the actual implementation once that plan exists.

1231
01:40:16,046 --> 01:40:25,764
And the fundamentals you're learning in this course, understanding requirements, systems, and how the pieces fit together are exactly what makes you valuable in that kind of environment.

1232
01:40:26,030 --> 01:40:27,684
Let's recap module 8.

1233
01:40:28,142 --> 01:40:31,460
We started with a question, and the answer is yes.

1234
01:40:31,886 --> 01:40:34,148
Everything you've learned still matters.

1235
01:40:34,574 --> 01:40:46,052
An agent can read your code, change many files, and run the tests mostly on its own, but it only knows what's in its context, so a change can pass every test and still be wrong.

1236
01:40:46,542 --> 01:40:47,908
You saw that in our review.

1237
01:40:48,270 --> 01:40:52,196
The email check rejected valid addresses and ran in the wrong function.

1238
01:40:52,622 --> 01:40:57,056
The agent wrote its own tests, so they passed, and neither problem showed up.

1239
01:40:57,422 --> 01:40:59,524
Your basics are what let you catch that.

1240
01:41:00,078 --> 01:41:08,336
AI is changing other roles too, and companies are spending more time on planning because a vague request can now go wrong very fast.

1241
01:41:08,974 --> 01:41:12,896
In module 9, we leave the code for a while and look at your working day.

1242
01:41:13,070 --> 01:41:14,596
Welcome to module 9.

1243
01:41:15,118 --> 01:41:17,252
Where do your hours go on a real team?

1244
01:41:17,902 --> 01:41:20,804
This is a big module, so here's what it covers.

1245
01:41:21,326 --> 01:41:24,228
The topics are mixed together, so they won't come in this order.

1246
01:41:24,846 --> 01:41:26,816
Some of it is about normal days.

1247
01:41:27,054 --> 01:41:30,816
That means your meetings and what it's like to work across time zones.

1248
01:41:31,182 --> 01:41:35,076
It also means the many hours that go into keeping older software running.

1249
01:41:35,758 --> 01:41:37,776
Some of it is about the bad days.

1250
01:41:38,094 --> 01:41:41,776
That's when production has a problem, or when you're the one on call.

1251
01:41:42,222 --> 01:41:46,212
You'll also see how a team watches its systems so it can find trouble early.

1252
01:41:46,894 --> 01:41:49,732
The rest is about you as the new person on the team.

1253
01:41:50,222 --> 01:41:55,748
You'll learn how to ask for help, what to do when you first join, and how to write things people will read.

1254
01:41:56,238 --> 01:42:03,568
A few topics come up twice, like team review meetings and production problems, so if something sounds familiar, you're not lost.

1255
01:42:03,822 --> 01:42:05,924
We're just going a little deeper the second time.

1256
01:42:06,606 --> 01:42:10,372
By the end of this module, you'll know what to expect from your days on a team.

1257
01:42:10,926 --> 01:42:12,516
Let's start with a normal day.

1258
01:42:12,814 --> 01:42:18,436
A developer's day usually doesn't start with opening an editor and coding for eight straight hours.

1259
01:42:18,926 --> 01:42:27,076
A normal day could start with a standup meeting, where you talk about what you worked on yesterday, what you're working on today, and whether anything is blocking you.

1260
01:42:27,694 --> 01:42:32,448
These meetings are usually short, but they help the team understand where things stand.

1261
01:42:32,974 --> 01:42:40,292
If you're stuck waiting for another team or need help with a technical problem, this is a chance to bring it up before it delays your work further.

1262
01:42:40,942 --> 01:42:55,268
Then you might spend the next couple of hours working on your ticket, reading existing code, searching the repository, checking a database query, running the application, looking at logs, and probably asking another developer a question at some point.

1263
01:42:55,822 --> 01:43:00,868
And sometimes, what looks like a small task turns out to be much bigger than expected.

1264
01:43:01,422 --> 01:43:12,868
You might discover that the code depends on another service, that the existing behavior isn't documented, or that you need to understand how an older part of the application works before you can safely make a change.

1265
01:43:13,454 --> 01:43:15,940
Then you might have a meeting about a new requirement.

1266
01:43:16,462 --> 01:43:20,100
Later, someone reviews your pull request and asks you to change something.

1267
01:43:20,590 --> 01:43:24,420
You make the change, run the tests again, and update the pull request.

1268
01:43:24,942 --> 01:43:27,716
This is also where communication becomes important.

1269
01:43:28,142 --> 01:43:37,252
You might need to explain why you chose a particular approach, discuss an alternative with a teammate, or clarify a requirement with the person who requested the change.

1270
01:43:37,838 --> 01:43:41,636
Writing code is only one part of getting the right solution delivered.

1271
01:43:42,254 --> 01:43:48,868
And then you might spend the afternoon investigating a completely different problem because something broke in another part of the system.

1272
01:43:49,454 --> 01:43:50,148
That's normal.

1273
01:43:50,638 --> 01:43:54,276
Some days have lots of coding, some days have almost none.

1274
01:43:54,958 --> 01:44:05,764
As you gain experience, you'll also get better at managing these interruptions, deciding what needs attention first, and knowing when to ask for help instead of spending hours stuck on the same problem.

1275
01:44:06,414 --> 01:44:15,328
The job is really a combination of coding, debugging, communication, investigation, meetings, reviews, and problem solving.

1276
01:44:15,502 --> 01:44:18,404
You will probably hear the word stand-up quite a lot.

1277
01:44:18,862 --> 01:44:24,900
A typical daily stand-up is a short team meeting where everyone shares what they're working on and whether they're blocked.

1278
01:44:25,294 --> 01:44:30,020
It usually sounds something like, yesterday I finished the customer API changes.

1279
01:44:30,478 --> 01:44:32,580
Today I'm working on the validation ticket.

1280
01:44:33,070 --> 01:44:36,036
I'm blocked because I don't have access to the test database.

1281
01:44:36,686 --> 01:44:40,420
That's useful because everyone gets visibility into what the team is doing.

1282
01:44:41,038 --> 01:44:43,076
You'll also hear a handful of other terms.

1283
01:44:43,566 --> 01:44:47,972
A sprint is a fixed period during which a team works on a set of planned work.

1284
01:44:48,398 --> 01:44:51,460
The backlog is the list of work that may need to be done.

1285
01:44:51,950 --> 01:44:55,236
Planning is when the team decides what work to take on next.

1286
01:44:55,534 --> 01:45:01,124
And a retrospective is when the team discusses what went well, what didn't, and what could improve.

1287
01:45:01,646 --> 01:45:10,532
A retro is worth understanding properly because it's one of the few meetings built entirely around improving how the team works, not just what the team ships.

1288
01:45:11,086 --> 01:45:18,756
It usually follows a simple pattern: what went well, what didn't, and what the team will actually try differently next sprint.

1289
01:45:19,214 --> 01:45:30,980
The value isn't in the complaining part, it's in that last step, walking away with one or two concrete changes, rather than just a list of frustrations that gets repeated every retro with nothing changing.

1290
01:45:31,598 --> 01:45:34,148
As a junior, you don't need to run these meetings.

1291
01:45:34,542 --> 01:45:36,292
You just need to actually contribute.

1292
01:45:36,782 --> 01:45:41,636
A small process friction you noticed is exactly the kind of thing retros exist to surface.

1293
01:45:42,318 --> 01:45:46,340
Different companies follow Scrum, Kanban, or something customized.

1294
01:45:46,926 --> 01:45:49,540
Don't get too caught up in memorizing the terminology.

1295
01:45:49,998 --> 01:45:54,308
The important thing is understanding that software development is a team activity.

1296
01:45:54,766 --> 01:45:57,220
Your code is only one part of the process.

1297
01:45:57,774 --> 01:46:04,516
And on some teams, that daily standup doesn't even happen live because the team isn't all working the same hours to begin with.

1298
01:46:04,782 --> 01:46:09,764
A growing number of teams aren't all sitting in the same office, or even the same country.

1299
01:46:10,190 --> 01:46:13,348
You might have a teammate finishing their day just as yours starts.

1300
01:46:13,966 --> 01:46:18,528
That changes how the team actually communicates, and it's worth understanding early.

1301
01:46:19,086 --> 01:46:26,660
The biggest shift is that async, meaning not expecting an immediate reply, often becomes the default instead of the exception.

1302
01:46:27,214 --> 01:46:30,048
If you ask a one-line question like, does this work?

1303
01:46:30,318 --> 01:46:39,652
to someone eight hours ahead of you, you might not hear back until tomorrow, and then you'll likely need another full day for the follow-up question you didn't think to ask up front.

1304
01:46:40,238 --> 01:46:48,740
The fix is writing messages with enough context the first time, what you're trying to do, what you've already tried, and exactly what you need from them.

1305
01:46:49,134 --> 01:46:53,924
So a single reply can actually unblock you instead of starting a slow back and forth.

1306
01:46:54,574 --> 01:46:58,404
For example, instead of saying, I'm getting an error, can you help?

1307
01:46:58,734 --> 01:47:05,540
Explain which application you're working on, what you were trying to do, the error you received, and what you've already checked.

1308
01:47:05,998 --> 01:47:09,248
If you can include a relevant log or screenshot, even better.

1309
01:47:09,678 --> 01:47:15,652
Your teammate can investigate while you're offline, rather than spending another day just trying to understand the problem.

1310
01:47:16,398 --> 01:47:19,780
Some distributed teams also run stand-up asynchronously.

1311
01:47:20,142 --> 01:47:28,836
Everyone posts their yesterday, today, and blockers in a channel instead of talking live, precisely because there's no single time that works for everyone.

1312
01:47:29,422 --> 01:47:36,100
That's not a lesser version of a stand-up, it's a reasonable adaptation once a team is genuinely spread across time zones.

1313
01:47:36,814 --> 01:47:40,484
This also means you need to get comfortable making progress independently.

1314
01:47:41,006 --> 01:47:49,540
If you're blocked on one task, you might document what you found, move to another ticket, or investigate a separate part of the problem while you wait for a response.

1315
01:47:50,062 --> 01:47:53,988
That way, your entire day doesn't depend on someone else being available.

1316
01:47:54,606 --> 01:47:56,804
And here's something worth internalizing early.

1317
01:47:57,358 --> 01:48:00,196
A fast reply doesn't make you look more dedicated.

1318
01:48:00,494 --> 01:48:04,420
And a slower one, within reason, doesn't make you look less committed.

1319
01:48:04,878 --> 01:48:13,668
What actually matters on a distributed team is whether your communication gives people what they need without requiring you to be online at the same moment they are.

1320
01:48:14,222 --> 01:48:20,100
Whichever hours your team works, a lot of what actually fills those hours isn't building new features at all.

1321
01:48:20,494 --> 01:48:21,252
It's maintenance.

1322
01:48:21,678 --> 01:48:24,132
Here's something that surprises many beginners.

1323
01:48:24,590 --> 01:48:28,388
Not every developer spends their time building exciting new features.

1324
01:48:28,878 --> 01:48:30,980
A lot of software work is maintenance.

1325
01:48:31,726 --> 01:48:49,604
You might fix a bug that appeared after a release, update an old dependency, improve a slow database query, tweak a configuration value, look into a customer complaint, add some logging, clean out unused code, upgrade a framework version, or modify a feature that's existed for five years.

1326
01:48:50,126 --> 01:48:53,668
And sometimes, the fix itself is surprisingly small.

1327
01:48:54,158 --> 01:49:00,516
A single configuration change might resolve the issue, but finding that setting could take hours of investigation.

1328
01:49:01,134 --> 01:49:05,892
That's because understanding why something broke is often harder than actually fixing it.

1329
01:49:06,446 --> 01:49:12,772
That might sound less exciting than building something brand new, but it's a huge part of how real software works.

1330
01:49:13,326 --> 01:49:16,288
Companies have applications that have been running for years.

1331
01:49:16,686 --> 01:49:22,084
Those applications make money, customers depend on them, and someone has to keep them working.

1332
01:49:22,702 --> 01:49:25,444
Think about an online banking application, for example.

1333
01:49:25,870 --> 01:49:32,836
It might have been built years ago, but customers still use it every day to check balances, transfer money, and pay bills.

1334
01:49:33,486 --> 01:49:39,568
The team can't simply stop everything and rebuild the entire application whenever a better technology comes along.

1335
01:49:39,950 --> 01:49:44,612
They need to improve it carefully, without breaking the things people already rely on.

1336
01:49:45,294 --> 01:49:49,924
A lot of this maintenance work exists because of something you'll hear called technical debt.

1337
01:49:50,446 --> 01:49:55,364
It's not necessarily bad code, it's usually a shortcut that made sense at the time.

1338
01:49:55,694 --> 01:50:03,300
A quick fix shipped to hit a deadline, or a design decision that worked fine until the application grew past what it was originally built for.

1339
01:50:03,886 --> 01:50:09,328
Like financial debt, it's not inherently a disaster, but it accumulates interest.

1340
01:50:09,742 --> 01:50:14,436
The longer it sits unaddressed, the slower and riskier future changes become.

1341
01:50:15,022 --> 01:50:20,324
Part of maintenance work is paying some of that down deliberately, instead of only ever adding more of it.

1342
01:50:20,878 --> 01:50:27,908
For example, imagine a piece of code that works perfectly well today but is difficult to understand and has no tests.

1343
01:50:28,366 --> 01:50:35,044
Every time someone needs to change it, they have to spend extra time figuring out how it works and checking that nothing else breaks.

1344
01:50:35,662 --> 01:50:43,524
Adding tests and simplifying that code might not deliver a new feature, but it can make every future change safer and faster.

1345
01:50:44,046 --> 01:50:53,024
So, when you get your first job, don't be surprised if your first ticket is fix this existing issue instead of build this amazing new application.

1346
01:50:53,198 --> 01:50:55,264
That's normal software development.

1347
01:50:55,598 --> 01:50:59,620
Now let's talk about one of the most stressful situations for a developer.

1348
01:51:00,078 --> 01:51:01,984
Something breaks in production.

1349
01:51:02,350 --> 01:51:12,836
Maybe customers can't log in, maybe an API is suddenly returning errors, maybe orders are getting stuck, or maybe a recent deployment introduced a problem nobody caught.

1350
01:51:13,358 --> 01:51:18,116
The first priority isn't writing new code, it's understanding the impact.

1351
01:51:18,510 --> 01:51:26,052
What's actually broken, how many users are affected, when it started, what changed recently, and what the logs show.

1352
01:51:26,638 --> 01:51:36,100
Depending on what they find, the team might roll back the deployment, disable a feature, fix a bad configuration value, or ship a quick hotfix to stop the bleeding.

1353
01:51:36,686 --> 01:51:43,428
After the problem is under control, the team usually investigates why it happened and what can prevent it from happening again.

1354
01:51:44,046 --> 01:51:48,644
This step is often called a root cause analysis, or a post-incident review.

1355
01:51:49,230 --> 01:51:54,052
A useful one usually asks a chain of questions rather than stopping at the first answer.

1356
01:51:54,574 --> 01:52:00,580
The deployment caused it, sure, but why did that change get through review and testing without anyone catching it?

1357
01:52:00,942 --> 01:52:03,684
And what would need to be true for it to get caught next time?

1358
01:52:04,302 --> 01:52:06,864
The output isn't just a written explanation.

1359
01:52:07,150 --> 01:52:09,828
It's a short list of concrete follow-up actions.

1360
01:52:10,318 --> 01:52:16,932
Better test coverage here, an alert that should have fired sooner there, that someone actually owns and follows up on.

1361
01:52:17,550 --> 01:52:23,984
You'll hear terms like incident, hotfix, rollback, root cause, and post-incident review.

1362
01:52:24,366 --> 01:52:26,052
These aren't just technical words.

1363
01:52:26,382 --> 01:52:29,700
They describe how teams respond when real systems fail.

1364
01:52:30,190 --> 01:52:35,684
And eventually, every developer learns that production problems are just part of working with software.

1365
01:52:36,174 --> 01:52:42,784
And sometimes, that problem shows up outside of normal working hours entirely, which is where on call comes in.

1366
01:52:43,054 --> 01:52:51,332
At some point, you'll hear that a teammate is on call this week, and if you've never experienced it, the phrase alone can sound intimidating.

1367
01:52:51,918 --> 01:52:56,964
It's worth understanding what it actually involves before you ever get anywhere near it yourself.

1368
01:52:57,454 --> 01:53:10,084
On call means that for a set period, usually a week, someone on the team is the person who gets alerted if something breaks outside of normal working hours, a server issue at 2 a.m., a spike of errors on a weekend.

1369
01:53:10,542 --> 01:53:19,044
It exists because real systems don't stop running just because the office is closed, and someone needs to be reachable if something genuinely urgent happens.

1370
01:53:20,078 --> 01:53:25,956
For example, imagine an online shopping application suddenly stops processing payments late at night.

1371
01:53:26,446 --> 01:53:36,900
The on-call engineer might receive an alert, check the monitoring dashboard, look at the error logs, and work out whether the issue is affecting all customers or just a small part of the system.

1372
01:53:37,454 --> 01:53:44,292
The immediate priority is to understand the impact and restore the service safely, not to rush into a random fix.

1373
01:53:44,910 --> 01:53:49,156
For a junior developer, on-call is rarely something you're handed on day one.

1374
01:53:49,710 --> 01:54:00,036
Most teams ease people into it gradually, often shadowing a more experienced teammate first, watching how they respond to an alert before ever being the primary responder yourself.

1375
01:54:00,558 --> 01:54:05,344
And being on call doesn't mean you're expected to fix every problem alone at 2 a.m.

1376
01:54:05,806 --> 01:54:16,100
The actual job, in that moment, is usually to acknowledge the alert, assess how serious it is, and escalate to someone with more context if it's beyond what you can safely handle.

1377
01:54:16,654 --> 01:54:22,500
Knowing when to wake someone else up is a completely legitimate on call skill, not a failure.

1378
01:54:23,054 --> 01:54:27,396
You'll also find that teams prepare for these situations before anything goes wrong.

1379
01:54:27,918 --> 01:54:34,948
They document common issues, define who should be contacted, and create run books that explain the first checks to perform.

1380
01:54:35,566 --> 01:54:43,748
Good monitoring and clear instructions make incidents easier to handle, especially when the person responding didn't build the part of the system that's failing.

1381
01:54:44,366 --> 01:54:48,164
Not every company has on call, and not every role includes it.

1382
01:54:48,526 --> 01:54:52,644
It depends heavily on what you're building and how critical it is that it stays running.

1383
01:54:53,230 --> 01:55:02,052
But if your team does have it, know that it's a rotation, it's usually gradual, and asking for help in the middle of an alert is exactly what it's there for.

1384
01:55:02,638 --> 01:55:06,276
And that willingness to ask for help isn't just an on-call skill.

1385
01:55:06,670 --> 01:55:10,272
It's one of the most important habits you can build in your first few months.

1386
01:55:10,592 --> 01:55:11,792
On call or not.

1387
01:55:12,110 --> 01:55:15,332
Here's a skill that can save a beginner weeks of frustration.

1388
01:55:15,822 --> 01:55:17,796
Knowing when to ask for help.

1389
01:55:18,350 --> 01:55:22,112
When you're new, it's easy to think, I should figure this out myself.

1390
01:55:22,414 --> 01:55:25,348
So you spend three hours trying random things.

1391
01:55:25,902 --> 01:55:29,508
That's usually worse than spending 10 minutes asking a good question.

1392
01:55:30,030 --> 01:55:35,940
But before you ask, there's a quick order of operations that experienced developers follow without thinking about it.

1393
01:55:36,398 --> 01:55:39,232
First, read the actual error message.

1394
01:55:39,534 --> 01:55:41,476
All of it, not just the first line.

1395
01:55:41,998 --> 01:55:48,836
Second, check the project's own documentation or README, because a lot of bugs are setup steps you skipped.

1396
01:55:49,358 --> 01:55:55,812
Third, search the exact error text, in quotes, because someone has almost certainly hit it before.

1397
01:55:56,270 --> 01:56:02,404
Fourth, ask an AI assistant to explain the error and treat the answer as a lead, not a verdict.

1398
01:56:02,830 --> 01:56:07,268
And then, if you're still stuck after 15 or 20 minutes, ask a person.

1399
01:56:07,822 --> 01:56:12,164
One thing worth remembering is that the 15 or 20 minutes isn't a strict timer.

1400
01:56:12,686 --> 01:56:21,572
Some problems genuinely need longer investigation, while others are worth escalating sooner, especially if they affect production or block the rest of the team.

1401
01:56:22,030 --> 01:56:28,260
The goal isn't to solve everything alone, it's to make a reasonable effort before getting someone else involved.

1402
01:56:28,654 --> 01:56:32,548
That sequence matters because of what it gives you when you finally do ask.

1403
01:56:32,974 --> 01:56:35,204
A list of what you already tried.

1404
01:56:35,630 --> 01:56:37,892
The important part is how you ask.

1405
01:56:38,318 --> 01:56:41,188
Instead of just saying, it's not working, can you help?

1406
01:56:41,614 --> 01:56:45,028
Try something like, I'm working on the customer API.

1407
01:56:45,390 --> 01:56:49,092
The request reaches the service, but the database call is timing out.

1408
01:56:49,518 --> 01:56:52,964
I check the logs and the timeout starts right after the query runs.

1409
01:56:53,582 --> 01:56:55,588
I haven't changed the database configuration.

1410
01:56:56,046 --> 01:56:58,692
Do you know whether this environment has had this issue before?

1411
01:56:59,150 --> 01:57:02,372
Notice that this question gives your teammates somewhere to start.

1412
01:57:02,830 --> 01:57:09,380
They can look at the database connection, check whether the environment has a known issue, or suggest the next investigation step.

1413
01:57:10,030 --> 01:57:12,868
You're not asking them to solve a mystery with no clues.

1414
01:57:13,326 --> 01:57:14,756
That's a much better question.

1415
01:57:15,182 --> 01:57:20,772
You've explained what you're doing, what you expected, what actually happened, and what you've already checked.

1416
01:57:21,390 --> 01:57:25,732
And when someone helps you solve the problem, take a moment to understand the fix.

1417
01:57:26,318 --> 01:57:32,452
Write down what caused the issue, why the solution worked, and how you might recognize the same problem next time.

1418
01:57:32,942 --> 01:57:37,028
Otherwise, you risk getting stuck on the exact same thing a few weeks later.

1419
01:57:37,454 --> 01:57:41,392
Experienced developers generally don't expect beginners to know everything.

1420
01:57:41,582 --> 01:57:44,512
They do expect you to investigate before asking.

1421
01:57:44,846 --> 01:57:48,228
Your first few weeks at a new company can feel overwhelming.

1422
01:57:48,686 --> 01:57:54,116
You'll hear a hundred terms you've never seen, and you'll understand maybe half of most conversations.

1423
01:57:54,574 --> 01:57:55,364
That's normal.

1424
01:57:55,854 --> 01:58:01,700
So instead of trying to understand everything, here's what experienced developers actually do in week one.

1425
01:58:02,318 --> 01:58:05,156
First, get the application running on your machine.

1426
01:58:05,486 --> 01:58:07,396
Not perfectly, just running.

1427
01:58:07,886 --> 01:58:16,112
Clone the repository, follow the README, and when the setup steps are out of date, and they usually are, write down every step that didn't work.

1428
01:58:16,526 --> 01:58:19,460
Fixing that README is often a great first pull request.

1429
01:58:20,046 --> 01:58:30,980
Second, find the five places where everything happens where the tickets live, where the code lives, where the pipeline runs, where the application is deployed, and where the logs are.

1430
01:58:31,374 --> 01:58:35,396
If you know those five, you can follow almost any conversation on the team.

1431
01:58:36,078 --> 01:58:39,300
Third, watch one change go all the way through.

1432
01:58:39,854 --> 01:58:42,276
Pick any teammate's pull request and follow it.

1433
01:58:42,638 --> 01:58:46,372
The review comments, the merge, the pipeline, the deployment.

1434
01:58:46,830 --> 01:58:52,132
You'll learn more about your team's real workflow from one PR than from any onboarding document.

1435
01:58:52,654 --> 01:58:55,076
Fourth, take a small ticket early.

1436
01:58:55,438 --> 01:58:58,564
A typo fix, a log message, a tiny bug.

1437
01:58:59,022 --> 01:59:00,228
The point isn't the ticket.

1438
01:59:00,622 --> 01:59:05,412
The point is going through the whole workflow once, end to end, while the stakes are low.

1439
01:59:05,934 --> 01:59:08,420
And fifth, keep a personal glossary.

1440
01:59:08,942 --> 01:59:15,120
Every internal system name, every acronym, every, oh, that's the service that handles payments moment.

1441
01:59:15,470 --> 01:59:16,260
Write it down.

1442
01:59:16,718 --> 01:59:20,260
In a month, that document is the map of the system in your head.

1443
01:59:20,750 --> 01:59:23,044
And that's how confidence actually develops.

1444
01:59:23,438 --> 01:59:29,028
Not by knowing everything, by gradually needing less context to understand what's going on around you.

1445
01:59:29,582 --> 01:59:35,172
That confidence gets tested for real the first time something breaks badly enough that the whole team notices.

1446
01:59:35,520 --> 01:59:41,600
Somewhere, a customer is staring at an error page right now, and your team just found out.

1447
01:59:42,094 --> 01:59:46,756
What happens in the next 10 minutes matters more than what happens in the next 10 hours.

1448
01:59:47,470 --> 01:59:53,252
Most teams grade how serious a problem is using a severity level, often shortened to SEV.

1449
01:59:53,710 --> 01:59:58,884
SEV1 is the worst, a full outage or data loss affecting many customers.

1450
01:59:59,374 --> 02:00:05,380
SEV3 or SEV4 is a minor issue affecting a few people with an easy workaround.

1451
02:00:05,870 --> 02:00:08,868
The severity decides how loudly to raise the alarm.

1452
02:00:09,230 --> 02:00:15,172
It decides whether to wake people up overnight, and it decides whether leadership needs to know immediately.

1453
02:00:15,694 --> 02:00:20,868
Getting the severity roughly right, quickly, matters more than getting it exactly right.

1454
02:00:21,326 --> 02:00:26,308
A SEV1 called in as a SEV3 can sit ignored while customers suffer.

1455
02:00:26,766 --> 02:00:29,892
The reverse just means someone double checks and downgrades it.

1456
02:00:30,446 --> 02:00:35,972
During an active incident, the instinct of a new developer is often to immediately start trying fixes.

1457
02:00:36,590 --> 02:00:40,228
A more useful first move is almost always to communicate.

1458
02:00:40,750 --> 02:00:48,484
Say what you know, say what you don't know yet, say what you're checking next, often in a dedicated channel everyone affected can watch.

1459
02:00:48,974 --> 02:00:51,200
This isn't bureaucracy for its own sake.

1460
02:00:51,438 --> 02:01:02,532
An incident with several people quietly trying different fixes in isolation is slower and more dangerous than one person stating clearly, I'm about to restart this service, before doing it.

1461
02:01:03,182 --> 02:01:12,612
Once the immediate fire is out, most teams hold a post-mortem, a written account of what happened, why, and what will change so it's less likely to happen again.

1462
02:01:13,166 --> 02:01:15,780
The standard the better ones hold to is blameless.

1463
02:01:16,270 --> 02:01:22,404
The document describes the sequence of events and the gaps in the system that allowed it, not which person to blame.

1464
02:01:23,022 --> 02:01:24,560
This isn't about being nice.

1465
02:01:24,878 --> 02:01:36,164
A team where admitting, I pushed the change that caused this leads to blame is a team where people start hiding problems instead of reporting them early, which makes the next incident worse, not better.

1466
02:01:36,718 --> 02:01:39,940
A good postmortem usually separates two different questions.

1467
02:01:40,590 --> 02:01:44,420
The first is what happened, in plain, specific detail.

1468
02:01:44,814 --> 02:01:48,420
The sequence of events, timestamps, the exact symptoms.

1469
02:01:49,006 --> 02:01:51,040
The second is what will change.

1470
02:01:51,406 --> 02:01:53,156
Is there a test that should have caught this?

1471
02:01:53,550 --> 02:01:55,588
A check missing from the deploy pipeline?

1472
02:01:55,918 --> 02:01:58,308
An alert that should have fired sooner but didn't?

1473
02:01:58,830 --> 02:02:01,200
The second part is where the real value is.

1474
02:02:01,614 --> 02:02:07,840
A postmortem that just describes the outage without naming the concrete follow-up tends to repeat itself eventually.

1475
02:02:08,238 --> 02:02:13,060
If you're newer on the team, you'll often be reading more postmortems than writing them at first.

1476
02:02:13,486 --> 02:02:14,720
Read them anyway.

1477
02:02:15,150 --> 02:02:21,988
They're one of the fastest ways to learn how a system actually behaves under stress instead of how it's supposed to behave on a good day.

1478
02:02:22,478 --> 02:02:30,640
A good postmortem is also, quietly, a piece of writing, and the habits that make it useful apply to a lot of what you'll write day to day.

1479
02:02:30,926 --> 02:02:39,812
You finish a feature, and three weeks later, someone asks you the exact question your own commit message could have answered, if only you'd written one worth reading.

1480
02:02:40,494 --> 02:02:43,652
Most documentation problems aren't about writing too little.

1481
02:02:44,110 --> 02:02:46,692
They're about writing in a way nobody comes back to.

1482
02:02:47,182 --> 02:02:51,332
A giant README nobody updates after the first month helps nobody.

1483
02:02:51,854 --> 02:02:56,356
A short, specific note placed where someone will actually look for it helps a lot.

1484
02:02:56,910 --> 02:03:00,932
Start with the README every project seems to have and few people maintain.

1485
02:03:01,422 --> 02:03:04,272
At minimum, it should answer three questions fast.

1486
02:03:04,814 --> 02:03:05,572
What is this?

1487
02:03:05,966 --> 02:03:06,852
How do I run it?

1488
02:03:07,214 --> 02:03:08,868
Where do I go if something's broken?

1489
02:03:09,390 --> 02:03:16,836
If a new teammate still has to ask you those three questions after reading it, the README isn't doing its job yet, no matter how long it is.

1490
02:03:17,454 --> 02:03:24,164
For a bigger decision, some teams keep a short document often called an ADR, an architectured decision record.

1491
02:03:24,622 --> 02:03:29,988
It covers what problem you were solving, what options you considered, and why you picked the one you did.

1492
02:03:30,510 --> 02:03:32,324
The value isn't really for today.

1493
02:03:32,590 --> 02:03:40,932
It's for the person six months from now, possibly you, wondering why on earth it was done this way, who would otherwise have to guess or ask around.

1494
02:03:41,646 --> 02:03:48,196
Async communication, a message in a channel instead of a conversation in person, has its own habit worth building.

1495
02:03:48,782 --> 02:03:52,836
Say the conclusion first, then the reasoning, not the other way around.

1496
02:03:53,422 --> 02:03:56,996
Going with option B, here's why, reads in two seconds.

1497
02:03:57,582 --> 02:04:08,228
A message that opens with several paragraphs of context before finally landing on the point often gets skimmed, misread, or answered incorrectly by someone who stopped reading halfway through.

1498
02:04:08,654 --> 02:04:15,044
If someone needs to act on what you wrote, make the action obvious without requiring them to read the whole thing first.

1499
02:04:15,534 --> 02:04:19,428
The same applies to status updates, especially during something ongoing.

1500
02:04:19,982 --> 02:04:29,572
Still looking into it, said once, with silence after, reads very differently from the same words repeated every so often along with what's been ruled out so far.

1501
02:04:30,030 --> 02:04:36,644
The second version costs you almost nothing extra to write and tells everyone watching that progress is actually happening.

1502
02:04:37,262 --> 02:04:40,164
None of this requires good writing in some formal sense.

1503
02:04:40,718 --> 02:04:44,260
It requires one question before you hit send or save.

1504
02:04:44,686 --> 02:04:49,124
If someone read only the first sentence of this, would they still know what to do next?

1505
02:04:49,710 --> 02:04:53,156
If the answer is no, that sentence needs to move to the top.

1506
02:04:53,518 --> 02:05:07,012
Stand-ups are the meeting most people picture when they hear scrum, but a team running sprints usually has two other recurring meetings that shape the work just as much, and new developers often walk into both without knowing what to expect.

1507
02:05:07,502 --> 02:05:14,948
Sprint planning happens at the start of each sprint, a fixed chunk of time, often one or two weeks, that the team is committing to.

1508
02:05:15,502 --> 02:05:21,796
The team looks at the backlog, the pile of tickets waiting to be worked on, and decides what fits into this sprint.

1509
02:05:22,286 --> 02:05:30,116
For each ticket, someone explains what it involves and the team estimates how much effort it takes, often in story points rather than hours.

1510
02:05:30,734 --> 02:05:39,552
This is where you'll hear a ticket argued over for 10 minutes, not because the task is hard, but because nobody's sure yet what done actually means for it.

1511
02:05:39,982 --> 02:05:42,372
Planning goes better when tickets are specific.

1512
02:05:42,894 --> 02:05:45,700
Fix the login page invites that confusion.

1513
02:05:46,254 --> 02:05:50,628
Fix the login page so it shows an error message when the password is wrong doesn't.

1514
02:05:51,182 --> 02:05:56,772
A retro, short for retrospective, happens at the end of the sprint, after the work is done.

1515
02:05:57,358 --> 02:06:01,572
It isn't about reviewing the product, it's about reviewing how the team worked.

1516
02:06:02,062 --> 02:06:04,260
A common format asks three questions.

1517
02:06:04,910 --> 02:06:09,092
What went well, what didn't, what should we change going forward?

1518
02:06:09,582 --> 02:06:16,420
A flaky test that wasted a day, a ticket that turned out way bigger than estimated, a deployment that went smoothly for once.

1519
02:06:16,878 --> 02:06:18,692
All of that is retro material.

1520
02:06:19,246 --> 02:06:30,340
The part that makes retros actually useful is psychological safety, the sense that you can say, I underestimated this, or that process didn't work, without it being held against you.

1521
02:06:30,862 --> 02:06:36,100
A retro where nobody says anything honest just produces the same problems again next sprint.

1522
02:06:36,590 --> 02:06:44,740
So, if you're new to a team and planning feels like arguing over tickets, and retro feels like complaining with structure, that's normal.

1523
02:06:45,294 --> 02:06:50,752
Both are the team's way of getting a little better at predicting and working together, one sprint at a time.

1524
02:06:51,022 --> 02:06:55,236
A service has been quietly failing for 20 minutes before anyone notices.

1525
02:06:55,630 --> 02:07:02,692
Not because it crashed, but because one in every 20 requests was returning an error, and nobody was watching that number.

1526
02:07:03,214 --> 02:07:04,672
The service looked up.

1527
02:07:05,166 --> 02:07:06,756
It just wasn't fully working.

1528
02:07:07,406 --> 02:07:10,532
This is the gap that monitoring exists to close.

1529
02:07:11,118 --> 02:07:22,276
Shipping code and watching it work are two different skills, and a team that's good at the first but blind to the second finds out about problems from angry users instead of from their own tools.

1530
02:07:23,022 --> 02:07:27,812
At its simplest, monitoring means collecting numbers about a running system over time.

1531
02:07:28,366 --> 02:07:38,660
The most common ones are error rate, how often requests fail, latency, how long requests take to come back, and traffic, how many requests are coming in at all.

1532
02:07:39,182 --> 02:07:46,052
A dashboard turns those numbers into a graph you can glance at, instead of a wall of log lines you'd have to read one by one.

1533
02:07:46,606 --> 02:07:57,220
An alert is a rule that watches one of those graphs and notifies someone when it crosses a line that matters, error rate above a certain percentage, or latency above a certain number of seconds.

1534
02:07:57,838 --> 02:08:00,388
Good alerts are specific enough to act on.

1535
02:08:00,814 --> 02:08:09,860
A vague alert that fires constantly for things nobody needs to act on trains people to ignore it, which means the one time it matters, it gets ignored too.

1536
02:08:10,446 --> 02:08:14,372
Monitoring is different from the incident response you may have heard about elsewhere.

1537
02:08:14,862 --> 02:08:19,652
Incident response is what happens after something is clearly broken and everyone knows it.

1538
02:08:20,142 --> 02:08:27,396
Monitoring is what catches the problem quietly, often before users notice, sometimes before it becomes an incident at all.

1539
02:08:27,950 --> 02:08:31,172
You don't need to build any of this yourself as a junior developer.

1540
02:08:31,630 --> 02:08:40,688
But you will be expected to glance at a dashboard before and after you deploy something, and to know that a quiet dashboard is a good sign, not a boring one.

1541
02:08:40,942 --> 02:08:42,564
Let's recap module 9.

1542
02:08:43,086 --> 02:08:47,588
A normal day is a mix of coding, reading, meetings, and questions.

1543
02:08:48,206 --> 02:08:55,248
You learned what those meetings are for, and how teams in different time zones keep work moving by writing clearly the first time.

1544
02:08:55,534 --> 02:08:59,684
And you saw how much of the job is looking after software that already exists.

1545
02:09:00,398 --> 02:09:02,048
Then came the bad days.

1546
02:09:02,446 --> 02:09:08,708
When production breaks, you find out the impact first, and you tell people what you're doing before you try a fix.

1547
02:09:09,326 --> 02:09:16,324
For a new developer, on call usually means checking the alert and bringing in the right person if it's too big to handle safely.

1548
02:09:16,846 --> 02:09:23,972
A blameless post-mortem turns a failure into real changes, and good monitoring can catch trouble before customers do.

1549
02:09:24,494 --> 02:09:28,452
For your own start, you now have an order to follow before asking for help.

1550
02:09:28,878 --> 02:09:35,168
You also have five things to do in your first weeks, and you can write notes and updates that put the point first.

1551
02:09:35,758 --> 02:09:37,328
That covers the work itself.

1552
02:09:37,742 --> 02:09:40,356
Module 10 is about your place in all of this.

1553
02:09:40,750 --> 02:09:43,460
Welcome to module 10, the last one.

1554
02:09:43,950 --> 02:09:46,208
It's about everything around the work.

1555
02:09:46,510 --> 02:09:51,524
That means the company you join, the people you work with, and where all of it can take you.

1556
02:09:52,078 --> 02:09:53,988
We'll start before you're even hired.

1557
02:09:54,414 --> 02:10:00,452
You'll see the kinds of companies out there, the roles on a team, and what a company really expects from a junior.

1558
02:10:01,006 --> 02:10:04,036
From there, we get to the harder side of working with people.

1559
02:10:04,590 --> 02:10:06,288
Someone will reject your work.

1560
02:10:06,638 --> 02:10:09,648
Someone else will want it sooner than you can safely ship it.

1561
02:10:09,934 --> 02:10:13,700
And some days you'll get one more task when you're already too busy.

1562
02:10:14,190 --> 02:10:15,812
You'll know what to say when that happens.

1563
02:10:16,558 --> 02:10:19,364
After that, the focus moves to your own growth.

1564
02:10:19,822 --> 02:10:24,452
You'll see how to make sure your work gets noticed and how to use your time with your manager well.

1565
02:10:24,910 --> 02:10:27,328
You'll learn to talk with people who don't write code.

1566
02:10:27,598 --> 02:10:31,588
And you'll see where you fit in the company and how to find someone who can guide you.

1567
02:10:32,174 --> 02:10:36,100
By the end of this module, you'll know how to handle the people side of the job.

1568
02:10:36,366 --> 02:10:38,820
You'll also know how to look after your own growth.

1569
02:10:39,406 --> 02:10:42,768
Let's begin with the choice you'll run into as soon as you start applying.

1570
02:10:43,086 --> 02:10:49,508
One thing you'll hear a lot when you start looking for IT jobs is product company versus service company.

1571
02:10:50,094 --> 02:10:59,248
A product company builds and operates its own software product, a banking platform, an e-commerce app, a developer tool, a SaaS product.

1572
02:10:59,630 --> 02:11:04,292
The team is usually focused on improving and maintaining that one product over time.

1573
02:11:04,942 --> 02:11:10,404
For example, imagine a company that builds a project management tool used by thousands of businesses.

1574
02:11:10,926 --> 02:11:18,500
Its developers might spend months improving the same product, adding features, fixing bugs, and responding to customer feedback.

1575
02:11:18,990 --> 02:11:24,132
Over time, they get to understand the product and its architecture in considerable depth.

1576
02:11:24,942 --> 02:11:26,980
A service company works differently.

1577
02:11:27,374 --> 02:11:35,620
It provides technology services to clients, so you might work on a banking project for one client and a retail project for another right after.

1578
02:11:36,174 --> 02:11:40,804
That can mean jumping between different technologies, industries, and project environments.

1579
02:11:41,614 --> 02:11:45,060
Imagine you're working for a service company and your client is a bank.

1580
02:11:45,518 --> 02:11:51,140
You might help build an API, migrate data to the cloud, or maintain an existing application.

1581
02:11:51,726 --> 02:11:59,588
If the project ends and you're assigned to a retail client, you could find yourself working with a completely different technology stack and business domain.

1582
02:12:00,206 --> 02:12:04,772
That variety can be a valuable learning experience, especially early in your career.

1583
02:12:05,230 --> 02:12:09,572
But don't oversimplify this into product is good, service is bad.

1584
02:12:09,966 --> 02:12:11,684
That's not how the real world works.

1585
02:12:12,142 --> 02:12:17,924
A great team in a service company can give you more practical experience than a poorly managed product team.

1586
02:12:18,478 --> 02:12:20,452
There's also some overlap between the two.

1587
02:12:21,006 --> 02:12:29,476
Product companies hire consultants and external teams, while service companies sometimes build and maintain systems for the same client for many years.

1588
02:12:30,030 --> 02:12:33,892
The actual work can matter more than the category the company falls into.

1589
02:12:34,766 --> 02:12:49,700
What actually matters is what you'll be working on day to day, what technology you'll use, whether you'll touch real production systems, whether you'll actually get code reviews, whether there are experienced people around you, and whether you'll own work that matters.

1590
02:12:50,190 --> 02:12:53,412
Those questions tell you a lot more than the label on the company.

1591
02:12:54,062 --> 02:12:56,612
And your first job doesn't lock you into one path.

1592
02:12:57,070 --> 02:13:01,424
You can start in one type of company, build real experience, and move later.

1593
02:13:01,646 --> 02:13:07,428
Another thing beginners often struggle with is understanding all the different roles inside an IT team.

1594
02:13:07,950 --> 02:13:22,944
You'll hear titles like software developer, front-end developer, backend developer, data engineer, data analyst, DevOps engineer, cloud engineer, QA engineer, security engineer, product manager, and plenty more.

1595
02:13:23,374 --> 02:13:28,548
These roles overlap at some companies, but they generally focus on different parts of the system.

1596
02:13:29,134 --> 02:13:33,316
A front-end developer focuses heavily on what users actually interact with.

1597
02:13:33,934 --> 02:13:39,300
A backend developer works more with APIs, business logic, databases, and services.

1598
02:13:40,014 --> 02:13:45,412
A data engineer builds the systems that collect, transform, move, and prepare data.

1599
02:13:46,030 --> 02:13:53,124
A DevOps or platform engineer usually focuses on infrastructure, deployment, automation, and reliability.

1600
02:13:53,710 --> 02:13:59,812
And a QA engineer focuses on validating software and catching problems before users ever see them.

1601
02:14:00,622 --> 02:14:03,524
Let's take an online shopping application as an example.

1602
02:14:04,046 --> 02:14:08,324
The front end developer builds the product pages and shopping cart that customers see.

1603
02:14:08,878 --> 02:14:13,188
The back end developer handles things like orders, payments, and customer accounts.

1604
02:14:13,870 --> 02:14:25,636
The data engineer makes sure sales and customer data flows into systems where it can be analyzed, while the QA engineer tests whether the checkout process works correctly, including situations where a payment fails.

1605
02:14:26,478 --> 02:14:36,804
Meanwhile, the cloud or platform engineer helps keep the application running in the right environment, manages deployment pipelines, and makes sure the infrastructure can handle the workload.

1606
02:14:37,390 --> 02:14:41,956
The security engineer looks for vulnerabilities and helps protect customer information.

1607
02:14:42,638 --> 02:14:47,300
Each role contributes to making the same application work reliably and safely.

1608
02:14:48,110 --> 02:14:51,108
Then there are roles outside direct development entirely.

1609
02:14:51,566 --> 02:15:00,868
Product managers who think about what should be built and why, designers who shape the user experience, and project or delivery people who help coordinate the work.

1610
02:15:01,582 --> 02:15:07,652
For example, before a team builds a new checkout feature, the product manager might define the customer problem.

1611
02:15:08,078 --> 02:15:13,348
The designer works out how the experience should look, and the developers figure out how to implement it.

1612
02:15:13,902 --> 02:15:20,868
The QA engineer validates the finished feature, while other team members help make sure it can be deployed and operated reliably.

1613
02:15:21,518 --> 02:15:24,740
These people are solving different parts of the same problem.

1614
02:15:25,486 --> 02:15:29,892
The important thing is that real software is rarely built by one person.

1615
02:15:30,414 --> 02:15:35,236
A production application is usually the result of a lot of different roles working together.

1616
02:15:35,630 --> 02:15:41,092
So when you join a company, don't only learn who writes code, learn who does what.

1617
02:15:41,454 --> 02:15:44,624
That makes the entire system much easier to understand.

1618
02:15:44,942 --> 02:15:50,340
A common mistake beginners make is thinking companies expect a junior developer to know everything.

1619
02:15:50,798 --> 02:15:52,324
Usually, they don't.

1620
02:15:52,878 --> 02:15:58,944
Nobody expects you to know every framework, every cloud service, or every internal tool on your first day.

1621
02:15:59,694 --> 02:16:03,204
What companies actually need from a junior is much more basic.

1622
02:16:03,598 --> 02:16:08,964
Can you understand a requirement, read existing code, and solve a reasonably scoped problem?

1623
02:16:09,422 --> 02:16:17,540
Can you test your own work, speak up clearly when you're blocked, pick up something you've never seen before, and take feedback without getting defensive about it?

1624
02:16:18,158 --> 02:16:19,972
Think about it from the team's perspective.

1625
02:16:20,622 --> 02:16:25,988
A senior developer can teach you how their application works, or help you understand a new technology.

1626
02:16:26,446 --> 02:16:34,532
But they need to be able to trust that you'll ask when something is unclear, check your work before handing it over, and tell them honestly when something goes wrong.

1627
02:16:35,054 --> 02:16:37,508
Those things matter a lot more than they sound.

1628
02:16:37,966 --> 02:16:40,708
You might not know how the company's deployment system works.

1629
02:16:41,070 --> 02:16:42,660
That's okay, you can learn it.

1630
02:16:43,150 --> 02:16:52,292
But if you don't ask questions, don't test your changes, don't communicate problems, and can't take feedback, all the technical knowledge in the world won't fix that.

1631
02:16:52,782 --> 02:16:56,452
And remember, nobody expects you to learn everything overnight.

1632
02:16:56,910 --> 02:17:02,948
You might make mistakes, misunderstand a requirement, or need help with something that seems obvious to everyone else.

1633
02:17:03,534 --> 02:17:09,764
What matters is whether you learn from those experiences and gradually become more confident working independently.

1634
02:17:10,254 --> 02:17:14,628
So when preparing for your first job, don't focus only on collecting technologies.

1635
02:17:15,214 --> 02:17:18,820
Build the ability to learn and work inside an existing system.

1636
02:17:19,246 --> 02:17:22,532
That's much closer to what your first year will actually require.

1637
02:17:22,990 --> 02:17:27,524
And part of working inside a system is working with people who don't always agree with you.

1638
02:17:28,078 --> 02:17:29,008
Let's talk about that.

1639
02:17:29,262 --> 02:17:31,408
Here's a moment nobody warns you about.

1640
02:17:31,758 --> 02:17:41,028
You finish your ticket, you're proud of it, and the QA engineer moves it straight back to in progress with a note, fails when the email has uppercase letters.

1641
02:17:41,678 --> 02:17:44,132
Your first reaction is going to be a little sting.

1642
02:17:44,494 --> 02:17:45,220
That's human.

1643
02:17:45,870 --> 02:17:47,328
Here's the thing to understand.

1644
02:17:47,758 --> 02:17:51,620
QA rejecting your ticket is not QA rejecting you.

1645
02:17:52,014 --> 02:17:54,724
It's the system working exactly as designed.

1646
02:17:55,150 --> 02:17:57,252
That bug was going to be found by somebody.

1647
02:17:57,710 --> 02:18:00,740
Better a teammate on Tuesday than a customer on Saturday.

1648
02:18:01,422 --> 02:18:02,212
So what do you do?

1649
02:18:02,670 --> 02:18:04,048
You say, good catch.

1650
02:18:04,270 --> 02:18:09,828
You reproduce it, you fix it, and you add the uppercase case to your tests so it never comes back.

1651
02:18:10,222 --> 02:18:10,756
That's it.

1652
02:18:11,342 --> 02:18:15,300
The developers who get respected fast are not the ones who never get bugs.

1653
02:18:15,662 --> 02:18:17,892
They're the ones who handle them without drama.

1654
02:18:18,702 --> 02:18:19,844
Now the other direction.

1655
02:18:20,334 --> 02:18:24,288
A product manager pings you, can we just ship the registration change today?

1656
02:18:24,448 --> 02:18:26,372
The marketing campaign starts tomorrow.

1657
02:18:26,990 --> 02:18:30,084
Your code review isn't done, the staging tests haven't run.

1658
02:18:30,414 --> 02:18:31,008
You're new.

1659
02:18:31,246 --> 02:18:35,364
You want to be helpful, and just ship it sounds like being helpful.

1660
02:18:35,822 --> 02:18:36,452
It isn't.

1661
02:18:36,942 --> 02:18:39,972
Here's a better answer, and you can use it word for word.

1662
02:18:40,494 --> 02:18:43,044
I can't skip the review and staging run safely.

1663
02:18:43,342 --> 02:18:47,268
What I can do is get it into review right now and flag it as urgent.

1664
02:18:47,566 --> 02:18:49,796
If it passes, it can go out this afternoon.

1665
02:18:50,158 --> 02:18:55,684
If we skip those steps and it breaks registration during the campaign, that's a much worse day for everyone.

1666
02:18:56,526 --> 02:18:57,604
Notice what that does.

1667
02:18:58,030 --> 02:19:04,036
It doesn't say no, it says what's possible, what the risk is, and puts the decision where it belongs.

1668
02:19:04,750 --> 02:19:07,620
Which brings us to the most important rule for a junior.

1669
02:19:08,014 --> 02:19:10,756
Decisions like that don't happen in a private chat.

1670
02:19:11,086 --> 02:19:15,044
They happen on the ticket or in the team channel where your lead can see them.

1671
02:19:15,598 --> 02:19:20,644
If the product manager still wants to skip steps, that's a call for your lead to make, not you.

1672
02:19:21,198 --> 02:19:25,988
Escalating isn't tattling, it's how the team protects both the product and you.

1673
02:19:26,830 --> 02:19:27,568
And one more.

1674
02:19:28,078 --> 02:19:33,508
Sometimes a senior developer will push back on your approach in code review and you'll be sure you're right.

1675
02:19:33,966 --> 02:19:34,848
Ask why.

1676
02:19:35,150 --> 02:19:35,844
Genuinely.

1677
02:19:36,334 --> 02:19:38,820
Most of the time there's a reason you haven't seen yet.

1678
02:19:39,054 --> 02:19:41,540
An old incident, a system you didn't know about.

1679
02:19:41,966 --> 02:19:47,328
Occasionally you'll actually be right, and saying, here's what I was thinking, does that change anything?

1680
02:19:47,488 --> 02:19:48,516
is how you find out.

1681
02:19:49,198 --> 02:19:52,740
Disagreement is a normal part of building software with other people.

1682
02:19:53,134 --> 02:19:58,608
Handle it calmly, keep it visible, and it turns into trust faster than anything else you can do.

1683
02:19:58,894 --> 02:20:01,348
Here's something nobody puts in a job posting.

1684
02:20:01,902 --> 02:20:10,020
Even on a healthy team, people have different incentives, different priorities, and sometimes different opinions about who should get credit for what.

1685
02:20:10,606 --> 02:20:16,548
That's not necessarily a toxic workplace, that's just what happens whenever more than a few people work together.

1686
02:20:17,102 --> 02:20:23,716
The question isn't how to avoid this entirely, it's how to move through it without damaging your own reputation in the process.

1687
02:20:24,494 --> 02:20:30,084
The most reliable protection you have is keeping decisions and disagreements visible instead of private.

1688
02:20:30,670 --> 02:20:37,028
If a disagreement with a teammate happens in a side conversation, it can quietly turn into two different stories later.

1689
02:20:37,582 --> 02:20:44,420
If it happens on the ticket, in the pull request, or in the team channel, everyone can see what was actually said and agreed to.

1690
02:20:45,006 --> 02:20:49,796
This isn't about distrust, it's just a habit that protects everyone, including you.

1691
02:20:50,414 --> 02:20:56,772
For example, suppose you suggest one approach to solving a problem, but another developer recommends something different.

1692
02:20:57,326 --> 02:21:04,004
Instead of turning it into a personal argument, explain your reasoning, listen to theirs, and document the final decision.

1693
02:21:04,622 --> 02:21:08,452
If the team chooses their approach, support that decision and move forward.

1694
02:21:08,910 --> 02:21:12,004
You don't have to win the argument to contribute something valuable.

1695
02:21:12,750 --> 02:21:16,164
The second habit is simple but easy to forget under pressure.

1696
02:21:16,718 --> 02:21:19,716
Don't vent about a coworker to other coworkers.

1697
02:21:20,302 --> 02:21:24,516
It feels satisfying in the moment, and it almost always comes back around.

1698
02:21:25,070 --> 02:21:34,340
If someone's work or behavior is genuinely a problem, that's a conversation for your manager, calmly and factually, not a conversation for the group chat.

1699
02:21:34,894 --> 02:21:41,956
And when you raise a concern, focus on specific behavior and its impact rather than making assumptions about someone's intentions.

1700
02:21:42,670 --> 02:21:47,556
Saying that a missed handover delayed your work gives your manager something concrete to address.

1701
02:21:48,142 --> 02:21:53,956
Saying that someone is lazy or doesn't care turns the conversation into a judgment about their character.

1702
02:21:54,670 --> 02:22:03,300
And the third habit is the one that actually builds a good reputation over time, not through impressing people, but through being consistently easy to work with.

1703
02:22:03,950 --> 02:22:12,420
Following through on what you say you'll do, giving credit to people who helped you, helping a teammate without immediately expecting something back.

1704
02:22:12,974 --> 02:22:21,572
None of this is about being naive, it's that a reputation for being straightforward and reliable tends to open more doors than any amount of self-promotion.

1705
02:22:22,094 --> 02:22:27,716
That doesn't mean you should quietly accept everything or take responsibility for problems that aren't yours.

1706
02:22:28,206 --> 02:22:33,060
You can disagree respectfully, set boundaries, and speak up when something is unfair.

1707
02:22:33,582 --> 02:22:38,916
Being professional means handling those situations constructively, not pretending they don't exist.

1708
02:22:39,502 --> 02:22:43,076
You don't need to win every disagreement or be liked by everyone.

1709
02:22:43,534 --> 02:22:48,036
You need to be someone people trust to act the same way whether or not they're in the room.

1710
02:22:48,526 --> 02:22:52,900
Staying professional through moments like this is one part of protecting your reputation.

1711
02:22:53,390 --> 02:22:57,540
The other part is protecting your time, which is its own skill entirely.

1712
02:22:57,744 --> 02:23:06,468
At some point, someone is going to hand you a new task while you're already juggling two others, and every instinct you have will want to say yes anyway.

1713
02:23:06,990 --> 02:23:10,884
As a beginner, saying yes to everything can feel like the safe choice.

1714
02:23:11,406 --> 02:23:15,204
Often, it's actually the choice that causes the most damage later.

1715
02:23:15,662 --> 02:23:16,324
Here's why.

1716
02:23:16,814 --> 02:23:21,572
If you quietly take on more than you can realistically finish, one of two things happens.

1717
02:23:22,158 --> 02:23:28,356
Something slips through with less attention than it deserved, or you end up working long hours just to keep up.

1718
02:23:28,910 --> 02:23:36,580
Neither one is sustainable, and neither one is actually what a good manager wants from you, even if it feels like the helpful answer in the moment.

1719
02:23:37,294 --> 02:23:39,844
And there's another problem with constantly saying yes.

1720
02:23:40,334 --> 02:23:44,836
Your manager might assume your workload is manageable because you're never raising any concerns.

1721
02:23:45,550 --> 02:23:52,356
By the time a deadline slips or the quality of your work starts to suffer, the problem has already become much harder to fix.

1722
02:23:53,006 --> 02:23:55,012
The skill isn't refusing work.

1723
02:23:55,342 --> 02:23:58,276
It's making the trade-off visible instead of invisible.

1724
02:23:58,862 --> 02:24:03,780
Something like, I'm currently finishing the payment ticket and reviewing a teammate's pull request.

1725
02:24:04,110 --> 02:24:07,780
I can start this new one today, but the payment ticket would slip to tomorrow.

1726
02:24:08,142 --> 02:24:09,668
Which would you like me to prioritize?

1727
02:24:10,318 --> 02:24:11,556
That's not saying no.

1728
02:24:11,886 --> 02:24:18,820
It's handing the actual decision to the person who has the full picture, instead of silently overcommitting and hoping it works out.

1729
02:24:19,374 --> 02:24:24,324
This is especially important when you're new because you might not yet know how long a task will take.

1730
02:24:24,846 --> 02:24:29,604
If you discover that something is more complicated than expected, communicate that early.

1731
02:24:30,158 --> 02:24:33,348
You don't need to have the perfect solution or a new deadline ready.

1732
02:24:33,774 --> 02:24:37,424
Just explain what you found, what remains, and where you need help.

1733
02:24:37,998 --> 02:24:41,028
The same idea applies to your time outside of work hours.

1734
02:24:41,614 --> 02:24:44,772
Occasionally, something urgent genuinely can't wait.

1735
02:24:45,230 --> 02:24:58,660
But if every evening or weekend starts to feel like an emergency, that's not a personal failing to push through quietly, it's a signal worth raising with your manager, because it usually means something in the workload or the process needs to change.

1736
02:24:59,150 --> 02:25:05,508
Of course, different teams have different expectations, and some roles do involve occasional after-hours support.

1737
02:25:05,998 --> 02:25:14,916
The important distinction is between an agreed responsibility that you can plan for and a constant pattern of unexpected work that leaves you unable to switch off.

1738
02:25:15,438 --> 02:25:17,892
Protecting your time isn't about doing less.

1739
02:25:18,414 --> 02:25:24,804
It's about making sure the person deciding what gets prioritized actually has accurate information to decide with.

1740
02:25:25,326 --> 02:25:32,516
And all of this, the workload, the pushback, the politics, feeds into a bigger question worth thinking about early.

1741
02:25:33,198 --> 02:25:35,408
Where do you actually want this career to go?

1742
02:25:35,758 --> 02:25:40,048
Your career probably won't go exactly according to the plan you have right now.

1743
02:25:40,430 --> 02:25:43,012
You might start as a developer and move into cloud.

1744
02:25:43,342 --> 02:25:46,128
You might start in testing and end up in automation.

1745
02:25:46,446 --> 02:25:51,808
You might begin with one programming language and spend most of your career using a completely different one.

1746
02:25:52,238 --> 02:25:59,588
You might discover you actually enjoy working with data more than building applications, or that you like infrastructure more than writing application code.

1747
02:26:00,110 --> 02:26:04,708
And sometimes the change happens because of a project you never expected to work on.

1748
02:26:05,230 --> 02:26:12,228
You might get assigned to a cloud migration, start learning how the infrastructure works, and discover that's the part of the job you enjoy most.

1749
02:26:12,750 --> 02:26:16,356
You don't have to figure out your entire career before you start working.

1750
02:26:16,654 --> 02:26:17,380
That's normal.

1751
02:26:17,838 --> 02:26:24,356
Technology changes, companies change, your own interests change, and your career changes right along with them.

1752
02:26:24,974 --> 02:26:29,508
The useful thing to build early isn't an attachment to one particular technology.

1753
02:26:29,838 --> 02:26:31,364
It's a strong foundation.

1754
02:26:31,888 --> 02:26:39,684
Programming, databases, networking, Git, cloud concepts, debugging, system thinking, and communication.

1755
02:26:40,302 --> 02:26:44,708
Once those fundamentals are solid, picking up a new tool becomes far easier.

1756
02:26:45,358 --> 02:26:46,404
Think about it this way.

1757
02:26:46,798 --> 02:26:53,764
If you understand databases, learning a new database platform becomes much easier because the underlying concepts are familiar.

1758
02:26:54,254 --> 02:26:59,684
If you understand networking, a new cloud provider's networking services won't feel completely foreign.

1759
02:27:00,142 --> 02:27:03,876
You're learning a different implementation of concepts you already understand.

1760
02:27:04,558 --> 02:27:06,528
A framework can fall out of fashion.

1761
02:27:06,734 --> 02:27:08,528
A cloud service can get replaced.

1762
02:27:08,814 --> 02:27:17,764
A programming language can rise or fall in popularity, but the ability to learn quickly and solve problems stays useful no matter what changes around it.

1763
02:27:18,478 --> 02:27:23,396
This is also why you shouldn't feel pressured to learn every new technology the moment it appears.

1764
02:27:23,918 --> 02:27:31,396
Pay attention to what's relevant to your role, build depth in the areas you use, and explore new tools when there's a genuine reason to.

1765
02:27:31,950 --> 02:27:36,964
Understanding a few things well is often more valuable than knowing a little about everything.

1766
02:27:37,806 --> 02:27:41,828
And since we just talked about logging your work, it's worth saying plainly.

1767
02:27:42,254 --> 02:27:47,076
Genuine career growth usually isn't the result of politics or self-promotion.

1768
02:27:47,502 --> 02:27:56,128
It's the compounding effect of consistently delivering, taking ownership of problems beyond just your assigned ticket, and being someone people can rely on.

1769
02:27:56,368 --> 02:28:01,728
Visibility matters, which is exactly why that habit of logging your own work matters.

1770
02:28:02,094 --> 02:28:06,048
But visibility only helps you if there's real substance underneath it.

1771
02:28:06,414 --> 02:28:10,048
Growth that isn't built on that foundation tends to be short lived.

1772
02:28:10,254 --> 02:28:12,644
Building that kind of foundation is one thing.

1773
02:28:13,070 --> 02:28:16,048
Making sure anyone actually notices it is another.

1774
02:28:16,750 --> 02:28:19,748
Here's something that surprises a lot of first year developers.

1775
02:28:20,238 --> 02:28:23,972
Your manager does not remember most of what you did six months ago.

1776
02:28:24,558 --> 02:28:29,316
Not because they don't care, but because they're tracking an entire team, not just you.

1777
02:28:29,838 --> 02:28:36,836
When appraisal season comes around, they're often reconstructing your year from memory, a few JIRA tickets, and whatever you tell them.

1778
02:28:37,422 --> 02:28:41,252
If you don't tell them, that work quietly disappears from the story.

1779
02:28:41,806 --> 02:28:44,740
The fix is a habit, not a personality trait.

1780
02:28:45,262 --> 02:28:47,972
Once a week, write two or three lines for yourself.

1781
02:28:48,398 --> 02:28:52,644
What you shipped, what problem it actually solved, and anything notable.

1782
02:28:53,038 --> 02:28:58,436
A teammate you helped unblock, a bug you caught before it reached customers, a process you improved.

1783
02:28:58,926 --> 02:29:03,748
It doesn't need to be polished, it just needs to exist somewhere outside your own memory.

1784
02:29:04,398 --> 02:29:13,252
For example, instead of writing, worked on the payment API, record that you fixed a timeout issue that was causing payment requests to fail intermittently.

1785
02:29:13,806 --> 02:29:22,628
The second version captures the actual impact of your work, and six months later, you'll remember much more clearly what you contributed and why it mattered.

1786
02:29:23,214 --> 02:29:25,572
This isn't self-promotion in the pushy sense.

1787
02:29:26,190 --> 02:29:35,812
Your manager is very likely advocating for you in rooms you're not in, deciding on raises, promotions, or new opportunities, often based on incomplete information.

1788
02:29:36,462 --> 02:29:40,740
Giving them an accurate, specific record of your impact isn't political maneuvering.

1789
02:29:41,166 --> 02:29:44,740
It's making sure the person representing you actually has the facts.

1790
02:29:45,326 --> 02:29:58,436
When appraisal time comes, that running log turns into your self-review almost automatically, specific, honest, and grounded in things that actually happened, instead of a vague attempt to remember your whole year in one sitting.

1791
02:29:59,118 --> 02:30:01,732
It can also help you spot patterns in your own development.

1792
02:30:02,254 --> 02:30:10,692
You might notice that you're taking on more complex tasks, helping teammates more often, or becoming the person people approach for a particular area of the system.

1793
02:30:11,374 --> 02:30:16,196
Those patterns can help you identify what you're getting better at and where you want to grow next.

1794
02:30:16,814 --> 02:30:20,324
Genuine growth in your career tends to come from this same place.

1795
02:30:20,848 --> 02:30:27,812
Consistent, visible impact over time, not from politics and not from constantly promoting yourself in the moment.

1796
02:30:28,366 --> 02:30:32,196
The log is just what makes sure that consistency doesn't get forgotten.

1797
02:30:32,846 --> 02:30:36,036
And that log doesn't have to wait for appraisal season to be useful.

1798
02:30:36,366 --> 02:30:38,756
There's a much more regular place it belongs.

1799
02:30:39,246 --> 02:30:40,135
Your one-on-ones.

1800
02:30:40,654 --> 02:30:47,716
At some point, your manager will start scheduling a recurring one-on-ones, usually 15 to 30 minutes, just the two of you.

1801
02:30:48,270 --> 02:30:50,820
A lot of juniors treat this as a status update.

1802
02:30:51,118 --> 02:30:54,660
Here's what I've been working on, and leave most of the value on the table.

1803
02:30:55,278 --> 02:31:01,988
A one-on-ones is one of the few pieces of time at work that's actually meant to be about you, not about the sprint.

1804
02:31:02,414 --> 02:31:04,708
Status updates already happen at stand-up.

1805
02:31:05,230 --> 02:31:08,836
This is the time for things that don't fit into a five-minute team meeting.

1806
02:31:09,262 --> 02:31:21,860
A blocker you've been sitting on quietly, a piece of feedback you want but haven't been given, a question about where your career could go, or simply flagging that a project's timeline feels off before it becomes a bigger problem.

1807
02:31:22,414 --> 02:31:30,724
For example, maybe you've been struggling with a particular part of the code base for two weeks, but you haven't mentioned it because you don't want to look inexperienced.

1808
02:31:31,182 --> 02:31:33,604
Your one-on-one is a good place to bring that up.

1809
02:31:33,934 --> 02:31:36,260
Explain what you've tried and ask for guidance.

1810
02:31:36,974 --> 02:31:40,164
You don't have to arrive with every problem already solved.

1811
02:31:40,590 --> 02:31:45,028
Sometimes, knowing when to ask for help is part of becoming a better developer.

1812
02:31:45,550 --> 02:31:52,516
This is also exactly where that work log from earlier becomes useful, in the moment, not just at appraisal time.

1813
02:31:53,134 --> 02:31:57,808
Instead of a vague things are going fine, you can walk in with something concrete.

1814
02:31:58,286 --> 02:31:59,648
I shipped the discount feature.

1815
02:31:59,854 --> 02:32:02,116
It's the one the support team mentioned came up a lot.

1816
02:32:02,510 --> 02:32:05,728
I'd like to try picking up something closer to the payment service next.

1817
02:32:05,966 --> 02:32:06,884
Is that realistic?

1818
02:32:07,502 --> 02:32:11,460
That's a very different conversation than one built entirely on the spot.

1819
02:32:11,950 --> 02:32:14,340
And pay attention to the feedback you receive.

1820
02:32:14,766 --> 02:32:22,084
If your manager suggests improving how you communicate progress, for example, don't treat it as a criticism to forget once the meeting ends.

1821
02:32:22,734 --> 02:32:28,164
Pick one practical thing to work on before your next one-on-one, then follow up on how it went.

1822
02:32:28,686 --> 02:32:32,740
That's how a short conversation turns into actual progress over time.

1823
02:32:33,326 --> 02:32:45,348
You don't need an agenda every single week, but it's worth walking in with at least one real thing a blocker, a question, or something you want feedback on, rather than treating the meeting as something to get through.

1824
02:32:45,934 --> 02:32:51,108
Managers generally want to help, but they can only act on what you actually bring to them.

1825
02:32:51,726 --> 02:32:59,652
And all of this the log, the one on ones, the honest conversations about workload, points toward the same bigger question.

1826
02:33:00,302 --> 02:33:03,012
What actually makes someone good at this job?

1827
02:33:03,342 --> 02:33:07,108
After working with developers for a while, you notice something interesting.

1828
02:33:07,790 --> 02:33:12,420
The person who knows the most syntax isn't necessarily the strongest developer.

1829
02:33:13,102 --> 02:33:18,544
Someone might know 10 programming languages and still struggle to understand a simple production issue.

1830
02:33:18,958 --> 02:33:30,308
Meanwhile, another developer might know just one language extremely well, but be excellent at breaking down problems, debugging systems, communicating with the team, and making changes safely.

1831
02:33:30,958 --> 02:33:34,148
That's because software engineering is bigger than writing code.

1832
02:33:34,670 --> 02:33:49,828
You need the technical skills, sure, but you also need judgment, knowing when to ask for help, when to keep investigating, when not to make a risky change, when a quick fix is genuinely enough, and when a problem actually needs a deeper solution.

1833
02:33:50,510 --> 02:33:56,804
You build that judgment by working through real problems, not just by watching tutorials or learning new frameworks.

1834
02:33:57,454 --> 02:34:05,156
When something breaks, take the time to understand why it broke, what caused it, and how you could prevent the same issue from happening again.

1835
02:34:05,870 --> 02:34:09,732
Over time, those experiences start changing how you approach problems.

1836
02:34:10,414 --> 02:34:12,260
You need to communicate clearly.

1837
02:34:12,686 --> 02:34:17,732
And you need to understand that your code affects other people, not just your own to-do list.

1838
02:34:18,318 --> 02:34:20,580
There's a related trap worth naming directly.

1839
02:34:21,102 --> 02:34:27,940
It's possible to complete ticket after ticket for months and still barely understand how the system actually fits together.

1840
02:34:28,590 --> 02:34:30,436
Finishing tickets keeps you employed.

1841
02:34:30,864 --> 02:34:34,212
Understanding the system is what makes you genuinely valuable.

1842
02:34:34,766 --> 02:34:46,404
That means occasionally reading code you weren't assigned to touch, asking why something was built a certain way instead of just how to change it, and noticing how your one piece connects to the rest of the application.

1843
02:34:47,022 --> 02:34:52,384
The developers people actually go to for help aren't necessarily the ones who've closed the most tickets.

1844
02:34:52,782 --> 02:34:57,060
They're the ones who can answer, wait, how does this whole thing actually work?

1845
02:34:57,678 --> 02:35:00,548
And you don't have to understand the entire system overnight.

1846
02:35:00,942 --> 02:35:04,132
Start with the part you're working on, then follow the connections.

1847
02:35:04,782 --> 02:35:06,020
Where does the data come from?

1848
02:35:06,478 --> 02:35:07,716
What calls this service?

1849
02:35:08,142 --> 02:35:09,828
What happens after your code runs?

1850
02:35:10,670 --> 02:35:17,380
Each answer gives you a slightly bigger picture, and eventually, problems that once seemed confusing start making sense.

1851
02:35:18,222 --> 02:35:23,524
So, as you progress in your career, don't measure yourself only by how much code you can write.

1852
02:35:24,014 --> 02:35:27,428
Measure yourself by the problems you can understand and solve.

1853
02:35:28,078 --> 02:35:30,596
Understanding the system is only half of it though.

1854
02:35:30,894 --> 02:35:39,584
You also have to be able to explain what you understand to people who don't share your technical vocabulary, which is its own skill worth building on purpose.

1855
02:35:39,886 --> 02:35:44,064
A product manager asks, can we just add a filter to this page by Friday?

1856
02:35:44,398 --> 02:35:50,020
And a developer answers with a sentence full of words like schema migration and cash invalidation.

1857
02:35:50,542 --> 02:35:55,748
Both people walk away from that conversation a little frustrated, and neither one was wrong.

1858
02:35:56,270 --> 02:36:05,540
Different roles on a software team aren't just doing different tasks, they're usually optimizing for different things, and talking past each other usually comes from forgetting that.

1859
02:36:05,998 --> 02:36:10,820
A product manager is mainly thinking about what the business and the customer need and when.

1860
02:36:11,246 --> 02:36:15,972
A designer is thinking about how it should look and feel and whether it's actually usable.

1861
02:36:16,430 --> 02:36:20,196
QA is thinking about what could go wrong and whether it's been checked.

1862
02:36:20,622 --> 02:36:25,028
None of them are thinking primarily about your database schema, and they shouldn't have to.

1863
02:36:25,550 --> 02:36:31,108
The habit that closes that gap is translating constraints into outcomes instead of mechanisms.

1864
02:36:31,598 --> 02:36:35,972
Instead of saying that requires a schema migration, try something like this.

1865
02:36:36,558 --> 02:36:45,284
That's possible, but it touches how we store existing data, so it'll take a bit longer and needs to go out carefully, probably Tuesday instead of Friday.

1866
02:36:45,774 --> 02:36:53,092
That gives a non technical teammate something they can actually act on a timeline and a reason, not a term they have to look up.

1867
02:36:53,646 --> 02:36:55,140
This works in both directions.

1868
02:36:55,630 --> 02:37:04,036
When a request seems vague, like make it faster or make it feel more modern, it helps to resist answering it purely technically right away.

1869
02:37:04,430 --> 02:37:07,268
Ask what specific moment prompted the request.

1870
02:37:07,726 --> 02:37:16,932
Make it faster often means one particular screen felt slow to one particular person on one particular day, not that the whole system needs a redesign.

1871
02:37:17,390 --> 02:37:22,948
Finding that specific moment usually turns a vague ask into a concrete, solvable problem.

1872
02:37:23,406 --> 02:37:26,372
QA deserves that same respect in the other direction.

1873
02:37:26,798 --> 02:37:29,668
You already know not to take a rejected ticket personally.

1874
02:37:30,062 --> 02:37:32,900
The missing half of that habit is what to do next.

1875
02:37:33,262 --> 02:37:36,224
Ask for the exact steps that reproduce what they saw.

1876
02:37:36,654 --> 02:37:45,060
A bug report with a vague recollection on your side and precise steps on theirs isn't a disagreement to win, it's missing information to go find.

1877
02:37:45,582 --> 02:37:50,052
None of this means hiding technical reality or pretending constraints don't exist.

1878
02:37:50,446 --> 02:37:56,612
It means remembering that the person across from you is optimizing for something just as real as your technical concern.

1879
02:37:56,974 --> 02:38:04,544
And the fastest way through a disagreement is usually translating your constraint into their terms, not insisting they learn yours first.

1880
02:38:04,878 --> 02:38:12,384
Get comfortable with that kind of translation, and a lot of the friction that makes a first job feel exhausting quietly disappears.

1881
02:38:12,686 --> 02:38:19,236
You ask your manager for something small, a paid tool license, say, something that costs less than a dinner out.

1882
02:38:19,758 --> 02:38:22,212
Two weeks later, you still don't have an answer.

1883
02:38:22,766 --> 02:38:26,436
It's tempting to assume the company is just slow or doesn't care.

1884
02:38:26,990 --> 02:38:28,484
Usually that's not it.

1885
02:38:29,006 --> 02:38:34,020
Your request is standing in a line you can't see, and it helps to know who else is in that line.

1886
02:38:34,798 --> 02:38:42,340
Most engineering teams run two separate ladders at once, and the confusing part is that both of them use the word senior somewhere.

1887
02:38:42,926 --> 02:38:51,940
One ladder is for people who keep writing code and solving technical problems directly, often called the individual contributor track, or IC for short.

1888
02:38:52,462 --> 02:38:55,716
It usually runs from junior to mid-level to senior.

1889
02:38:56,206 --> 02:39:00,004
At some companies, it continues into staff or principal engineer.

1890
02:39:00,590 --> 02:39:07,236
Those are people who are still hands-on but whose decisions now shape how multiple teams build things, not just their own code.

1891
02:39:07,886 --> 02:39:10,468
The other ladder is for people who manage people.

1892
02:39:10,926 --> 02:39:21,636
That one usually runs from engineering manager to director to VP of engineering, and sometimes, at the very top, a CTO, the most senior engineering role in the company.

1893
02:39:22,478 --> 02:39:25,220
Here's the distinction that actually matters day to day.

1894
02:39:25,678 --> 02:39:28,708
An engineering manager's job is to support their team.

1895
02:39:29,070 --> 02:39:34,308
That means running one-on-ones, giving feedback, deciding priorities, and clearing obstacles.

1896
02:39:34,830 --> 02:39:38,084
They're usually not writing much production code themselves anymore.

1897
02:39:38,798 --> 02:39:46,724
A senior or staff engineer, on the other hand, might outrank a lot of people in technical judgment while having nobody reporting to them at all.

1898
02:39:47,214 --> 02:39:54,660
Seniority and being someone's boss are not the same thing, and mixing them up is an easy mistake to make in your first few months.

1899
02:39:55,566 --> 02:39:58,532
So where did your two-week request actually go?

1900
02:39:59,086 --> 02:40:05,668
In a lot of companies, something like a budget item, even a small one, follows the management ladder, not the technical one.

1901
02:40:06,254 --> 02:40:08,516
Your manager might be able to approve it alone.

1902
02:40:08,910 --> 02:40:15,940
If not, it goes up to their manager, a director, who's balancing it against requests from several teams, not just yours.

1903
02:40:16,462 --> 02:40:24,100
If it's bigger, it might need a VP's sign off, someone thinking about the budget for an entire part of the company, not a single request.

1904
02:40:24,782 --> 02:40:27,664
Each step isn't necessarily someone being difficult.

1905
02:40:27,982 --> 02:40:34,500
It's someone who has to weigh your one request against everyone else's, and that takes longer than you'd expect from the outside.

1906
02:40:35,310 --> 02:40:37,668
And where does the CEO fit into all this?

1907
02:40:38,254 --> 02:40:41,136
At a small startup, you might actually talk to them directly.

1908
02:40:41,422 --> 02:40:51,588
At a larger company, the CEO is usually several layers removed from your day-to-day work entirely, focused on the company as a whole rather than any single team's decisions.

1909
02:40:52,014 --> 02:40:55,204
The product side of the house runs a similar ladder in parallel.

1910
02:40:55,662 --> 02:41:01,988
Product managers, then a director of product, then a VP, sometimes up to a chief product officer.

1911
02:41:02,446 --> 02:41:08,164
The engineering and product ladders meet near the top to decide what the company builds and in what order.

1912
02:41:08,590 --> 02:41:11,620
None of this means you need to learn everyone's name or title.

1913
02:41:12,046 --> 02:41:19,620
In practice, almost everything you do will run through your direct manager, and maybe a tech lead or senior engineer you work closely with.

1914
02:41:20,110 --> 02:41:22,884
The rest of the chain mostly matters for one reason.

1915
02:41:23,406 --> 02:41:24,964
It explains the timing.

1916
02:41:25,376 --> 02:41:30,468
And it explains the no you sometimes get for reasons that have nothing to do with you.

1917
02:41:30,862 --> 02:41:33,796
A slow approval is rarely about you personally.

1918
02:41:34,254 --> 02:41:38,884
It's usually just a request waiting its turn in a line with a lot of other requests in it.

1919
02:41:39,310 --> 02:41:44,132
So the next time something you asked for takes longer than it seems like it should, remember this.

1920
02:41:44,686 --> 02:41:47,492
It's probably not stuck because it's a bad idea.

1921
02:41:47,950 --> 02:41:50,276
It's stuck because it's standing in a line.

1922
02:41:50,734 --> 02:41:52,996
And now you know roughly how long that line is.

1923
02:41:53,582 --> 02:41:57,060
Understanding that chain helps you make sense of decisions above you.

1924
02:41:57,550 --> 02:42:01,188
It's a separate question who you can actually turn to for advice.

1925
02:42:01,550 --> 02:42:05,156
You have a question that doesn't belong in a one-to-one with your manager.

1926
02:42:05,518 --> 02:42:09,988
Something like, should I stay on this team or should I try to move to a different one?

1927
02:42:10,510 --> 02:42:14,468
Your manager is the wrong person to ask that to, at least at first.

1928
02:42:14,830 --> 02:42:16,548
They're the person evaluating you.

1929
02:42:16,942 --> 02:42:19,140
That changes what you can safely say to them.

1930
02:42:19,696 --> 02:42:21,540
This is where a mentor comes in.

1931
02:42:21,998 --> 02:42:25,216
A mentor is different from a manager in one important way.

1932
02:42:25,742 --> 02:42:27,780
A mentor doesn't decide your raise.

1933
02:42:28,206 --> 02:42:29,700
They don't write your performance review.

1934
02:42:30,030 --> 02:42:32,292
They're not involved in whether you get promoted.

1935
02:42:32,750 --> 02:42:34,788
That's exactly what makes them useful.

1936
02:42:35,214 --> 02:42:41,220
You can ask them things you wouldn't ask your manager, and they can give you an honest answer without it affecting your career.

1937
02:42:41,774 --> 02:42:44,164
Some companies assign you a mentor on day one.

1938
02:42:44,558 --> 02:42:45,444
Most don't.

1939
02:42:45,870 --> 02:42:48,964
If nobody hands you one, you're allowed to go find one yourself.

1940
02:42:49,486 --> 02:42:51,204
Here's how people actually do it.

1941
02:42:51,726 --> 02:42:55,972
Look for someone whose day-to-day work looks like where you'd like to be in a couple of years.

1942
02:42:56,462 --> 02:42:59,620
That person doesn't have to be the most senior engineer in the building.

1943
02:42:59,982 --> 02:43:05,060
It just has to be someone a few steps ahead of you doing something you find genuinely interesting.

1944
02:43:05,614 --> 02:43:07,524
Now the part that feels awkward.

1945
02:43:07,936 --> 02:43:11,556
You do not walk up to them and ask, will you be my mentor?

1946
02:43:11,950 --> 02:43:19,364
That question puts a lot of weight on one moment, and most people freeze up and say no, even if they'd have said yes to something smaller.

1947
02:43:19,854 --> 02:43:21,508
Ask a small thing instead.

1948
02:43:21,838 --> 02:43:25,776
Something like, I've got a couple of questions about how you approach code review.

1949
02:43:26,062 --> 02:43:27,748
Could I grab 15 minutes sometime?

1950
02:43:28,206 --> 02:43:29,540
That's an easy yes.

1951
02:43:30,062 --> 02:43:33,284
And if the conversation goes well, you ask again next month.

1952
02:43:33,806 --> 02:43:37,060
A few habits make this sustainable instead of a burden on them.

1953
02:43:37,582 --> 02:43:40,868
Come with a specific question, not just can we chat?

1954
02:43:41,358 --> 02:43:45,124
Keep it short and end on time even if they offer to go longer.

1955
02:43:45,582 --> 02:43:47,780
And actually use what they tell you.

1956
02:43:48,238 --> 02:43:54,980
Nothing kills a mentor relationship faster than asking the same question twice because you didn't act on the answer the first time.

1957
02:43:55,566 --> 02:43:57,444
You don't need a title for any of this.

1958
02:43:57,806 --> 02:44:03,620
You just need one person, a little further down the road than you, willing to answer a question every few weeks.

1959
02:44:04,078 --> 02:44:08,176
That's the whole relationship, and it's worth more than it sounds like at first.

1960
02:44:08,398 --> 02:44:12,420
Let's finish with the most important thing I want you to take away from this course.

1961
02:44:13,006 --> 02:44:15,748
Your first IT job can feel overwhelming.

1962
02:44:16,238 --> 02:44:18,176
There will be tools you've never used.

1963
02:44:18,542 --> 02:44:28,644
There will be meetings full of terminology you don't recognize, code bases will look enormous at first, and tickets will sometimes make no sense until you actually dig in.

1964
02:44:29,166 --> 02:44:33,636
There will be days you feel like everyone else already knows what they're doing and you don't.

1965
02:44:34,190 --> 02:44:34,948
That's normal.

1966
02:44:35,406 --> 02:44:38,532
Don't expect yourself to become an expert in your first few weeks.

1967
02:44:39,054 --> 02:44:43,812
And remember, the people around you who seem to know everything were beginners once too.

1968
02:44:44,302 --> 02:44:52,452
They've had their own confusing days, broken things they didn't mean to break, and spent hours trying to understand problems that seem obvious to them now.

1969
02:44:52,910 --> 02:44:57,136
You're seeing their experience today, not all the steps it took to get there.

1970
02:44:57,774 --> 02:45:00,612
Your goal is simply to keep building context.

1971
02:45:00,910 --> 02:45:03,620
Then gradually take on more responsibility.

1972
02:45:04,142 --> 02:45:08,196
After a few months, things that felt complicated will start looking familiar.

1973
02:45:08,654 --> 02:45:16,996
You'll recognize the tools, you'll understand the architecture, you'll know exactly where to look when something breaks, and exactly who to ask when you're stuck.

1974
02:45:17,582 --> 02:45:20,036
Don't worry about learning everything at once either.

1975
02:45:20,430 --> 02:45:22,052
Focus on the task in front of you.

1976
02:45:22,414 --> 02:45:26,788
Understand what you're doing and why, and learn something from each experience.

1977
02:45:27,310 --> 02:45:29,156
You don't need to know every answer.

1978
02:45:29,518 --> 02:45:32,292
You need to get a little better at finding the answers.

1979
02:45:32,750 --> 02:45:37,028
And someday, a new developer will ask you a question you once had yourself.

1980
02:45:37,518 --> 02:45:40,356
That's when you'll realize how much you've actually learned.

1981
02:45:40,654 --> 02:45:48,900
If this course has helped you understand a little more about how IT works and feel more confident about getting started, then it's achieved what I hoped it would.

1982
02:45:49,294 --> 02:45:52,964
It took almost a month to research, write, and edit this course.

1983
02:45:53,422 --> 02:45:57,380
If you found it useful, please leave a comment and tell me which part you liked most.

1984
02:45:57,646 --> 02:46:00,544
And if you'd like to support the channel, please subscribe.

1985
02:46:00,704 --> 02:46:01,700
It really helps.

1986
02:46:02,295 --> 02:46:05,195
Thanks for watching, and best of luck on your journey into tech.

